% Ensuring complex system compliance with regulations and standards requires rigorous validation of assurance cases, which are typically structured as claim-argument-evidence frameworks. However, compliance detection contains several challenges, such as the complexity of legal-technical texts, the need for interpretability, and the limited availability and access to assurance case data. 

% We analyse the robustness of local explanations from the NLI model and the traceability of compliance decisions using multi-hop reasoning. 

Ensuring complex systems meet regulations typically requires checking the validity of assurance cases through a claim-argument-evidence framework. Some challenges in this process include the complicated nature of legal and technical texts, the need for the model explanations, and limited access to assurance case data.
We propose a compliance detection approach based on Natural Language Inference (NLI): \textbf{EX}plainable \textbf{C}omp\textbf{L}iance detection with \textbf{A}rgumentative \textbf{I}nference of \textbf{M}ulti-hop reasoning (\textbf{EXCLAIM}). We formulate the claim-argument-evidence structure of an assurance case as a multi-hop inference for explainable and traceable compliance detection. We address the limited number of assurance cases by generating them using LLMs. We introduce metrics that measure the generated assurance cases' coverage and structural consistency. We demonstrate the effectiveness of the generated assurance case from GDPR requirements in a multi-hop inference task as a case study. 
Our results highlight the potential of NLI-based approaches in automating the regulatory compliance process.
\end{abstract}

