\subsection{NLI-Based Compliance Detection}
% \paragraph{Compliance Detection as NLI}

To address the challenges of legal text ambiguity, domain specificity, and evolving regulations in compliance detection, we explore transfer learning methods and compare the results with a previous study~\citep{azeem2024multi} to answer \textbf{[RQ1]}.
In this scenario, we compare three different transfer learning algorithms, namely Fine-tuning (FT), Linear-Probing-Fine-tuning (LP-FT)~\citep{kumarfine} and Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA)~\citep{hu2022lora}. 

% Fine-tuning is a transfer learning technique where a pre-trained model is further trained on a specific domain dataset to adapt it to specialised tasks. 
% Linear probing is a technique commonly employed in transfer learning to evaluate the quality of features learned by a pre-trained model. This method involves training a simple linear classifier, such as logistic regression or a linear support vector machine, on top of the fixed features extracted by the pre-trained model. 
% Linear Probing Fine-tuning (LP-FT) is an extension of the linear probing technique that combines the simplicity of linear probing with the adaptability of fine-tuning. While linear probing evaluates the quality of features learned by a pre-trained model by training a linear classifier on top of frozen features, LP-FT goes a step further by allowing some degree of fine-tuning of the pre-trained model's layers. 
% Low-rank adaptation (LoRA) is a technique for efficiently adapting pre-trained language models to new tasks. It introduces low-rank matrices into the model's architecture, reducing the number of parameters to be updated.

Our approach considers two main pre-trained language models (LM) categories: general-purpose and domain-specific legal models. For general domain, we consider BERT-large~\citep{devlin-etal-2019-bert} (BERT), RoBERTa-large~\citep{liu2019roberta} (RoBERTa). For domain-specific legal models, we consider Legal-BERT-base~\citep{chalkidis-etal-2020-legal} (Legal-BERT) as the counterpart of BERT and Legal-RoBERTa~\citep{chalkidis-etal-2023-lexfiles} as the counterpart of RoBERTa. In addition, we also explore the decoder-based LM, such as GPT-2-XL\footnote{\href{openai-community/gpt2-xl}{openai-community/gpt2-xl}} and Llama-3.2-1B~\citep{grattafiori2024llama}, to classify the entailment as a text generation baseline. 

