\begin{abstract}
Space missions rely on precise command execution across multiple phases, between spacecraft and mission control. Two of the most vulnerable phases are launch and reentry, where timing is critical. An attacker can exploit these moments to prematurely terminate a mission or disrupt recovery. Notably, the perimeter during these phases is closely monitored due to their mission-critical nature. However, during launch and reentry, radio communications, characterized by low power, high mobility, and time sensitivity are particularly vulnerable to replay attacks where an attacker can replicate legitimate signals from the source, making detection and mitigation complex and demanding advanced link-level security architectures \cite{Morioka2024}. Additionally, as reported in \cite{9256695}, not all attacks necessarily demand high resources and come from state-sponsored entities. Low-cost software-defined radios (SDRs) can enable adversaries to intercept and manipulate communication links without requiring extensive expertise \cite{Estevez2022Decoding}. 

In this paper, we demonstrate that despite the protections surrounding high-risk mission phases, certain vulnerabilities in signal transmission remain exploitable. Specifically, we investigate how replay timing-based attacks can be executed at the physical layer, targeting the uplink during launch and reentry when ground stations (GS) transmit commands to the Orion spacecraft - one of the most significant crewed capsules planned. While traditional security models typically rely on physical limitations, such as geographical boundaries to protect and mitigate risks, we reveal how aerial nodes—particularly High Altitude Long Endurance (HALE) drones—can bypass these.




% Orion is launched aboard the Space Launch System (SLS) megarocket, making the integrity of its communication links even more crucial.

% We focus on the Orion capsule in the Artemis missions due to its crewed nature, high-stakes operations, and reliance on precise communication links for safety and navigation. The Orion capsule in Artemis II is launched aboard the Space Launch System (SLS) megarocket, making the protection of communication links even more critical.

We focus on the physical layer because of known vulnerabilities and recent timing flaws in space-ground communications. We use a Doppler fading channel emulator to assess the feasibility and impact of these attacks. We further demonstrate how these high-impact cyber threats can escalate into lower layers, allowing attackers to manipulate synchronization mechanisms and induce operational failures. For our two critical phases—launch and reentry—they are modeled as follows:

\begin{itemize}[left=0pt, labelsep=1em]
    \item Launch Phase: Orion is launched aboard the Space Launch System (SLS) megarocket for Artemis II. We analyze the Max Q stage, where the SLS rocket reaches peak mechanical stress; this dictates the altitude used to calculate propagation delay. 
    \item Reentry Phase: For Artemis I, reentry lasts at least five minutes in the subsonic region, starting with a blackout from electrically charged plasma. We evaluate the attacker's ability to exploit link reconnection at this altitude.
\end{itemize}

In both phases, our model includes Orion in the static uplink parameters (maximum HALE velocity, GS angle, distance, attacker transceiver gain, and Orion receiver gain) and a dynamic channel modeled with one-tap pure-Doppler fading. With Quadrature Phase Shift Keying (QPSK)-modulated message bits using a Galois-configured linear-feedback shift register, multi-level synchronization, namely sampling, phase, and timing synchronization follows the bit error rate (BER) is analyzed. A secure transmission achieves 0 BER, whereas an overpowering HALE attack can also yield 0 BER beyond the link tolerance, resulting in a 100\% replay success rate without defence measures.


% Attacks during both phases are carried out in three key steps:
% \begin{enumerate*}
% \item Exposure of the communication channel,
% \item Recording of transmitted signals by the attacker, and
% \item Execution of the replay attack.
% \end{enumerate*}

Through experimental analysis, we examine replay attack feasibility using HALE drones with varying capabilities in the reentry phase. A channel emulator (PROPSIM) is utilized with multiple SDRs (USRP-B200) to demonstrate the attack scenario in dynamic radio environment. In our preliminary emulations, we find that a
Northrop Grumman RQ-4 Global Hawk-class drone can initiate an overpowering attack starting at an output gain increment of 5 dBFS. We will extend the tests with launch-phase emulations to assess attack impacts.


Based on the emulations, we will propose protective countermeasures and define security checks in accordance with \cite{9925759} to ensure that the critical uplink signal remains secure from potential interference during the intervals assessed in our system model. This approach will address both replay attack defenses and mitigate the power elevation issues identified in our findings. Preliminary controls under consideration include challenge-response mechanisms to identify attackers and close the communication window. This will provide an opportunity to either proceed with the launch or reprogram it as needed, ensuring the mission’s security before continuing.

% We focus on two critical scenes: launch and reentry. During launch, we examine Max Q stage, where the SLS rocket reaches the peak of mechanical stress and is highly susceptible to communication disruptions. During reentry, we focus on the subsonic region, specifically at the point of drogue chute deployment following the communications blackout. This phase is crucial as it marks the restoration of telemetry links, making it a potential attack vector for replaying delayed or manipulated data.  
\textit{Contributions - (Synopsis)} This paper examines the vulnerability of spacecraft communication systems to replay attacks targeting critical timing during launch and reentry. Through channel emulations of the physical layer, we expose how such attacks can disrupt spacecraft operations on link-layer and escalate further into the physical layer. Our key contributions include a detailed system model that illustrates these vulnerabilities and {software defined radio based} demonstration of high-impact cyber threats in such settings, for which we propose protection countermeasures. Based on our findings, an energy efficient, event-driven replay-resilient protocol will be presented and compared to conventional timestamp based mitigation techniques.
\end{abstract}

