    \label{fig:zm_reverse_non_inflated_robust}
\end{figure}
Similar behavior is found for the forward and reverse team by team consistency $z_{m,n}$ for the three models that exclude the teams without labor moves (Fig.~\ref{fig:zm_reverse_non_inflated_robust}). In this case, there is hardly any discernible difference between models or time periods. This represents very strong evidence to the robustness of the influence of reuniting moves in job movility.


\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{plots/robustness_checks/robust_y_m_forward.png}
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{plots/robustness_checks/robust_y_m_reverse.png}
    \caption{Box plots for system-wide forward (top) and reverse (bottom) average consistency $\langle y_{m}\rangle$. The box plots are constructed under the same rules as those in Fig.~\ref{fig:ym} but organized from left to right with $\Delta t=6$, $7$, and $9$ months.}
    \label{fig:ym_reverse_robust}
\end{figure}
Qualitatively, the behavior of system-wide forward and reverse consistency consistency $y_{m,n}$ is virtually the same across $\Delta t$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:ym_reverse_robust}). There is a slight increase in performance with increasing $\Delta t$ which again supports the importance of reuniting moves in narrowing down the possible job changes that the organization can experience.


\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{plots/robustness_checks/robust_l_m_forward.png}
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{plots/robustness_checks/robust_l_m_reverse.png}
    \caption{Box plots for the forward (top) and reverse (bottom) ratios of observed versus random reuniting moves from the $\text{SP}$ and $\text{OSP}$ models, $\langle\lambda_m\rangle$. The box plots are constructed under the same rules as those in Fig.~\ref{fig:per_follow} but organized from left to right with $\Delta t=6$, $7$, and $9$ months.}
    \label{fig:l_m_reverse_robust}
