We briefly list our main theoretical results. We design a novel flow model with $L_1$-radially monotonic base distributions that provides the following theoretical properties crucial for the verification domain while outperforming its normalizing counterparts in nearly all benchmarks.
Specifically, we design a flow model that:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \label{contribution_log_level} Maps upper/lower (log-)density level sets of the target distribution to upper/lower \mbox{(log-)density} level sets of the base distribution allowing for fine-grained probabilistic control during sampling.
    \item \label{laten_udl} Allows the definition of the pre-image of a density level set in the latent space via linear constraints.
    \item \label{contribution_affine} Has a piece-wise affine log-density function for the case of a Laplacian base distribution, facilitating efficient computation and analysis.
\end{enumerate}

We identify sufficient conditions for a flow layer to have the aforementioned properties and survey the literature and present a set of several layers - with its most representative members being additive coupling layers and bijective affine layers - that can be arbitrarily combined to yield a flow with the desired properties.

\section{Verification Background}
Verification of neural networks refers to checking whether a given network~$f$ satisfies a given semantic property~$P$ for all possible inputs $x$. 
This semantic property~$P$ may consist of a precondition $\phi$ and a postcondition $\psi$ usually related as an implication $\phi(x) \implies \psi(f(x))$.
There are two conceptually different approaches for verifying such properties for neural networks, namely \textit{SMT based verification} and \textit{abstract interpretation}. 
\paragraph{SMT based Verification}
An SMT based verification approach typically involves translating~$f$ along with~$P$ to a logical formula~$\Psi_{f,P}$ that is designed to be valid if and only if the network~$f$ satisfies the property~$P$. For an understanding on how the neural network along with the verification condition is compiled into the formula, we refer to prior works such as the book by Albarghouthi \cite{albarghouthi-book,xie_neurosymbolic_2022}.

On a technical level, the validity of such logical formulas can be checked using off-the-shelf Satisfiability Modulo Theorem (SMT) Solvers. The runtime of such solvers depends on the pre-/post- and verification condition which in turn depends on the architecture of the neural networks that are compiled into the formula. 
If the verification condition falls into a quantifier free fragment of linear arithmetic, efficient solvers such as Marabou can be used \cite{katz2019marabou}. 
This is the case if the network only used linear components, as we ensure in our case with ReLu-activation functions. 
If, however, the formula contains non-linearities (e.g., due to the neural network containing non-linear activation functions such as tanh), then one may need to use more powerful but slower solvers such as dReal~\cite{gao2012delta}.

On a conceptual level, we check the validity of $\Psi_{f,P}$ by checking the unsatisfiability of the negated verification condition $\neg\Psi_{f,P}$.
If the negated verification condition $\neg \Psi_{f,P}$ is unsatisfiable, then $\Psi_{f,P}$ is valid and by construction $f$ satisfies $P$. 
If, however, $\neg \Psi_{f,P}$ is satisfiable, then $\Psi_{f,P}$ is invalid and the neural network is incorrect. In that case, the assignment $x$ for satisfying $\neg \Psi_{f,P}$ is a counterexample that shows the violation of the verification property by the neural network.
More precisely, the counterexample $x$ satisfies the precondition $\phi(x)$ but violates postcondition $\psi(f(x))$.
Such a counterexample was presented in Figure~\ref{intro_flow_example}.
While a main advantage of SMT based verification tools is their precision and ability to provide counterexamples for when the verification property is violated by the neural network, their drawback is the computational runtime for generating positive proof certificates.
In contrast to verifying unsafe neural networks that merely require a counterexample to be found, which terminates the verification process, safe neural networks require fully rejecting the satisfiability of $\neg \Psi_{f,P}$.
This gap is closed with a less precise and conceptually different verification approach called abstract interpretation. Abstract interpretation can provide fast positive proof certificates for safe neural networks.

\paragraph{Abstract Interpretation}\label{sec:background:abstract_interpretation}
The main idea of abstract interpretation is to symbolically run a neural network on an abstract domain. 
An abstract domain provides the basic structure of the symbolic elements that in turn represent a subspace of the input, latent or output space.
Examples for abstract domains are polytopes and widely-used zonotopes \cite{chen2008sound,singh2018fast}.
In this paper, we exclusively consider the latter.

The zonotope in the input space of the neural network represents the set of all inputs satisfying the precondition.
This is a the geometric representation of the aforementioned logical precondition.
During the symbolic execution procedure, the input zonotope is iteratively passed through each layer of the neural network by over-approximating the behaviour of each layer.
This results in an over-approximation of all possible outputs of the neural network to the given input-level zonotope.

Until now, the aforementioned postcondition of our logical verification property has been disregarded.
However, similar to the precondition, the postcondition also induces a subspace in the output space.
Therefore, it is sufficient to check whether the output-level zonotope provided by the abstract interpretation procedure lays inside the zonotope induced by the postcondition. The latter is called \textit{safe space}. If the output-level zonotope lays inside the safe space, the verification property is guaranteed by the neural network and the verification was successful.
If the output-level zonotope lays fully outside safe space, then the verification property is guaranteed to not hold.
If, however, the output-level zonotope partially lays outside the safe space, no assertion can be made due to the over-approximation of the abstract interpreter. This means, the neural network may or may not satisfy the verification property.

There exists several verification frameworks that implement abstract interpretation. \cite{singh2018fast,wang2021beta}.
Unlike the aforementioned SMT based verifiers, abstract interpretation frameworks do not conceptually rely on piecewise affinity of the neural network, may allow more divers neural network architectures but are not able to return counterexamples if the verification condition is violated \cite{albarghouthi-book,ERAN_website}.

\section{Flow Models}\label{sec:flow_models}
Flow models provide an elegant way to define a density estimator and a generative model by a single network.  
More precisely, we train a flow to transform a
simple base (or latent) distribution $B$ into the data (or target) distribution $D$ using a continuous invertible map with continuous inverse,
i.e. a diffeomorphism \footnote{Note that we define the flows in the direction from base distribution to data distribution. This is inverse to the direction suggested by the common name "Normalizing Flow", but better suited for our analysis. Apart from that, the two definitions are obviously equivalent.}. The map $F$ is implemented by an invertible neural
network. We obtain a a density estimator and a generative model by applying the flow in both directions:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Sampling is performed by first sampling $z \sim B$ and then computing
  the map $F(z)$.

  \item Computing the likelihood
   $p_D(x)$ can be done using the change of variables formula
  {\cite{folland_real_1999}}:
  \begin{eqnarray} \label{eqnnice}
    p_D (x) & = & \left| \textrm{det}\frac{\partial F^{-1}}{\partial x^T} \right| p_B (F^{-1} (x)).
  \end{eqnarray}
\end{enumerate}
While most neural networks are intrinsically differentiable, they do not represent
bijections in general. One needs to design specific architectures
that restrict the hypothesis space to diffeomorphisms. Note that the existence of an inverse does not necessarily imply
that the inverse can be easily computed. There are
flow architectures that allow only one of the above operations to be efficiently performed
\cite{kobyzev_normalizing_2020,papamakarios_normalizing_2021}. However, it is also not uncommon that both operation have the same complexity \cite{dinh_nice_2015}. The major goal of this work is to design a flow architecture that does not only allow to perform both operations efficiently, but it also allows an efficient analysis of the flow in a verification context.

For the latter, it is often required to not only sample individually, but verify on a space of sampled objects. 
We define the spaces containing high-density and low-density samples as upper- and lower density level sets.

\begin{definition}
Given the density of the input distribution $p_D:\mathbb{R}^d \rightarrow \mathbb{R}_+$. The set of points whose density exceeds a given threshold $t$ is called the upper density level set (UDL) and is defined as ${L^{\uparrow}_D
(t) \coloneqq \{ x \in \mathbb{R}^d \mid p_D (x) > t \}}$.
Respectively, the lower density level set (LDL) contains the set of points deceeding the density threshold: ${{L^{\downarrow}_D (t)\coloneqq\{ x \in \mathbb{R}^d \mid p_D (x) \leq t \}}= \mathbb{R}^d\setminus L^{\uparrow}_D}$. If for $q\in[0,1]$ there is a unique UDL of $D$ with probability $q$, then we denote this set by $\text{UDL}_D(q)$.
\end{definition}
Upper density level sets naturally capture the center of the distribution while LDLs capture the tail.
Note that the existence and uniqueness of the UDL with given probability is guaranteed if $P_D(\{x\mid p(x)_D =t\}) = 0$ for all $t>0$ and the image of the density function $p_D$ is a (possibly infinite) initial segment of the positive reals.

\paragraph{Base Distributions}
An isotropic Gaussian is the by far most common choice for the base distribution of a flow model. In this case, we refer to the model as a normalizing flow. A normal distribution might seem to be the natural choice, but it is definitely not the only option.
In fact, the Knothe-Rosenblatt Rearrangement Theorem \cite{knothe_contributions_1957,rosenblatt_remarks_1952} guarantees that any two absolutely continuous distributions can be transformed into one another via a diffeomorphism between their supports.
In the next section, we will show that under certain conditions $p$-radial monotonic base distributions with $p\in\{1,\infty\}$, especially the Laplacian distributions, provide some merits that allow efficient analysis of our flow model in verification scenarios. In our case, it even turned out that changing the base distribution boosted the performance of the model and the stability of the training.


\begin{definition}
    Let $X$ be random variable over $\mathbb{R}^d$. We say that $X$ is $p$-radially distributed if there is a function $g:\mathbb{R}_+\to\mathbb{R}_+$ such $p(x) = g(|x|_p)$.
    If $g$ is also strictly monotonically decreasing, then we say that $X$ is $p$-radial monotonic.
\end{definition}
Moreover, $p$-radial distributions are easily definable starting from the corresponding distribution of $p$-norms.  
In the following, let $V^d_p(r)$ is the hyper volume of the $L^p$-ball of radius $r$ in $\mathbb{R}^d$. 
\begin{definition}
  Let $\rho: \RR_+\to\RR_+$ be a probability density and $p\in \NN_{>0}\cup\{\infty\}$. 
  Then we call $R_{\rho,p,d}$ the $p$-radial distribution with $p$-norm distribution $\rho$ in $d$-dimensional space, which is given by the probability density function 
  $p_{R_{\rho,p,d}}(x) = \rho(|x|_p) \left(\frac{\partial V^d_p(r)}{\partial r}(|x|_p)\right)^{-1}$.
\end{definition}
In other words, a $p$-radial distribution is completely determined by the distribution of the $p$-norm. 
Note that if $X$ is radial monotonic, it does not imply that $p_{|X|_p}$ is monotonic. For instance, if $X$ is a $d$-dimensional standard Gaussian, then $X$ is $2$-radial monotonic but $|X|_2$ is $\chi(d)$ distributed and hence not monotonic for $d>1$.

The following observation is crucial for our application: The density level sets of a $p$-radial monotonic distribution are $L^p$-balls. By choosing $r(q) \coloneqq \textrm{quantile}_{|X|_p}\left(q\right)$, we obtain the following result:
\begin{restatable}{prop}{lp_ball_udl}\label{prop:lp_ball_udl}
    Let $X$ be a $p$-radial monotonic random variable on $\mathbb{R}^d$. Then there is a function $r:[0,1)\to\mathbb{R}_+$ such that for any $q\in[0,1)$, the upper density level set of probability $q$ is given by $\textrm{UDL}_X(q) = \mathbb{B}^d_p(r(q))$, where $\mathbb{B}^d_p(r)$ is the $L^p$-ball of radius $r$ with center at the origin. 
\end{restatable}

\subsection{Good Properties for Verification}\label{sec:good_props_verification}
As we argued earlier, piece-wise affinity is crucial for efficient SMT based verification. For the usage of our flow model this means that the transformation as well as the (log-)likelihood of the model should be piece-wise affine.
A natural approach to start off is therefore the use of piece-wise affine networks, represented e.g. by ReLU networks. If we can ensure that the defined function is bijective, then we obtain a continuous piece-wise affine bijection where
the affine regions can be represented as intersections of open and closed half-spaces \cite{moser_tessellationfiltering_2022}. Hence, the regions are contained in
in the Borel algebra $\mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}^d)$. It is straight forward to 
show that the change of variables formula applies piece-wise. We include a proof for the sake of self-containedness.

 \begin{restatable}{prop}{paflow}\label{prop:pa-flow}
     Let $F:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^n$ be a piece-wise affine bijection with affine partition $R_1,\ldots,R_n\in \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ of the input space and corresponding affine functions $f_1,\ldots, f_n$.
     Let $X$ be an absolutely continuous random variable. Then 
     $p_{F(X)}(y) = p_{X}(F^{-1}(y))\left|\det\frac{\partial F^{-1}}{\partial y}\right|$,
     where the Jacobean of $F^{-1}$ is evaluated piece-wise. More precisely,
     $\left|\det\frac{\partial F^{-1}}{\partial y}\right| = \sum_i \left|\det\frac{\partial f_i^{-1}}{\partial y}\right| \cdot \mathbb{I}\left[F^{-1}(x)\in R_i\right]$, where $
    \mathbb{I}$ is the indicator function.
 \end{restatable}

\begin{proof}
We define the random variable 
$$C: \mathbb{R}^d\to \{1,\ldots, n\}; x\mapsto \sum_{k=1}^n k\cdot \mathbb{I}\left[x \in R_k\right]$$ and consider the conditional probability densities
$p_{X}(x\mid C=i) = P(X\in R_i)^{-1} p_X(x) \cdot \mathbb{I}[x\in R_i]$. Since $F$ is an affine bijection on $R_i$,
the support of $p_X(\cdot | C=i)$, we can employ the change of variables formula and obtain that $p_{F(X)}(y\mid C=i)= P(X\in R_i)^{-1} p_X(f_i^{-1}(y)) \left|\det\frac{\partial f_i^{-1}}{\partial y}\right| \cdot \mathbb{I}[F^{-1}(y)\in R_i]$. Finally, we obtain by the sum rule that 
\begin{align*}
    p_{F(X)}(y) &= \sum_{i=1}^n P(C=i) p_{F(X)}(y\mid C=i) \\
                &=\sum_{i=1}^n P_X(X\in R_i) p_{F(X)}(y\mid C=i) \\
                &= \sum_{i=1}^n p_X(f_i^{-1}(y)) \left|\det\frac{\partial f_i^{-1}}{\partial y}\right| \cdot \mathbb{I}[F^{-1}(y)\in R_i] \\
                &\stackrel{\ast}{=} \sum_{i=1}^n \left(\underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^n p_X(f_i^{-1}(y))\mathbb{I}[F^{-1}(y)\in R_j]}_{=p_X(F^{-1}(y))}\right) \left|\det\frac{\partial f_i^{-1}}{\partial y}\right| \cdot \mathbb{I}[F^{-1}(y)\in R_i] \\
                &= p_X(F^{-1}(y)) \sum_{i=1}^n \left|\det\frac{\partial f_i^{-1}}{\partial y}\right| \cdot \mathbb{I}[F^{-1}(y)\in R_i] \\
                &= p_{X}(F^{-1}(y))\left|\det\frac{\partial F^{-1}}{\partial y}\right|,
\end{align*}
where $(\ast)$ holds since 
$\mathbb{I}[F^{-1}(y)\in R_i]\cdot \mathbb{I}[F^{-1}(y)\in R_j]=\delta_{ij} \mathbb{I}[F^{-1}(y)\in R_i]$, where $\delta_{ij}=\begin{cases} 1; i=j \\ 0; \text{ else}\end{cases}$ is the Kronecker-Delta.\qed
\end{proof}



Next, we consider the choice of the base distribution.

\begin{restatable}{prop}{padensity}\label{prop:pw_affine}
  Let $p_D$ be defined by a piece-wise affine flow $F$ and a log-piece-wise affine base distribution $p_B$. Then $\log p_D$ is piece-wise affine. \ 
\end{restatable}

\begin{proof}
  As we have seen,

  \begin{align*}
    \log p_D (\mathbf{x}) &= \log
    p_{B} (F (x)) + \log \left| \textrm{det}\frac{\partial
  F}{\partial x}  \right|
  \end{align*}

  Since $F$ and $\log p_B(\cdot)$ are piece-wise affine, $\log p_{B} (F (x))$ is also piece-wise affine. Similarly, $\left|\textrm{det}\frac{\partial F}{\partial x} \right|$ is piece-wise constant, which implies that $\log\left|\textrm{det}\frac{\partial F}{\partial x} \right|$ is piece-wise constant too. The claim follows immediately.\qed
\end{proof}


If restrict the flow model to further have a constant Jacobian determinant, then we obtain an even more intriguing way defining density level sets by the flow.
\begin{restatable}{prop}{pudl}
  \label{prop:levelsets}
  If the determinant of the Jacobian of a flow $F$ on $\mathbb{R}^d$ is constant, then $F$ maps upper density level sets of the target distribution to upper density level sets of the base distribution.
  Hence, if $B$ is a $p$-radial monotonic distribution over the domain of $F$, then there is a function $r:[0,1)\to \mathbb{R}_+$ such that $\textrm{UDL}_{F(B)}(q) = F(\mathbb{B}^d_p(r(q)))$.
\end{restatable}

\begin{proof}
  This is a direct consequence of the change of variables formula.
  \begin{eqnarray*}
    F (\{ x \mid \log p_D (x) > t \}) & = & \{ F (x) \mid \log p_D (x) > t
    \}\\
    &= & \left\{ F (x) \mid \log p_B (F
    (x)) > t - \underbrace{\log \left| \textrm{det}\frac{\partial
  F}{\partial x}  \right|}_{\text{const}} \right\}\\
    & = & \{ y \mid \log p_B (y) > t'\},
  \end{eqnarray*}
  which is obviously an upper log-density level set w.r.t. the latent
  distribution $B$. The last equation holds since $F$ is a bijection and $\log \left| \textrm{det}\frac{\partial
  F}{\partial x}  \right|$ is constant. We combine the observation with the idea from \ref{prop:lp_ball_udl} and conclude that for radial monotonic $B$ and $r(q) = \textrm{quantile}_{|B|_p}(q)$ the identity
  $\textrm{UDL}_{F(B)}(q) = F\left(\mathbb{B}^d_p(r(q))\right)$ is indeed correct.\qed
\end{proof}

The latter proposition is especially attractive for abstract interpretation methods.
For a radial monotonic base distribution w.r.t. the $1$- or the $\infty$-norm, density level sets are definable by linear constraints.
They can therefore act as an initial
set that is propagated through the flow to obtain an approximation
of the upper density level set of the data distribution. As we can derive from Proposition \ref{prop:lp_ball_udl}, the function $r$ is simply the quantile function of the $p$-norm distribution of $B$. Hence, Proposition \ref{prop:levelsets} yields an effective procedure. The approach is summarized in Figure \ref{fig:abstact-interpretation}. A major challenge in the application, however, will be the tightness of the approximation of the non-linearity. There is a delicate trade off to be made between tightness of approximation and complexity of the description. Interesting work in this direction has been done by Stanley Bak \cite{bak_nnenum_2021}.

