\section{Related Work}
In the verification domain, our paper is conceptually in-line with the recently introduced Neuro-Symbolic verification framework by Xie et al.~\cite{xie_neurosymbolic_2022}. 
They propose verifying high-level semantics of neural network by using other specialized networks for capturing the semantics in the specification. 
In our case, the specialized network is our flow model that we use for verifying another neural network, for example an MNIST-classifier.

Similar to our concern of designing a flow model such that it can be verified using existing verification infrastructure, there are recent works on automated preprocessing of neural network via pruning \cite{guidotti2020verification} or via regularization techniques \cite{leofante2023verification,boing2022training} for the same goal, namely making them more suitable for verification with state-of-the-art verifiers.

Flow models are on the forefront of modern density estimation techniques and have received significant attention over the last decade \cite{papamakarios_normalizing_2019}. A constant Jacobian determinant is usually observed at the time of the introduction of the respective layer in the context of the likelihood computation \cite{dinh_nice_2015,ma_macow_2019,kingma_improved_2016,huang_neural_2018,papamakarios_masked_2017}, although typically without further investigation of the induced properties. The role of the Jacobian determinant in general has been investigated in the context of the exploding determinant phenomenon \cite{kim_softflow_2020,DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2102-06539,lyu_paracflows_2022}. There is also a notable application of flow architectures with constant Jacobian determinant for anomaly detection. OneFlow uses a constant Jacobian determinant to compute and minimize the volume the image of a unit hyper sphere around the origin in the latent space, drawing a connection between flows and deep one-class SVMs \cite{maziarka_oneflow_2022}.

\section{Further Directions}
We summarize the research and engineering challenges that we belief to be important for the advancement of the field:
    (1) Verification tools need to provide more complete support for the capabilities of deep learning frameworks such as torch, including support for scenarios that involve more than one network. 
    (2) Theoretical and practical understanding of the expressivity of piece-wise affine uniformly scaling flows. To the best of our knowledge there is no known universal approximation theorem that applies to our architectures \cite{teshima_couplingbased_2020,lyu_paracflows_2022,lee_universal_2021,koehler_representational_2021,zhang_approximation_2020}.
    (3) Well calibrated density level sets are crucial for interpretable verification results. Improving the consistency of density level sets of deep learning models is an important challenge.

