\section{Methodology}
This section describes the methodology of the proposed fabric selection robotic system (Fig.~\ref{fig:fig1}), detailing the data acquisition, multimodal perception, model training, and inference processes that enable accurate fabric property prediction and selection.

\subsection{Visual Data Processing and Fabric Localization}
The overall system workflow is illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:fig2}. During system operation, an Intel RealSense D435 camera mounted on the robotic arm captures RGB images and depth data to provide the necessary visual information for fabric localization and segmentation. 
To facilitate fabric identification and ensure consistent data association, ArUco tags are attached to each fabric sample, serving as unique IDs for pose tracking on the hanger. This step uses standard computer vision techniques to identify and record each sample’s position and orientation in 3D space. Depth data along the Z-axis is integrated with ArUco-based tracking to enhance pose estimation and account for shape variations or hanger misalignments. This integrated process ensures reliable visual localization, supporting the subsequent multimodal perception pipeline.

Following localization, the Segment Anything Model (SAM) \cite{Segment_Anything} performs pixel-level segmentation to isolate each fabric sample from the background and neighboring objects in the RGB image. The segmented images are resized to 224$\times$224 pixels and stored with structured metadata, comprising tuples \( (F_i, P_i, I_i) \), where \( F_i \) denotes the fabric ID, \( P_i \in \mathbb{R}^3 \) represents the 3D position vector, and \( I_i \) corresponds to the segmented RGB image. This structured data ensures consistency across sensing modalities and supports downstream multimodal reasoning and property prediction tasks.

