As a widely recognized industry standard,
SPEC CPU2017 serves as a `standard athletic stadium' for CPU evaluation, utilizing forty-three crafted workloads and three prescribed standard datasets (`test,' `train,' `ref'). SPEC CPU2017 recommends some specific workload and system configurations while leaving ~\textit{other system configurations undefined}, exposing arbitrary specificity and ambiguity. Regarding the arbitrary specificity,  it employs the `O3' compiler level/flag and sets the number of copies/threads of the workload to the maximum number of hardware threads supported by the CPU. Regarding arbitrary ambiguity, it chooses to make the compiler, Operating System (OS), memory, and other system configurations undefined. Under this case, different CPUs are assessed and compared, with scores indicating performance and higher scores representing better performance. It raises a valid question as to why arbitrary specificity and arbitrary ambiguity co-exist in the SPEC CPU evaluation methodology.

