In questioning the validity of the above decision in the SPEC CPU evaluation methodology,  we have two assumptions: First, adhering strictly to some system and workload configurations - arbitrary specificity - proves not meaningful. Only reporting the performance under a specific configuration will hide many scenarios in which the CPU or the differences between CPUs may behave significantly differently. Second, allowing the choice of non-mandated system configurations - arbitrary ambiguity - will not provide a fair playground as it fails to establish equivalent configurations for comparing different CPUs.

We designed a group of experiments to confirm our presumptions.  When designing valid configurations, we largely followed the decisions made by SPECCPU 2017. We only vary the compiler level/flag and the number of copies/threads of the workload while making the compiler, Operating System (OS), memory, and other system configurations the same. We include the settings in which different workloads may achieve optimal performance into the valid configurations. For example, for the 605.mcf\_s workload, it achieves the optimal performance when the compiler level/flag is `O1', so we include it in the valid configurations. We exclude the compiler level/flag `O0'. Consequently, it has a total of 903 different configurations (43 workloads with 21 configurations per workload).

Our experiments reveal that accessing the same CPU leads to highly variable evaluation outcomes under different configurations. For instance, a 74.49x performance difference was observed on the same CPU, a x86 Skylake micro-architecture CPU (CPU A). Furthermore, when comparing two different CPUs under different configurations, contrary results were observed. For example, when comparing CPU A with an ARM CPU (CPU B), CPU A outperformed CPU B in 441 configurations, while the latter was superior in the remaining 505 configurations across 946 different configurations (22 configurations per workload). Moreover, the arbitrary ambiguity in the SPEC recommended configuration provides users with significant flexibility, thereby introducing ambiguity into evaluation outcomes. For example, when evaluating CPU A performance using Intel's ICC compiler is 1.4 times greater than that obtained with the GCC compiler.  Given the widespread use of the SPEC CPU benchmark suites in both academic and industrial contexts, our findings have significant implications and indicate that we need a new CPU evaluation methodology rather than that of SPEC CPU. 


