Inspired by the previous work of evaluatology~\cite{zhan2024evaluatology,zhan2024short}, we propose an innovative CPU evaluation methodology, as shown in Figure~\ref{cpu-evaluatology}. To avoid arbitrary specificity, it considers all workload and system configurations valid, the ensemble of which we formally define as ~\textit{evaluation conditions (in short, EC)}. 
  To avoid arbitrary ambiguity, it mandates that each EC configuration be well-defined. To reduce the cost, we propose a sampling approach to select a subset of the EC configurations. 
  Rather than only being concerned with the outcome under the SPEC recommended configuration, it treats all outcomes under the subset of EC configurations as equally important because it reveals many scenarios in which the CPU or the performance differences between two CPUs may behave significantly differently. 
