\section{Background and Motivation}\label{Sec_B&M}
\subsection{The Background of SPEC CPU2017}
Benchmarks play a crucial role in evaluation, and the SPEC CPU is the most prominent benchmark. Over time, six versions of the SPEC CPU benchmark suite have been released, including SPEC CPU89~\cite{SPECCPU89}, SPEC CPU92~\cite{SPECCPU92}, SPEC CPU95~\cite{SPECCPU95}, SPEC CPU2000~\cite{SPECCPU2000}, SPEC CPU2006~\cite{SPECCPU2006}, and SPEC CPU2017~\cite{SPECCPU2017}. 





Furthermore, SPEC CPU2017 categorizes its benchmarks based on calculation type into integer (int) and floating-point (fp) workloads. According to the evaluation metrics shown in Table~\ref{SPEC-Metric}, SPEC divides the workloads into the SPECrate sub-suite (single-thread, multi-copies workloads; higher scores mean that more work is done per unit of time) and the SPECspeed sub-suite (single-copy, multi-threads workloads; higher scores mean that less time is needed). Therefore, SPEC CPU2017 comprises four sub-suites: fprate, intrate, fpspeed, and intspeed~\cite{SPECCPU2017}. 
The standard output files of SPEC CPU2017 will report the composite score (run each workload three times and use the median score; the sub-suite's score is the geometric mean of all its workloads' scores) of these four sub-suites.



\subsection{Motivation}\label{Sec_Mot}

This section shows that adhering strictly to system and workload configurations - arbitrary specificity - produces meaningless CPU evaluation outcomes. 
We compare two CPUs using SPEC CPU2017: one is a x86 Skylake micro-architecture CPU, and the other is an ARM CPU. The machine configurations are consistent with Table~\ref{Machine-Inf} in Section~\ref{TheEvaluationsection}.






%This section shows that we can not achieve optimal performance under SPEC recommended configuration both on our x86 and ARM machines. With the machines in Table~\ref{Machine-Inf},  our experiments show that the comparison between using SPEC CPU2017 under SPEC recommended configuration is not consistent with other configurations.


SPEC CPU2017 employs the `O3' compiler level/flag and sets the number of copies/threads of the workloads to the maximum number of hardware threads supported by the CPU while choosing to make the compiler, Operating System (OS), memory, and other system configurations undefined. In our experiments, we vary different compiler levels/flags and the number of copies/threads of the workloads while ~\textit{making the compiler, Operating System (OS), memory, and other system configurations the same}. 

As shown in Table~\ref{SPEC-Optimal_Con}, using the SPEC recommended `O3' compiler level/flag and setting the number of copies/threads to the maximum number of hardware threads supported by the CPU, 31 out of 43 workloads did not reach the highest SPEC score on our x86 processor, and 17 out of 43 workloads failed to achieve the highest score on our ARM processor. For some workloads, higher SPEC scores were achieved with the `O1' or `O2' compiler levels/flags compared to `O3'.  For example, for the 605.mcf\_s workload, it achieves the optimal performance when the compiler level/flag is `O1', so we include it in the valid configurations. Similarly, using different numbers of copies/threads, such as 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, and 32, rather than the maximum number of hardware threads, resulted in higher SPEC scores for certain workloads.  We exclude the compiler level/flag `O0'. 

