There are three important points to draw from Figure~\ref{SPEC-ARM-X86}(a). First, depending on varying configurations, the magnitude of the y-axis can significantly fluctuate (0.89 vs 1.34). Second, depending on varying configurations, the x86 processor either performs better than the ARM one (y is greater than 1.0 in 18 configurations) or worse than the ARM one (y is less than 1.0 in 4 configurations). Third, the comparison across the 22 configurations is unpredictable, and conclusions drawn from one specific configuration cannot be generalized to others. For example, while the SPEC recommended configuration indicates that the ARM processor performs better, using the same `-O3' compiler level/flag with 56 copies of the workload shows that the x86 processor performs better.

