There are three important points to draw from this group of experiments. First, the performance comparison between x86 and ARM processors across 22 different configurations of each SPEC CPU2017 workload varies significantly, with even 19 workloads yielding contrary conclusions. Second, using the SPEC recommended configuration to compare CPU A and CPU B performance is meaningless, as varying configurations can lead to significant data shifts and may even produce entirely contrary conclusions.  Third, considering that the SPEC CPU methodology keeps the other system configuration undefined, the evaluation outcomes will be much more significantly varied than those in our experiments as we keep the other system configuration the same. 

Based on the above discussion, when comparing different CPUs, it is much more reliable to evaluate under the entire valid configuration space of an EC, with each configuration being well-defined as opposed to relying on arbitrary specificity and arbitrary ambiguity in the SPEC CPU methodology.  


\section{CPU evaluatology}~\label{TheMethodSection}

This section presents our innovative CPU evaluation methodology: CPU evaluatology. Shown in Figure~\ref{Well_defined_EC}, CPU evaluatology consists of four essential steps. The first step is to design valid EC configurations, with each one being well-defined. The purpose is to avoid arbitrary specificity and arbitrary ambiguity found in SPEC CPU. The second step is to sample a subset of EC configurations. The aim is to reduce the evaluation cost. The third step is to treat all evaluation outcomes under the subset of EC configurations as equally important. The aim is to reveal the CPU performance in different scenarios. The fourth step is to utilize confidence level and confidence interval to report the evaluation outcomes under each valid EC configuration. The aim is to avoid biases and to achieve consistent and comparable evaluation outcomes. Throughout this section,  we take SPEC CPU2017 as an example to show how we apply our CPU evaluatology to remedy its shortcomings.

