\subsection{The design of valid EC configurations with each one being well-defined}\label{step_one}

Evaluating a CPU in isolation falls short of meeting the expectations of stakeholders, and hence, it is crucial to create a minimal evaluation system (ES)~\cite{zhan2024evaluatology,zhan2024short}. In addition to the CPU, a minimal ES includes workloads and the essential hardware and software systems required to run the workload on the CPU, such as disk, memory, OS, and compiler. We explain the meaning of ``minimal''.  For example, we exclude the networks from the minimal ES because the workloads only need to be executed locally when evaluating a CPU. In other words, the CPU evaluation can be seen as an experiment that deliberately applies a well-defined EC to a CPU to create a minimal ES. In this case, an EC can be formally defined as a minimal ES with the subject removed~\cite{zhan2024short}. 

We present a hierarchical methodology to define an EC and its valid configurations in CPU evaluation.
As shown in Figure~\ref{Well_defined_EC}, an EC consists of five components~\cite{zhan2024evaluatology}, and each component has various valid configurations. At the highest level are the problems that are of concern to users, such as arithmetic operations, matrix transposition, or primality testing. The next level is the problem instances, which are instantiations of the problems, providing data variables and constraints, such as the arithmetic operations on two 32-bit signed integers $n_1$ and $n_2$, transposing a $4\times4$ integer matrix A, or determining whether a given 32-bit unsigned integer $n$ is prime. The third level is the algorithms, an abstract representation of the solution for the given problem, which can be described in natural language or pseudocode. The next level is algorithm implementations, which involve the implementation of an algorithm in a specific programming language supported by the CPU, such as a C program for arithmetic operations on two 32-bit signed integers. The final level is the support systems, which encompass all system environments required to run the program on the specific CPU, including but not limited to the compilers, compiler levels/flags, copies/threads, OSes, OS settings, memories, memory settings, disks, and disk settings.

To avoid arbitrary specificity, we design the valid configurations of an EC instead of a single recommended configuration. Meanwhile,  to avoid arbitrary ambiguity, each EC configuration should be well-defined. That is to say, we define the other system configuration clearly. The SPEC CPU2017 only provides a small subset of valid configurations in the design of the EC configurations. SPEC CPU2017 recommends compiler configurations with the `O3' level/flag and setting the number of copies/threads to the maximum number of hardware threads supported by the CPU, which introduces arbitrary specificity. On the other hand, SPEC does not define the other system configurations, such as compiler, OS, and memory, which introduces arbitrary ambiguity as it fails to use the same evaluation configurations to evaluate the CPU or compare different CPUs. 

To emphasize our methodology with the primary aim of avoiding arbitrary specificity and arbitrary ambiguity, this article does not intend to design the CPU benchmark suite from scratch. Instead, we make a small but efficient change to the SPEC CPU methodology. To avoid arbitrary specificity, we vary different compiler levels/flags and the number of copies/threads of the workloads. Even this small change will lead to a large increase in configuration space. To prune the configuration space, we only include the settings of two configurations ( compiler levels/flags and the number of copies/threads of the workloads)  in which different SPEC CPU2017 workloads may achieve optimal performance. Meanwhile, to avoid arbitrary ambiguity, we choose to make the compiler, Operating System (OS), memory, and other system configurations the same. We think this restriction is enough powerful to reveal the issue of arbitrary ambiguity. If we continue keeping the other system configuration undefined, it will not be surprising that the performance will vary wildly significantly. Please note that making the compiler, operating system (OS), memory, and other system configurations the same also means that they are well-defined.

In addition, for each workload,  we utilize additional datasets rather than being limited to only three or six input datasets for each problem. Contrary to SPEC CPU2017, where the `ref' dataset is selected to measure the score of real-world processors, we do not impose restrictions on the dataset to a large extent. Instead, we chose datasets of different sizes closer to the `ref' dataset in our group of experiments. Our experiments in Section~\ref{Case-studies} validated the soundness of our design. By varying dataset sizes, we uncovered intriguing phenomena where the execution time does not linearly increase with larger datasets. Instead, it displays irregular local maxima, a pattern not captured by the SPEC CPU2017 benchmarks


To better clarify the concept of EC and its essential components. We use the EC terminologies~\cite{zhan2024evaluatology,zhan2024short} to describe the design of SPEC CPU2017. SPEC CPU2017 encompasses twenty-four \textit{problems} involving interpreters, compilers, route planning, simulation, format conversion, compression, artificial intelligence, modeling, high-performance computing, and image manipulation domains. Among them, nineteen out of twenty-four problems consist of six input datasets: three for the SPECrate category and three for the SPECspeed category. The other five problems consist of three input datasets, which are included in either the SPECrate or SPECspeed categories. The three input datasets are the `test,' `train,' and `ref' datasets. Consequently, the number of ~\textit{problem instances} amounts to 129. When assessing an actual CPU, SPEC CPU2017 utilizes the `ref' dataset as the standard for evaluation.

For each problem, there is a specific ~\textit{algorithm} and its corresponding ~\textit{algorithm implementations}. For example, in addressing the instance of the playing Go problem, where the input data constitutes a specific game on a $19\times19$ board, the Monte Carlo Tree Search algorithm~\cite{browne2012survey} is employed, and the algorithm implementations encompass the C++ single-threaded version and the C++ multi-threaded version. 

Nineteen out of twenty-four algorithms have two implementations (single-threaded version and multi-threaded version), and the other five have only one implementation (either single-threaded or multi-threaded version). Thus, there are twenty-four algorithms and forty-three algorithm implementations. Furthermore, eight algorithms are fully implemented in C, six algorithms are entirely in C++, and four algorithms are solely in Fortran. Three algorithms are partially implemented in C and partially in Fortran, two algorithms have modules implemented in C++ and other modules in C, and one algorithm comprises different modules implemented in C, C++, and Fortran.

We further discuss why we think SPEC CPU2017 only provides a small subset of valid configurations in the design of the EC configurations. For example, in the \textit{problems} level, it does not consider network applications, big data applications, IoT applications, and others.  %In the \textit{problem instances} level, it only provides three limited datasets for each workload and ignores other datasets. 
In the \textit{algorithms} level, it does not consider different algorithms resolving a specific problem, for instance, the deep learning algorithm in playing Go. 
In the \textit{implementation} level, it does not consider other programming languages such as Java, Python, Go, and more. 
%In the \textit{support system} level, it does not define the compiler, Os, memory, and disk and ignores the network.
The aim of this article is to resolve the issue of arbitrary specificity and arbitrary ambiguity found in SPEC CPU2017. So, we do not intend to fix other issues. 

