\subsection{Sampling a subset of EC configurations}\label{step_two}

Due to the extremely high cost of exploring all valid EC configurations, it is essential to propose a sampling approach to reduce the entire EC configuration space to an affordable subset of configurations. There are many sampling approaches that we can utilize in statistics textbooks. 

The SPEC CPU2017 benchmark suite utilizes sampling techniques to select 43 representative workloads from a wide range of real-world applications.  When designing valid configurations for EC, we largely followed the decisions made by SPECCPU 2017 while making the compiler, Operating System (OS), memory, and other system configurations the same. This controlled expansion of configuration size is both limited and manageable; hence, the focus of this article is not on sampling techniques. Nonetheless, sampling remains a crucial component of our methodology to extract an affordable subset of EC configurations. The importance of sampling will be revisited when developing a CPU benchmark suite from scratch.



%of increased varied scenarios. we should sample a subset of EC configurations to reduce the evaluation cost.   The sampling techniques can also be applied to choose configurations of support systems. Given that sampling techniques are not the primary focus of this article, we utilize the SPEC CPU2017 workloads and system configurations proposed in Section~\ref{step_one} as our subset of EC configurations.







\subsection{Treating all outcomes under the subset of EC configurations
as equally important}

By utilizing the appropriate sampling approach, we can both reduce the evaluation cost and achieve consistent outcomes. After that, the subset of EC configurations is a sample of the valid EC configurations.
We should treat the outcomes under this subset as equally important. Only reporting the CPU performance under a specific configuration will hide many scenarios in which the CPU or the difference between two CPUs may behave significantly differently.  We cannot discard any one of the evaluation outcomes because each one reflects the CPU performance under a different scenario. 

We also cannot choose a so-called representative outcome for the sake of simplification. No matter how representative the outcome is, it is only a local point in the sample space, which will inevitably hide the behavior of some other scenarios. We illustrate this using an example of workload execution time variation with the changing sizes of the datasets. According to the theory of algorithmic time complexity, as the dataset size increases, the workload execution time should also increase accordingly. However, we observe that when using SPEC CPU2017 workloads, the execution time does not consistently increase with larger datasets but instead exhibits irregular local maxima. It is crucial to treat all these evaluation outcomes as equally important to observe this phenomenon. If we were to select only a representative dataset or dismiss these local maxima as outliers, we would not be able to capture this behavior in the final report. For more details, please refer to Section~\ref{Case-studies}.

%We also cannot add weights to each evaluation outcome arbitrarily, and the weights just give a representation of the outcomes under specific requirements, which is subjective and introduces bias.


\subsection{Utilizing confidence level and confidence interval to report the evaluation outcomes}

We utilize confidence levels and confidence intervals to report evaluation outcomes under a subset of EC configurations when accessing the same CPU or comparing different CPUs. 

~\textit{To report the evaluation outcome under a well-defined EC configuration:} When a well-defined EC configuration is applied to a specific CPU, the evaluation outcome can be reported by taking the mean or median of multiple experiments~\cite{telford2007brief}. In CPU evaluation, when a well-defined EC  configuration is applied to a specific CPU, it yields a true value. However, due to the complexity of the OS, software stack, and the CPU's mechanisms, such as superscalar and out-of-order execution, there is inherent randomness in the instruction stream executed by the CPU pipeline. This randomness causes the evaluation outcomes to fluctuate slightly around the true value. To minimize this fluctuation and report the evaluation outcome for a specific CPU under a well-defined EC configuration, multiple experiments should be conducted, and their mean or median should be taken. Using the mean as the outcome can balance out the random noise from different experimental runs but may be affected by outliers and does not represent an actual experimental result. Using the median as the outcome is not influenced by outliers and is an actual experimental result, but it may not utilize all data points, which could impact balancing randomness. In practice, the choice between these methods depends on the specific circumstances. For example, performance monitoring tools like `perf'~\cite{de2010new} report mean values, while SPEC CPU2017 uses the median of scores from individual workloads to calculate the final score~\cite{SPECCPU2017}.

~\textit{To report the evaluation outcomes of a CPU under a subset of EC configurations:}  When a subset of EC configurations are applied to a specific CPU, the evaluation outcomes can be determined using confidence interval at a given confidence level, with the sample mean representing the outcome. In CPU evaluation, using only one specific EC configuration is meaningless and not consistent, and using all valid EC configurations is impractical. Therefore, a subset of EC configurations is sampled for the evaluation. According to our CPU evaluatology, each well-defined EC configuration yields an evaluation outcome, and we treat them as equally important for the evaluation outcomes under this subset of EC configurations. We further calculate the confidence intervals of these outcomes at a given confidence level to represent the evaluation outcomes for the subset of EC configurations. All values are consistent within these confidence intervals and have the same probability of being significant at the given confidence level. The sample mean, being one of the values within the confidence intervals, is thus chosen as the reported evaluation outcome for the evaluation outcomes under this subset of EC configurations. 
%For example, with a 99\% confidence interval of the SPEC score of 1.861 to 2.023, to report the evaluation outcomes of 600.perlbench\_s workload on the Intel Xeon Gold 5120T under a subset of 21 EC configurations, details in Section~\ref{Case-study-one}.

~\textit{To report the comparison outcomes of two CPUs under a subset of EC configurations:} We compare two CPUs under each configuration. For each configuration, we calculate the score ratio between two CPUs. Based on these data, we further calculate the confidence interval of these outcomes at a given confidence level to represent the comparison outcomes of two CPUs under the subset of EC configurations. For example, to report the score ratio of  CPU A to CPU B under a subset of 21 EC configurations, we report it as a 99\% confidence interval of 1.034 to  1.175, which is detailed in Section~\ref{Mean_&_Confidence}.

