There are two important points to draw from Figure~\ref{Method-SPEC-ARM-X86}. First, confidence intervals provide consistency across the valid configurations, as all points within the confidence interval are meaningful at the same confidence level, leading to reliable conclusions. Second, unlike a single SPEC recommended configuration, CPU evaluatology offers deeper insights into the EC configuration space, allowing for more meaningful and consistent CPU comparisons.
%We calculated the sample mean and 95\% confidence intervals of the score ratios between the two CPUs under different configurations. The sample mean of the execution time ratio between the two CPUs under the SPEC recommended configurations is 1.019, with a 95\% confidence interval of (0.903, 1.134) and the interval width is 0.231. Meanwhile, under the retrofitted SPEC CPU2017 configurations, the sample mean of the execution time ratio between the two CPUs is 0.986, with a 95\% confidence interval of (0.950, 1.021), and the interval width is 0.071. The presence of 1 in the 95\% confidence interval suggests that one CPU may outperform the other in certain configurations while underperforming in other configurations.  Furthermore, the 95\% confidence interval under the retrofitted SPEC CPU2017 configurations is significantly smaller than that under the SPEC recommended configurations, and the former interval is entirely encompassed by the latter. 


\subsection{The other case study using CPU evaluatology}\label{Case-studies}
% \setlength{\parindent}{9pt} 

This case study demonstrates that using CPU  evaluatology is capable of uncovering novel insights that might not be achievable merely through the utilization of SPEC CPU2017. 

%Generally, 
The performance of a workload implemented in C/C++ is profoundly influenced by the sizes of its datasets, as the dataset size is closely related to the CPU microarchitecture, including cache size, organization, and management mechanism. 

We select the 507.cactuBSSN\_r workload as the case study. The original setting is the `ref' dataset in SPEC CPU2017. Under CPU evaluatology, by setting the grid size of the input parameter file, we change the input 3-dimension grid size (all dimensions are the same size) of 507.cactuBSSN\_r workload. Figure~\ref{507_X86vsARM} shows the execution time of 507.cactuBSSN\_r workload on the CPU A and CPU B among different input datasets under SPEC recommended configuration. Dataset  $x_{0}$ represents the `ref' dataset ($x_{0} \times x_{0} \times x_{0}$ grid) provided by SPEC CPU2017, and we have selected 10 input grid size smaller than it ($(x_{0}-10) \times (x_{0}-10) \times (x_{0}-10)$ grid to $(x_{0}-1) \times (x_{0}-1) \times (x_{0}-1)$ grid) and 10 input grid size larger than it ($(x_{0}+1) \times (x_{0}+1) \times (x_{0}+1)$ grid to $(x_{0}+10) \times (x_{0}+10) \times (x_{0}+10)$ grid). 

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[scale=0.39]{templates/Six.pdf}
\caption{The execution time of 507.cactuBSSN\_r workload with different input datasets on the x86 and ARM processors. Dataset $x_{0}$ represents the `ref' dataset of SPEC CPU2017, and we find that there are peak (local maximum) points in both two CPUs with the size of dataset changes. The peak points are labeled in the red boxes for the x86 processor and the blue diamonds for the ARM one.}
\label{507_X86vsARM}
\end{figure}


As the size of the input dataset increases, it is expected that the workload execution time will increase accordingly, aligning with the algorithm's time complexity. However, as shown in Fig~\ref{507_X86vsARM}, we find the peak (local maximum) point on both two processors accompanied by the size change of input datasets. This phenomenon contradicts the theoretical workloads algorithm time complexity. We do not only find this phenomenon in 507.cactuBSSN\_r workload but also in some other workloads of SPEC CPU2017 (such as the 503.bwaves\_r, 603.bwaves\_s, 607.cactuBSSN\_s workload). This is because cache size, organization, and management mechanism affect the performance of the C/C++-based workload. In the experiment, we observe that the characteristics related to cache misses are exceptionally high at the peak point. For instance, the L1I Cache MPKI is 35.2, and the L1I Cache miss ratio is 31\% when size is $(x_{0}+3)$ (the peak point) on the x86 processor. Nevertheless, the L1I Cache MPKI is 21.4, and the L1I Cache miss ratio is 18\% when size is $(x_{0}+4)$ (not the peak point). The ARM processor also has the same phenomenon. The workloads implemented in C/C++ languages exhibit significant increases in execution time under the specific size of its data structure due to cache collision arising from their simple, linear memory allocation strategies. Frequent cache misses in these scenarios lead to notably longer execution times.  This phenomenon was also proved by Hanlon et al.~\cite{hanlon2001combinatorics}. However, the utilization of fixed input data in SPEC CPU2017 veiled the identification of peak points, and such an insight could not be discovered.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
%\raggedleft
\includegraphics[scale=0.34]{templates/Seven.pdf}
\caption{The sample mean, confidence level, and confidence interval of 507.cactuBSSN\_r workload under a sample of the configuration varying different input datasets. The red boxes indicate the peak points on the x86 processor, while the blue diamonds indicate the peak points on the ARM processor.}
\label{Confidence_507}
\end{figure} 


Then, we demonstrate that using CPU evaluatology can provide a more persuasive estimate of evaluation outcomes, facilitating a consistent comparison of results under the aforementioned microarchitecture-related scenarios. We utilize the execution time ratio for comparison, calculated by dividing the execution time on the x86 processor by that on the ARM one, to evaluate the performance of two CPUs.  Fig~\ref{Confidence_507} shows our evaluation outcome of the sample mean, confidence interval of 507.cactuBSSN\_r workload on the x86 and ARM processors under the 95\% confidence level. In Fig~\ref{Confidence_507}, different configurations correspond to different datasets in Figure~\ref{507_X86vsARM}. The confidence interval is $(0.914,1.014)$ under the 95\% confidence level of this configuration sample. 
In comparison to the original execution time ratio of $1.054$ provided by SPEC CPU2017 for the single `ref' dataset, we present a sample mean of $0.964$ as the estimate of the execution time ratio of the 507.cactuBSSN\_r workload running on the CPU B over CPU A with a confidence level of 95\%. With the assistance of the confidence interval, any execution time ratio that is calculated according to the peak points of only one CPU falls outside the confidence interval, thereby leading to a more consistent comparison. 


\section{Related Work}\label{Sec_Related_Work}
For the CPU benchmark suites, SPECCPU and PARSEC are two important benchmark suites. SPEC CPU~\cite{SPECCPU} provides a comparative measurement for compute-intensive performance across different hardware, whose workloads are developed from real user applications. In essence, the SPEC CPU is an industry-standardized CPU intensive suite to measure and compare Floating-point/Integer computation performance. The latest SPECCPU 2017~\cite{panda2018wait} benchmark package contains 43 benchmarks.  The Princeton Application Repository for Shared-Memory Computers (PARSEC)~\cite{parsec} is a benchmark suite composed of multi-threaded programs. It includes a number of Intel RMS applications and other typical emerging multi-threaded workloads.  There are 30 multi-threaded workloads in the latest PARSEC 3.0~\cite{zhan2017parsec3.0}. The goal of PARSEC is to create a suite of emerging multi-threaded workloads that can drive chip-multiprocessors (CMPs) research. 
%There also are many micro-benchmarks for CPU benchmarking. For example, the CIAO benchmark~\cite{CIAO} is designed to test the arithmetic capabilities of CPUs for specific floating point and integer operations.

Zhan et al.~\cite{zhan2024evaluatology} propose evaluatology as the science of evaluation
and formally define evaluation as “conducting deliberate experiments where a well-defined evaluation condition is applied to a well-defined subject.” Telford et al.~\cite{telford2007brief} had a brief introduction to the Design of the experiment (DOE); they concluded that the fundamental principles of DOE are randomization, replication, blocking, orthogonality, and factorial experimentation. Wang et al.~\cite{wang2022study} highlighted a significant issue in database performance evaluation: the experimental configuration has a substantial impact on evaluation outcomes. They discussed potential methods to address this problem. Hennessy et al.~\cite{hennessy2019new} changed the algorithm and implementation of matrix multiplication workload and observed several orders of magnitude performance gaps. Chen et al.~\cite{chen2014statistical} proposed a novel non-parametric statistical framework for comparing computer performance with small sample sizes. Thomas et al.~\cite{diciccio1996bootstrap} emphasized that the bootstrap method can automatically construct highly accurate approximate confidence intervals in complex data structures and probabilistic models, thereby enhancing the accuracy and reliability of confidence intervals in real-world applications. Mytkowicz et al.~\cite{mytkowicz2009producing} found the measurement bias under different experimental setups, and proposing methods to avoid and detect measurement bias.

Workload characterization and evaluation are interrelated. 
One common methodology involves profiling specific micro-architecture-related characteristics using hardware performance counters~\cite{panda2018wait,ferdman2011clearing,yasin2014deep}.  ISA related characteristic research~\cite{parsec,bienia2008parsec1,woo1995splash,hoste2006comparing,hoste2007analyzing,hoste2006performance,farhaditrace,hoste2007microarchitecture} involves profiling ISA-instruction traces not tied to specific micro-architectures.  Shao et al.~\cite{shao2013isa} proposed an ISA-independent workload analysis method, using the IR of ILJDIT~\cite{campanoni2010highly} to analyze workloads' ISA-independent metrics. Many studies conduct workload characterization based on SPEC CPU~\cite{shao2013isa,phansalkar2005measuring,phansalkar2007analysis,limaye2018workload,panda2018wait,wang2023wpc}. Phansalkar et al.~\cite{phansalkar2005measuring,phansalkar2007analysis} provided a subset of SPEC CPU2000 and SPEC CPU2006 with ISA related characteristics and validated the coverage of SPEC CPU2006, even after removing some EDA programs~\cite{phansalkar2007analysis}. Panda et al.~\cite{panda2018wait} found that certain EDA applications, database applications, and graph applications are grouped within the micro-architecture-related characteristics cluster of SPEC CPU2017, thereby confirming the coverage of SPEC CPU2017.

\section{Conclusion}\label{Sec_Conclusion}

This article reveals the two limitations of the SPEC CPU methodology. It adheres strictly to some workload and system configurations - arbitrary specificity. Arbitrary specificity proves meaningless, hiding many scenarios, as evidenced by significant performance variations observed on the same CPU. Meanwhile, it leaves other system configurations undefined - arbitrary ambiguity. Arbitrary ambiguity fails to provide a fair playground for comparing different CPUs.
We propose an innovative CPU evaluation methodology consisting of four essential steps: the design of valid and well-defined EC configurations, sampling a subset of EC configurations, treating all evaluation outcomes equally important, and utilizing confidence levels and intervals to report the evaluation outcomes. 




