\section{Introduction}
Eigenvalue problems are fundamental in science and engineering, with applications in areas such as photonic crystals \cite{dorfler2011photonic}, uncertainty quantification \cite{smith2024uncertainty}, and quantum theory \cite{dirac1929quantum}. For instance, the computation of an approximate Karhunen-Lo{\`e}ve (KL) expansion requires solving very large eigenvalue problems to obtain many KL eigenpairs \cite{breuer1991use,schwab2006karhunen}. Similarly, electronic structures are often modeled by Hartree-Fock equations or Kohn-Sham equations, which also involve many eigenpairs and can be reduced to repeatedly solving large-scale linearized eigenvalue problems through discretization and self-consistent field iterations \cite{cances2023density,chen2014adaptive,chen2013numerical,lebris2005computational,martin2020electronic,saad2010numerical}. 


The computational cost of solving large-scale eigenvalue problems is significant \cite{golub2013matrix,saad1992numerical,sholl2009density}, particularly when a substantial number of eigenpairs are required. A major bottleneck arises from orthogonalization procedures, whose computational complexity grows dramatically with the problem size. 
We understand that an alternative approach, particularly for approximations in Kohn-Sham density functional theory (DFT), is solving the energy minimization problem \cite{dai2017conjugate,Dai2021,payne1992iterative,schneider2009direct,Zhang2014,Zhao2015}. Notably, even in these approaches, orthogonalization operations are generally unavoidable due to the imposition of orthogonality constraints.


To address this computational challenge, Dai et al. proposed an extended gradient flow based Kohn-Sham DFT model with orthogonality-preserving solutions, effectively eliminating the need for explicit orthogonalization in computation \cite{dai2020,dai2021convergent}. However, the success of this approach critically depends on two factors: (1) the initial data must be orthogonal, and (2) numerical issues (e.g., round-off errors) must be absent, as their accumulation can destroy the orthogonality of the computed solutions. It is significant to set up a robust model for eigenvalue problems in practice.


When many eigenpairs are required, in this paper, we introduce a quasi-Grassmannian gradient flow model as a promising alternative for solving eigenvalue problems of operators. In our model, evolution is governed by the negative gradient---an extension of the standard gradient on Grassmann manifold to the ambient space. This design ensures that as time progresses, the solutions converge toward the Grassmann manifold, inherently exhibiting asymptotic orthogonality---even if the initial data are non-orthogonal, the solutions naturally converge to be orthogonal over time. Moreover, this asymptotic orthogonality mitigates the impact of numerical perturbations, so that, despite potential computational errors, the solution eventually remains orthogonal. Thus, this model circumvents the dual limitations of existing methods, offering a more reliable and practical approach.


More precisely, in this paper we propose and analyze the quasi-Grassmannian gradient flow model for eigenvalue problems of linear operators, particularly in settings where many eigenpairs are required. We analyze the well-posedness of this model and derive the solutions' analytical representations.
We also conduct a detailed analysis of the asymptotic behavior of the solutions as time goes to infinity. Notably, we demonstrate that the solutions exhibit asymptotic orthogonality, meaning that even if the initial data are not orthogonal, the solutions naturally evolve toward being orthogonal over time. Additionally, we prove that the gradient exponentially approaches zero, indicating that the model converges exponentially to an equilibrium state, where the system reaches stability. We further show that the energy decreases exponentially and ultimately converges to its minimum value. Importantly, the equilibrium point of the gradient flow model corresponds to the solution of the minimization problem. Since the solution of the minimization problem is equivalent to the solution of the eigenvalue problem, this demonstrates the potential and effectiveness of the proposed gradient flow model for solving operator eigenvalue problems.


While this paper focuses on a quasi-Grassmannian gradient flow model for solving operator eigenvalue problems, and Dai et al. \cite{dai2020,dai2021convergent} concentrate on an extended gradient flow model for the same purpose, we mention that gradient flow models, in general, play a crucial role across a wide range of fields. One notable application is in computing the ground state solution of Bose-Einstein condensates (BEC) \cite{Bao2004,Bao2007,Wang2014}. Beyond physics, gradient flow models have also been widely applied in machine learning and neural networks \cite{ambrosio2008gradient,bottou2018optimization}, where they provide a fundamental framework for understanding optimization dynamics. A seminal example is Oja's pioneering work \cite{oja1982simplified,oja1989neural}, which explored principal component extraction using unsupervised neural networks, laying the foundation for learning rules in artificial neural networks. 
These learning rules are often expressed as nonlinear differential or difference equations \cite{oja1985stochastic}, which are inherently consistent with the framework of our quasi-Grassmannian gradient flow model discussed herein. 


The existing related research has primarily focused on finite-dimensional settings, particularly in problems requiring the computation of many eigenpairs, such as matrix-based models like Oja's flow. Significant progress has been made in analyzing the convergence and stability of such models \cite{chen1998global,yan1994global}. 
In fact, the analysis of Oja's flow can be seamlessly integrated into our more general framework. Our model not only accommodates such matrix-based models under weaker conditions but also naturally extends beyond traditional matrix settings to the operators.


The remainder of the paper is organized as follows. Section 2 introduces the fundamental notation and formulates the operator eigenvalue problem. Section 3 presents the quasi-Grassmannian gradient flow model, analyzing its well-posedness, and deriving the solutions' analytical representation. Section 4 focuses on the asymptotic analysis, including asymptotic orthogonality, convergence to equilibrium, and convergence to the minimizer. Section 5 concludes the paper and outlines directions for our on-going and future works. Finally, Appendix provides the detailed proofs for the lemmas applied.
\section{Preliminaries}
In this section, we introduce the basic notation and preliminary lemmas, and discuss the eigenvalue problem of linear operators.
\subsection{Notation and preliminary lemma}
\subsubsection{Notation} 
We denote by $W^{s, p}(\Omega)$ $(p\geqslant 1, s\geqslant 0)$ the standard Sobolev spaces with the induced norm $\|\cdot\|_{s, p}$, where $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^d$ $(d\in \mathbb{N}_+)$ is a bounded domain with a regular boundary (see, e.g. \cite{evans2022partial,zeidler2013nonlinear}). For $p=2$, we denote by $H^s(\Omega)=W^{s, 2}(\Omega)$ with the norm $\|\cdot\|_{s}=\|\cdot\|_{s, 2}$ and 
$$H_0^1(\Omega)=\left\{u\in H^1(\Omega):\left.u\right|_{\partial \Omega}=0\right\},$$ where $\left.u\right|_{\partial \Omega}=0$ is understood in the sense of trace. 

Consider the Hilbert spaces
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
		\big(L^2(\Omega)\big)^N = \left\{\left(u_1, u_2, \cdots, u_N\right): u_i\in L^2(\Omega) , i = 1, 2, \cdots,N\right\}.
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
		\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N = \left\{\left(u_1, u_2, \cdots, u_N\right): u_i\in H_0^1(\Omega) , i = 1, 2, \cdots,N\right\}.
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
Let 
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
		U=\left(u_1, u_2, \cdots, u_N\right) \text{ and }V=\left(v_1, v_2, \cdots, v_N\right).
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
We introduce $L^2$ inner product matrix as follows
\begin{equation*}
	U^{\top} V=\Big(\left(u_i, v_j\right)\Big)_{i, j=1}^N \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N},\quad \forall U, V  \in 	\big(L^2(\Omega)\big)^N, 
\end{equation*}
where $(u_i, v_j)= \int_{\Omega} u_i(x) v_j(x) \mathrm{d}x$. And the inner products $(\cdot, \cdot)$ and its associated norm are then defined as
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
		(U, V)=\operatorname{tr}\left( U^{\top} V\right), \quad \| U \|=\sqrt{(U, U)},\quad \forall U, V  \in 	\big(L^2(\Omega)\big)^N.
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
Similarly, the inner products $(\cdot, \cdot)_1$ on $\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$ and the corresponding norm are given by
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
		(U, V)_1=\operatorname{tr}\left( U^{\top} V +\left(\nabla U \right)^{\top} \nabla V  \right) , \quad\| U \|_1=\sqrt{(U, U)_1}, \quad \forall U, V  \in\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N.
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
Here $\nabla U$ is obtained by taking the gradient of each component of $U$; that is, $$\nabla U = \left(\nabla u_1, \nabla u_2, \cdots, \nabla u_N\right).$$


For $\mathcal{F}=\left(\mathcal{F}_1, \mathcal{F}_2, \cdots, \mathcal{F}_N\right) \in\left(\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N\right)^{\prime}=\big(H^{-1}(\Omega)\big)^N$, we set
$$
\mathcal{F}^\top U=\Big( \left\langle\mathcal{F}_i, u_j\right\rangle\Big)_{i, j=1}^N \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N}, \quad  \forall U \in \left(H_0^1\left(\Omega\right)\right)^N,
$$
where $\langle\cdot, \cdot\rangle$ is the duality pair of $H^{-1}(\Omega)$ and $H_0^1(\Omega)$. We then define
$$
\langle\mathcal{F}, U\rangle  = \operatorname{tr}\left(\mathcal{F}^\top U \right),\quad  \forall U \in \left(H_0^1\left(\Omega\right)\right)^N,
$$
and the norm
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
		\|\mathcal{F}\|_{-1}= \sup\limits_{U \in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N \backslash \{0\}} \frac{\left|\langle\mathcal{F}, U\rangle \right|}{\| U \|_1}.
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}


We introduce the Stiefel manifold as follows
$$	\mathcal{M}^N=\left\{U \in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N: U^\top U=I_N\right\},$$
and it is clear to see that
$$U \in \mathcal{M}^N \iff UQ \in \mathcal{M}^N ,\quad \forall Q \in \mathcal{O}^N ,$$	
where $\mathcal{O}^N$ denotes the set of orthogonal matrices of order $N$. 

Let $\mathcal{G}^N$ be the Grassmann manifold (also known as Grassmannian), which is a quotient manifold of Stiefel manifold, that is
$$\mathcal{G}^N = \mathcal{M}^N/ \sim , $$
where $\sim$ denotes the equivalence relation defined as: $\hat{U} \sim U$ if and only if there exists $Q \in \mathcal{O}^N$ such that $\hat{U}=U Q$. For any $U \in \big(L^2(\Omega)\big)^N$, we denote the equivalence class as
$$
[U]=\left\{U Q: Q \in \mathcal{O}^N\right\},
$$
then $\mathcal{G}^N$ can be formulated as
$$
\mathcal{G}^N=\left\{[U]: U \in \mathcal{M}^N\right\} .
$$

We define the $L^2$ and $H_0^1$ distances between equivalence classes as
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
		\operatorname{dist}_{L^2}\left([U_1],[U_2]\right)&= \inf\limits_{Q\in \mathcal{O}^N} \left\|U_1Q-U_2\right\|, \quad \forall U_1, U_2 \in \big(L^2(\Omega)\big)^N,
		\\\operatorname{dist}_{H_0^1}\left([U_1],[U_2]\right)&= \inf\limits_{Q\in \mathcal{O}^N} \left\|U_1Q-U_2\right\|_1,\quad \forall U_1, U_2 \in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N.
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
Given $\delta>0$, for $ U \in \left(H_0^1\left(\Omega\right)\right)^N$ we define a closed $\delta$-neighborhood by
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
		B(U,\delta) = \left\{\hat{U}\in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N:\|U-\hat{U}\|_1\leqslant \delta\right\},
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
and for $V^{(*)} \in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N\bigcap\mathcal{M}^N$, we introduce a closed $\delta$-neighborhood of $[V^{(*)}]$ by
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
		B\left([V^{(*)}],\delta\right) = \left\{[\hat{U}]\in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N: \operatorname{dist}_{H_0^1}\left( [\hat{U}],[V^{(*)}]\right)\leqslant \delta\right\}.
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}



Let $Y$ denote a real Banach space with norm $\|\cdot\|_Y$. The Sobolev space for the time-dependent functions is defined as
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
		L^{p}\left(0, T ; Y\right)&=\left\{U:[0,T)\rightarrow Y\mid 
		\|U\|_{L^{p}\left(0, T ; Y\right)}<\infty	\right\},
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
where $T\in (0,+\infty]$ and the norm $\|U\|_{L^{p}\left(0, T ; Y\right)}$ is given by
\begin{equation*}
	\|U\|_{L^{p}\left(0, T ; Y\right)}=\left\{
	\begin{aligned}
		&	\left(\int_{0}^{T} \|U(\cdot, t)\|_Y^p \mathrm{d}t \right)^{\frac{1}{p}}    &\quad 1\leqslant p<\infty,
		\\&\text{ess}\sup\limits_{0 \leqslant t < T} \|U(\cdot, t)\|_Y & p=\infty.
	\end{aligned} \right. 
\end{equation*}

For symmetric matrices $A, B \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N}$, 
$$
A \leqslant B
$$
is defined in terms of the positive semidefinite order. Specifically,
$$
A \leqslant B \quad \text { if and only if } \quad a^{\top} A a \leqslant a^{\top} B a, \quad \forall a \in \mathbb{R}^N .
$$
And for convenience, we denote $\lambda_{\max}(A)$ as the largest eigenvalue and $\lambda_{\min}(A)$ as the smallest eigenvalue of matrix $A$.

\subsubsection{Preliminary lemma}
\label{Preliminary lemma} 
Let
$$
\mathcal{H}: H_0^1(\Omega) \rightarrow H^{-1}(\Omega)
$$
be the linear operator defined in the weak sense, whose domain is given by
$\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{H}) = \left\{u \in H_0^1(\Omega): \mathcal{H}u \in L^2(\Omega)\right\}$, with the closure $\overline{\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{H})} =H_0^1(\Omega)$. We impose the following assumptions:
\begin{itemize}
	\item $\left\langle\mathcal{H}u,v\right\rangle = \left\langle\mathcal{H}v,u\right\rangle$ for all $u, v \in H_0^1(\Omega)$. 
	\item The bilinear form $(u,v) \mapsto \left\langle\mathcal{H}u,v\right\rangle$ is bounded on $H_0^1(\Omega)\times H_0^1(\Omega)$. 
	\item The bilinear form satisfies the G\aa rding inequality, namely, there holds
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\langle\mathcal{H}u,u\rangle \geqslant c_1\|\nabla u\|^2 -c_2\|u\|^2 ,\quad \forall u \in  H_0^1(\Omega),
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	where $c_1>0$ and $c_2\geqslant 0$ are constants.
\end{itemize} 
The assumptions stated above are not overly restrictive. In fact, many classical examples of linear operators satisfy these conditions on an appropriate domain, such as the second-order elliptic operator.
From the conclusions in Section 6.5 of \cite{evans2022partial}, we have the following lemma:
\begin{lemma}
	There exists an increasing sequence of real eigenvalues $\left\{\lambda_i\right\}_{i=1}^{\infty}$ of $\mathcal{H}$ and corresponding eigenfunctions $\left\{v_i\right\}_{i=1}^{\infty} \subset H_0^1(\Omega)$ such that 	$$
	\left\langle \mathcal{H}v_i ,v\right\rangle=\left(\lambda_iv_i ,v\right), \quad \forall v\in H_0^1(\Omega)
	$$ 
	holds for all $ i\in \mathbb{N}_+$. Moreover, the set $\left\{v_i\right\}_{i=1}^{\infty}$ forms an orthonormal basis of $L^2(\Omega)$, i.e., $$\left(v_i, v_j\right)=\delta_{i j},$$ and it is also a basis of $H_0^1(\Omega)$. Thus, every $u\in H_0^1(\Omega)$ can be represented as
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			u = \sum_{i=1}^{\infty}\left(u, v_i\right) v_i
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}

\end{lemma}
We extend the operator $\mathcal{H}$ to $\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$ for some $N\in \mathbb{N}_+$, also denoted as $\mathcal{H}$ for simplicity. That is, for any $U=\left(u_1, u_2, \cdots, u_N\right) \in\left(H_0^1(\Omega)\right)^N$,
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
		\mathcal{H}U =\left(\mathcal{H}u_1,\mathcal{H} u_2, \cdots, \mathcal{H}u_N\right). 
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
\begin{lemma}
	Define the vector 
	$$
	V_{i p}=(0,0, \ldots, 0, \underbrace{v_i}_{p \text {-th position}}, 0, \ldots, 0) \in\left(H_0^1(\Omega)\right)^N 
	$$
	for each $p \in\{1,2, \ldots, N\}$ and $i \geqslant 1$.
	Then, 
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\left\langle\mathcal{H}V_{i p},V\right\rangle = \left( \lambda_i V_{i p} ,V \right) ,\quad \forall V\in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	holds for all $i\in \mathbb{N}_+$ and $p=1, 2,\cdots,N$. Moreover, $\left\{V_{i p}\right\}_{i \geqslant 1,1 \leqslant p \leqslant N}$ forms an orthonormal basis of $\left(L^2(\Omega)\right)^N$, that is,
	$$
	\left(V_{i p}, V_{j q}\right)=\delta_{i j} \delta_{p q},
	$$ and it is also a basis of $\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$.
	Thus, every $U=\left(u_1, u_2, \cdots, u_N\right) \in\left(H_0^1(\Omega)\right)^N$ can be represented as
	$$
	U=\sum_{i=1}^{\infty} \sum_{p=1}^N\left(U, V_{i p}\right) V_{i p}.
	$$ 
	It is clear that $\left(U, V_{i p}\right)=\left(u_p, v_i\right)$ for $p=1, \ldots, N$.
\end{lemma}


For a fixed $m\in \mathbb{N}_+$, denote the finite-dimensional space $\mathcal{V}_m = \operatorname{span}\{v_1, \cdots, v_m\}$ with the basis $\{v_i \}_{i=1}^{m}$, which also means that 
$$\left\{V_{11}, V_{21}, \cdots, V_{n1}, \cdots\cdots, V_{1N}, V_{2N}, \cdots, V_{mN}\right\}$$
is an orthogonal basis of $\left(\mathcal{V}_m\right)^N \subset \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$. We extend the proof of Theorem 30.B in \cite{zeidler2013nonlinear} on $H_0^1(\Omega)$ to $\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$, and obtain the following lemma.

\begin{lemma}
	Define the operator $\mathcal{P}_m: \big(H^{-1}(\Omega)\big)^N \rightarrow  \left(\mathcal{V}_m\right)^N$ by
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\mathcal{P}_m \mathcal{F} = \sum\limits_{i=1}^m \sum\limits_{p=1}^N  \langle \mathcal{F} , V_{ip}\rangle V_{ip}, \quad \forall \mathcal{F} \in \big(H^{-1}(\Omega)\big)^N.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	Then $\mathcal{P}_m$ is linear and continuous and satisfies
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\langle  \mathcal{P}_m \mathcal{F}, U\rangle = \langle \mathcal{F}, \mathcal{P}_m U\rangle \text{ for all } \mathcal{F} \in \big(H^{-1}(\Omega)\big)^N \text{ and }U \in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*} Moreover, its restriction to $\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$ can be written as
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\mathcal{P}_m U = \sum\limits_{i=1}^m  \sum\limits_{p=1}^N   \left(  U, V_{ip} \right) V_{ip}, \quad \forall U \in \left(H_0^{1}\left(\Omega\right)\right)^N.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	In other words, $\mathcal{P}_m$ is an orthogonal projection from $\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$ onto $ \left(\mathcal{V}_m\right)^N$.
\end{lemma}

Lastly, we introduce a lemma, which can be obtained from Lemma 2.2 of \cite{dai2017conjugate} together with a standard analysis.
\begin{lemma}
	Let $U=\left(u_1, u_2, \cdots, u_N\right) \in\big(L^2(\Omega)\big)^N$, $V=\left(v_1, v_2, \cdots, v_N\right) \in\big(L^2(\Omega)\big)^N$ and $A\in \mathbb{R}^{N\times N}$. There hold that
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			&	\| U^\top V \| \leqslant \|U\|\|V\|,
			\\&	\| U A \| \leqslant \|U\|\|A\|,
			\\&	\| U A \| \leqslant \sigma_{\max}(U)\|A\|,
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	where $\sigma_{\max}(U) = \sqrt{\lambda_{\max}\left( U^\top U\right)}$. It implies that the norms for functions and matrices are compatible.
\end{lemma}

\subsection{Eigenvalue problem}
We now introduce the eigenvalue problem with the goal to obtain the $N$ smallest eigenvalues of the operator $\mathcal{H}$: 
\begin{equation*}
	\left\{\begin{aligned}
		&\left\langle \mathcal{H}v_j ,v\right\rangle=\left(\lambda_jv_j ,v\right), \quad \forall v\in H_0^1(\Omega); j=1,2,\cdots,N,
		\\&(v_i,v_j)=\delta_{ij}, 
	\end{aligned}\right.
\end{equation*}
which is equivalent to
\begin{equation}
	\label{linear eigenvalue problem}
	\left\{ \begin{aligned} 
		&\left\langle \mathcal{H} V^{(*)} , V\right\rangle=\left( V^{(*)}\Lambda,  V\right) ,   \quad \forall V\in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N,
		\\&(V^{(*)})^\top V^{(*)}= I_N,
	\end{aligned}\right.
\end{equation} 
where $\Lambda =\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\cdots, \lambda_N)$ with $\lambda_1\leqslant \lambda_2\leqslant \cdots\leqslant \lambda_N    \ (<\lambda_{N+1})   $ being the $N$ smallest eigenvalues of $\mathcal{H}$, and the columns of $V^{(*)}=\left(v_1, v_2, \ldots, v_N\right) \in\left(H_0^1\left(\Omega\right)\right)^N$ are the corresponding eigenvectors.

Due to the translational invariance property of the eigenvalue problem, without loss of generality, we assume that $\mathcal{H}$ has at least $N$ negative eigenvalues.

We see that the eigenvalue problem (\ref{linear eigenvalue problem}) can be also formulated as the minimization problem
\begin{equation}
	\label{minimization problem}
	\begin{aligned}
		\min\limits_{ W\in \mathcal{M}^N} E(W) := \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{tr}\left(W^\top \mathcal{H} W\right).
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Obviously, the energy functional $E$ is invariant under the orthogonal transformations, i.e. 
$$E(U)=E(U Q),\quad \forall Q \in \mathcal{O}^N, U \in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N. $$
To get rid of the nonuniqueness of the minimizer caused by the invariance, we consider (\ref{minimization problem}) on the Grassmann manifold $\mathcal{G}^N$. Actually, (\ref{minimization problem}) is equivalent to 
\begin{equation}
	\label{minimization in Grassmann}
	\min _{[W] \in \mathcal{G}^N} E (W),
\end{equation} 
and $[V^{(*)}]$ is the unique minimizer of (\ref{minimization in Grassmann}) due to the spectral gap $\lambda_{N+1}- \lambda_N > 0$ (see \cite{schneider2009direct}). 


The gradient on the Grassmann manifold $\mathcal{G}^N$ of $E(U)$ at $[U]$ is 
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
		\nabla_G E(U) = \nabla E(U) - U U^\top\nabla E(U), 
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
where 
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
		\nabla E(U) = \mathcal{H}U
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
is the gradient of $E(U)$.



\section{A quasi-Grassmannian gradient flow model}
In this work, we extend the domain of the gradient $\nabla_GE$, originally defined on $\mathcal{G}^N$, to the full space $\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$, which is still denoted as $\nabla_GE$.
Based on the above extension, we introduce the following evolution equation 
\begin{equation}
	\label{operator of gradient flow based model}
	\left\{\begin{aligned}
		& \frac{\mathrm{d}  U  }{\mathrm{d}  t} =-\nabla_G E(U) &&\text{on } \Omega \times [0,\infty),
		\\&U(0)=U_0           &&\text{on }\Omega\\
	\end{aligned}\right.
\end{equation}
to address the eigenvalue problem (\ref{linear eigenvalue problem}). We refer to this system as the quasi-Grassmannian gradient flow model. The evolution is driven by the negative gradient $-\nabla_G E(U) $. As detailed in the following section, the asymptotic behavior of the solution $[U(t)]$ indicates that, as time progresses, the solution becomes ``quasi-confined" to the Grassmann manifold $\mathcal{G}^N$; in other words, $U(t)$ exhibits asymptotic orthogonality.

In the following, we will discuss the properties of the solution of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}) in detail.
\subsection{Well-posedness}
In this subsection, we discuss the well-posedness of the weak form of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}). 
The weak form of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}) is as follows
\begin{equation}
	\label{refined weak formulation}
	\begin{aligned}
		\left\{
		\begin{aligned}
			& \frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t}  \left( U(t), V\right)+ \left\langle \nabla_GE(U(t)), V \right\rangle  = 0, \quad \forall V \in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N,
			\\&U(0)  =U_0 \in  \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N ,
		\end{aligned}
		\right.
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\frac{d}{dt}$ is to be understood as the generalized derivative (see \cite{zeidler1989nonlinear}), namely, for any $T>0$
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
		\int_{0}^{T}  \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}   \left( U(t), V\right)   \phi(t)  \mathrm{d}t 	 = 	-\int_{0}^{T}   \left( U(t), V\right)\frac{\mathrm{d}\phi(t) }{\mathrm{d}t}\mathrm{d}t ,\quad \forall \phi \in C_0^{\infty}(0,T).
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}


The assumptions from Section \ref{Preliminary lemma} lead to:
\begin{itemize}
	\item The bilinear form matrix $\left(U,V\right)\mapsto V^\top \mathcal{H}U$ is bounded on $\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N\times\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$. Thus, $\left\langle\mathcal{H}U,V\right\rangle = \operatorname{tr}\left(V^\top \mathcal{H}U\right)$ is also bounded.
	That is, there exists a constant $C_b>0$ such that 
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			|\left\langle\mathcal{H}U,V\right\rangle | \leqslant C_b \|U\|_1\|V\|_1 ,\quad \forall U,V \in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	\item There holds G\aa rding inequality 
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\left\langle\mathcal{H}U,U\right\rangle  \geqslant c_1\|\nabla U\|^2 -c_2\|U\|^2 ,\quad \forall U \in  \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	Here, $c_1>0$ and $c_2\geqslant 0$ are same as those in Section \ref{Preliminary lemma}.
\end{itemize}
These results ensure the well-posedness of the quasi-Grassmannian gradient flow model.



\subsubsection{Well-posedness of the finite-dimensional approximation model}

We first investigate the finite-dimensional approximation of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}):
finding the solution $U^{(m)}: [0,T)\rightarrow \left(\mathcal{V}_m\right)^N$ for any given $T>0$ of the following equation
\begin{equation}
	\label{Galerkin equation}
	\left\{\begin{aligned}
		&\frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t} U^{(m)}(t)+ \mathcal{P}_m\nabla_GE(U^{(m)}(t)) = 0  ,
		\\&U^{(m)}(0) = \mathcal{P}_mU_0,
	\end{aligned}\right.
\end{equation}
and establish the existence and uniqueness of solutions of (\ref{Galerkin equation}) on $[0,T)$.

Due to translational invariance of the eigenvalue problem, we assume that the initial value $U_0^\top \mathcal{H}U_0\leqslant0$. And the following lemma can be obtained.
\begin{lemma}
	\label{lemma: UHU<0}
	If $U_0 \in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$ satisfies $U_0^\top \mathcal{H}U_0\leqslant0$, then for any solution $ U^{(m)}(t)$ of (\ref{Galerkin equation}), there holds
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\left( U^{(m)}(t)\right)^\top \mathcal{H} U^{(m)}(t) \leqslant0	,\quad \forall t\geqslant0.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
\end{lemma}
\noindent\textbf{Proof.} The proof has been deferred to Appendix~\ref{proof of lemma: UHU<0}. \qed

We define a admissible set as
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
		\mathcal{L}_0 = \left\{U\in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N: U^\top \mathcal{H}U\leqslant0\right\}.
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
Lemma \ref{lemma: UHU<0} implies that, for any initial condition $U_0\in \mathcal{L}_0$, the corresponding solution $ U^{(m)}(t)$ remains in $\mathcal{L}_0$ for all $t\geqslant 0$.  This invariance, known as the preservation of the admissible set. 


\begin{proposition}
	\label{existence of weak form of Galerkin equation}
	If $U_0\in 	\mathcal{L}_0 $, then there exists a time $T_m^*>0$ such that (\ref{Galerkin equation}) has a unique solution
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			U^{(m)} \in L^{2}\left(0, T_m^* ; \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N\right)  \text{ with } \frac{\mathrm{d}  U^{(m)} }{\mathrm{d}  t} \in L^{2}\left(0, T_m^* ; \big(H^{-1}(\Omega)\big)^N\right).
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
	The weak form of (\ref{Galerkin equation}) is as follows
	\begin{equation}
		\label{weak form of Galerkin equation}
		\left\{\begin{aligned}
			&  \frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t}\left(U^{(m)}(t), V\right)+\left\langle \mathcal{P}_m\nabla_GE(U^{(m)}(t)), V\right\rangle = 0 , \quad \forall V\in \left(\mathcal{V}_m\right)^N,
			\\&U^{(m)}(0) = \mathcal{P}_mU_0,
		\end{aligned}\right.
	\end{equation}
	which is equivalent to 
	\begin{equation}
		\label{weak form of Galerkin equation in terms}
		\begin{aligned}
			\frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t} \left(U^{(m)}(t), V_{jq}\right)+\left\langle \mathcal{P}_m\nabla_GE(U^{(m)}(t)), V_{jq}\right\rangle = 0 , \quad \forall j = 1,2,\cdots,m; \  q = 1, 2,\cdots,N,
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}
	where $U^{(m)}(t) = \sum\limits_{i=1}^{m} \sum\limits_{p=1}^N \alpha_{ip}^{(m)}(t) V_{ip} $. Equation (\ref{weak form of Galerkin equation in terms}) represents a system of ordinary differential equations. For the boundedness of $\mathcal{H}$ in the finite dimensional space $\mathcal{V}_m$, $\left\langle \mathcal{P}_m\nabla_GE(U), V_{jq}\right\rangle $ is continuous and satisfies a local Lipschitz condition with respect to $U\in \mathcal{V}_m$. 
	We obtain from the Picard–Lindel\"{o}f theorem that there exists a unique solution $U^{(m)}(t)$ defined on some time interval $[0, T_m^*)$, where $T_m^*>0$ depends on the dimension $m$.


	It follows from $\left(U^{(m)}(0)\right)^\top \mathcal{H}U^{(m)}(0)\leqslant0$ that $\left(U^{(m)}(t)\right)^\top \mathcal{H}U^{(m)}(t)\leqslant0$ for all $t\geqslant0$. Therefore,
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			&	\left\langle  \nabla_G  E\left( U^{(m)}(t)\right), U^{(m)}(t)\right\rangle  
			\\ =& 	\operatorname{tr}\left(\left(U^{(m)}(t)\right)^\top \mathcal{H} U^{(m)}(t)\right) -\operatorname{tr}\left(\left(U^{(m)}(t)\right)^\top U^{(m)}(t)\left(U^{(m)}(t)\right)^\top \mathcal{H}U^{(m)}(t)\right)
			\\ \geqslant &\left\langle\mathcal{H} U^{(m)}(t), U^{(m)}(t)\right\rangle
			\geqslant c_1\|\nabla U^{(m)}(t)\|^2 -c_2\|U^{(m)}(t)\|^2.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}

	We see from (\ref{weak form of Galerkin equation}) that
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\frac{1}{2} \frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t}\left\|U^{(m)}(t)\right\|^2 =&\left\langle \frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t}U^{(m)}(t), U^{(m)}(t) \right\rangle
			= - \left\langle \mathcal{P}_m \nabla_G E\left( U^{(m)}(t) \right),U^{(m)}(t)\right\rangle 
			\\=&-  \left\langle   \nabla_G E\left(U^{(m)}(t) \right), U^{(m)}(t) \right\rangle
			\leqslant   -c_1  \left\|\nabla U^{(m)}(t)\right\|^2 +c_2\left\|U^{(m)}(t)\right\|^2, 
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	that is,
	\begin{equation}
		\label{Assumption derived inequalities}
		\begin{aligned}
			\frac{1}{2} \frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t}\left\|U^{(m)}(t)\right\|^2 +c_1 \left\|                U^{(m)}(t)\right\|_1^2  \leqslant (c_1+c_2)\left\|U^{(m)}(t)\right\|^2.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}
	Then, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\frac{1}{2} \frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t}\left\|U^{(m)}(t)\right\|^2   \leqslant (c_1+c_2)\left\|U^{(m)}(t)\right\|^2
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	and $ \left\|U^{(m)}(0)\right\|=  \left\|\mathcal{P}_mU^{(m)}(0)\right\| \leqslant  \left\| U_0\right\|$. It follows from Gr\"{o}nwall's inequality that
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\left\|U^{(m)}(t)\right\|^2\leqslant \exp\left(2\left(c_1+c_2\right)t\right) \left\| U_0\right\|^2, \quad \forall t\in [0,T_m^*),
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	which implies that $\left\|U^{(m)}(t)\right\|^2$ is bounded for all $t\in [0,T_m^*)$. In fact, this bound is independent of $m$ (i.e., it is uniform with respect to $m$).  


	Integration (\ref{Assumption derived inequalities}) yields
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\frac{1}{2}\left\|U^{(m)}(T_m^*)\right\|^2 +c_1 \int_{0}^{T_m^*}  \left\|U^{(m)}(t)\right\|_1^2 \mathrm{d} t \leqslant 	\frac{1}{2}\left\|U^{(m)}(0)\right\|^2 +\left(c_1+c_2\right) \int_{0}^{T_m^*}  \left\|U^{(m)}(t)\right\|^2 \mathrm{d} t ,
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	which implies $\left\|U^{(m)}\right\|_{L^{2}\left(0, T_m^* ; \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N\right) }^2 = \int_{0}^{T_m^*}  \left\|U^{(m)}(t)\right\|_1^2 \mathrm{d} t<+\infty$, that is $U^{(m)} \in L^{2}\left(0, T_m^* ; \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N\right)  $. Moreover, since $\left\|U^{(m)}(t)\right\|$ is uniformly bounded with respect to $m$ for all $t\in [0,T_m^*)$, there exists a uniform bound for $\left\|U^{(m)}\right\|_{L^{2}\left(0, T_m^* ; \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N\right) } $.

	Since
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\lambda_1 	\left(U^{(m)}\right)^\top U^{(m)} 	\leqslant	\left(U^{(m)}\right)^\top \mathcal{H}U^{(m)} \leqslant 0 ,
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\left\| \left(U^{(m)}\right)^\top \mathcal{H}U^{(m)}\right\|\leqslant	|\lambda_1| 	\left\| \left(U^{(m)}\right)^\top U^{(m)}\right\|	\leqslant|\lambda_1| \left\|U^{(m)}\right\|^2.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}

	For any $V \in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$, it follows that
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\left|\left\langle  \mathcal{P}_m\nabla_G E\left( U^{(m)}\right), V\right\rangle \right| 
			=& \left|\left\langle  \nabla_G E\left( U^{(m)}\right), \mathcal{P}_mV\right\rangle \right| 
			\\ \leqslant & \left|\left\langle  \mathcal{H} U^{(m)},   \mathcal{P}_mV \right\rangle\right| + \left|\operatorname{tr}\left(  \left( \mathcal{P}_mV\right)^\top U^{(m)} \left(U^{(m)}\right)^\top \mathcal{H}U^{(m)} \right) \right|
			\\ \leqslant &C_b\left\|\mathcal{P}_mV\right\|_1 \left\|U^{(m)}\right\|_1 +  \left\| \left( \mathcal{P}_mV\right)^\top U^{(m)} \right\| \left\| \left(U^{(m)}\right)^\top \mathcal{H}U^{(m)}\right\|.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	Thus, 
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\left|\left\langle  \mathcal{P}_m\nabla_G E\left( U^{(m)}\right), V\right\rangle \right| 
			\leqslant & C_b\left\|\mathcal{P}_mV\right\|_1 \left\|U^{(m)}\right\|_1 + |\lambda_1|\left\|\mathcal{P}_mV\right\| \left\|U^{(m)}\right\| \left\|U^{(m)}\right\|^2
			\\ \leqslant &\left(C_b+  |\lambda_1| \left\|U^{(m)}\right\|^2 \right)\cdot \left\|\mathcal{P}_mV\right\|_1 \left\|U^{(m)}\right\|_1.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	Due to the boundedness of $\left\|U^{(m)}(t)\right\|$, there exists a constant $\tilde{C}_b>0$, independent of $m$, such that
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\frac{\left|\left\langle  \mathcal{P}_m\nabla_G E\left( U^{(m)}(t)\right), V\right\rangle \right|}{\|V\|_1} \leqslant \frac{\tilde{C}_b\left\|\mathcal{P}_mV\right\|_1 \left\|U^{(m)}(t)\right\|_1}{\|V\|_1} \leqslant \tilde{C}_b\left\|U^{(m)}(t)\right\|_1 , \quad \forall t\in [0, T_m^*).
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	Consequently,
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\left\|\frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t} U^{(m)}\right\|_{L^{2}\left(0, T_m^* ; \left(H^{-1}(\Omega)\right)^N\right) }^2 & = \int_{0}^{T_m^*}	\left\|\frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t} U^{(m)}(t)\right\|_{-1}^2 \mathrm{d}t  
			\\&=\int_{0}^{T_m^*}\left( \sup\limits_{V \in \left(H_0^1(\Omega)\right)^N \backslash\{0\}}\frac{	\left| \left\langle  \mathcal{P}_m  \nabla_G E\left( U^{(m)}(t)\right), V\right\rangle  \right|}{\|V\|_1}  \right)^2\mathrm{d} t
			\\ &\leqslant 	\tilde{C}_b^2\int_{0}^{T_m^*}	\left\|U^{(m)}(t)\right\|_1^2 \mathrm{d}t   =\tilde{C}_b^2 \left\|U^{(m)}\right\|_{L^{2}\left(0, T_m^* ; \left(H_0^1(\Omega)\right)^N\right) }^2 ,
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	which implies that $	\left\|\frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t} U^{(m)}\right\|_{L^{2}\left(0, T_m^* ; \big(H^{-1}(\Omega)\big)^N\right) }$ is finite and uniformly bounded with respect to $m$. Thus, we come to the conclusion that $\frac{\mathrm{d}   }{\mathrm{d}  t}U^{(m)} \in L^{2}\left(0, T_m^* ; \big(H^{-1}(\Omega)\big)^N\right)$.
\end{proof}


The well-posedness of (\ref{Galerkin equation}) and regularity of $	U^{(m)}(t)$ on $[0,T_m^*)$, as established in Proposition \ref{existence of weak form of Galerkin equation}, can actually be extended to any finite time interval $t\in [0, T)$ for any given $T> 0$. This means that the unique solution $U(t)$ is globally defined with its regularity properties maintained over the entire interval [0,T).
\begin{corollary}
	\label{T be infty}
	If $U_0\in 	\mathcal{L}_0 $, then for any given $T>0$, (\ref{Galerkin equation}) has a unique solution
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			U^{(m)} \in C\left([0, T) ;\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N\right).
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
	To prove the conclusion by contradiction, suppose $T_m^*< T$ is finite and maximal in the sense that the model (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}) is no longer well-posed for $t\geqslant T_m^*$.

	By applying the triangle inequality, for any $t_1,t_2\in [0,T_m^*)$, we derive the following estimate 
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\left\| U^{(m)}(t_2) -U^{(m)} (t_1)\right\|_{-1}^2 
			\leqslant &  \left( \int_{t_1}^{t_2}  \left\|\frac{\mathrm{d}  U^{(m)}   }{\mathrm{d}  t} \right\|_{-1} \mathrm{d}  t \right)^2 
			\\	\leqslant & \left|t_2 -t_1\right|  \int_{t_1}^{t_2}  \left\|\frac{\mathrm{d}  U^{(m)}   }{\mathrm{d}  t} \right\|_{-1}^2 \mathrm{d}  t  
			\\ \leqslant& \left|t_2 -t_1\right|	\left\| \frac{\mathrm{d} U^{(m)}    }{\mathrm{d}  t}  \right\|_{L^{2}\left(0, T_m^* ;  \left(H^{-1}(\Omega)\right)^N\right)}^2.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	Due to the equivalence of norms in the finite-dimensional space $\mathcal{V}^m$, there exists a constant $C_m>0$ such that
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\left\| U^{(m)}(t_2) -U^{(m)}(t_1)\right\|_1^2 \leqslant  C_m ^2\left\| U^{(m)}(t_2) -U^{(m)}(t_1)\right\|_{-1}^2 <+\infty,
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	which implies the uniform boundedness of $U^{(m)}(t)$ in $\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$ for all $t\in [0,T_m^*)$. Hence, there exists a sequence $\{ t_n \}_{n=1}^{\infty}$ with $t_n \rightarrow T_m^* $ and $U^{(m)}_{T_m^*}\in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$ such that $U^{(m)}(t_n)\rightharpoonup U^{(m)}_{T_m^*}$ in $\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$. This implies
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\left\|U^{(m)}(t_n)\right\|_1 \leqslant &	\left\|U^{(m)}_{T_m^*}\right\|_1 +	\left\|U^{(m)}(t_n)-U^{(m)}_{T_m^*}\right\|_1 
			\\	\leqslant & \left\|U^{(m)}_{T_m^*}\right\|_1  +  \liminf\limits_{k \rightarrow \infty}	\left\|U^{(m)}(t_n)-U^{(m)}(t_k)\right\|_1  
			\\	\leqslant & 	\left\|U^{(m)}_{T_m^*}\right\|_1  +  \liminf\limits_{k \rightarrow \infty}\sqrt{ \left|t_n-t_k\right|}	C_m^2\left\| \frac{\mathrm{d} U^{(m)}   }{\mathrm{d}  t}  \right\|_{L^{2}\left(0, T_m^* ;  \left(H^{-1}(\Omega)\right)^N\right)}.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	Consequently,
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}	\left\|U^{(m)}(t_n)\right\|_1  \leqslant 	\left\|U^{(m)}_{T_m^*}\right\|_1 .
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	The weak convergence relation $U^{(m)}(t_n)\rightharpoonup U^{(m)}_{T_m^*}$ in $\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$also implies $\left\|U^{(m)}_{T_m^*}\right\|_1 \leqslant	\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}	\left\|U^{(m)}(t_n)\right\|_1 $. Therefore,
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}	\left\|U^{(m)}(t_n)\right\|_1 =\left\|U^{(m)}_{T_m^*}\right\|_1, 
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	that is,
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			U^{(m)}(t_n) \rightarrow U^{(m)}_{T_m^*} \text{ in } \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}

	By the Lions–Magenes theorem in \cite{lionsmagenes1972}, we get that the solution of (\ref{Galerkin equation}) has a unique continuous representative in the space $C\left([0, T_m^*) ;\left(L^2(\Omega)\right)^N\right)$, namely,
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			U^{(m)} \in C\left([0, T_m^*) ;\left(L^2(\Omega)\right)^N\right).
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	It follows that $U^{(m)} \in C\left([0, T_m^*) ;\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N\right)$. This implies the independence of the above limit on the choice of the sequence $\{ t_n \}_{n=1}^{\infty}$, leading to
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			U^{(m)}(t) \rightarrow U^{(m)}_{T_m^*} \text{ in } \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N \text{ for } t\rightarrow T_m^*.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	Since we assumed $T_m^*$ is finite, we could use $ U^{(m)}(T_m^*) = U^{(m)}_{T_m^*}$ as a new starting value to guarantee the existence of $U^{(m)}$ on an extended interval $[0, T_m^*+t_{\delta})$ for some $t_{\delta}>0$. This contradicts the assumed maximality of $T_m^*$, thus we come to the conclusion that
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			U^{(m)} \in C\left([0, T) ;\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N\right).
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}


\end{proof}

\subsubsection{Well-posedness of the model}
Given $T>0$, we see from Proposition 23.20 in \cite{zeidler1989nonlinear} that 
\begin{lemma}
	\label{lemma of du}
	Suppose $U \in L^2\left(0, T ; \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N\right)$. If there is a function $\mathcal{W} \in L^2\left(0, T ; \big(H^{-1}(\Omega)\big)^N \right)$ satisfying
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\int_{0}^{T}  \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}   \left( U(t), V\right)   \phi(t)  \mathrm{d}t =  \int_0^T\langle \mathcal{W}(t), V\rangle \phi(t) d t, \quad \forall V \in  \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N, \phi \in C_0^{\infty}(0, T),
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	then there exists the generalized derivative $ 	\frac{dU}{dt} =\mathcal{W}$ such that
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\frac{\mathrm{d} U   }{\mathrm{d}  t} \in L^2\left(0, T ;  \big(H^{-1}(\Omega)\big)^N\right),
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	and
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t}(U , V)=\left\langle  \frac{\mathrm{d}  U  }{\mathrm{d}  t} , V\right\rangle, \quad \forall V \in  \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
\end{lemma}

We now present the following existence and uniqueness of solutions of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}).



\begin{theorem}
	\label{Existence of weak solutions}
	If $U_0\in 	\mathcal{L}_0 $, then for any given $T>0$, (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}) has a weak solution
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			U \in L^{2}\left(0,T ; \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N\right)  \text{ with } \frac{\mathrm{d}  U  }{\mathrm{d}  t} \in L^{2}\left(0,T; \big(H^{-1}(\Omega)\big)^N\right).
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
	We choose a sequence $\left\{\mathcal{V}_m\right\}_{m=1}^{\infty}$ of finite-dimensional subspaces of $H_0^1\left(\Omega\right)$ where $\mathcal{V}_m = \operatorname{span}\left\{v_1, \cdots, v_m\right\}$ so that $\bigcup\limits_{m=1}^{\infty} \mathcal{V}_m$ is dense in  $H_0^1\left(\Omega\right)$. 

	As a direct result of Proposition \ref{existence of weak form of Galerkin equation} and Corollary \ref{T be infty},  the sequence $\left\{U^{(m)}\right\}_{m=1}^{\infty}$ is uniformly bounded in $L^{2}\left(0,T; \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N\right) $ and $\left\{\frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t} U^{(m)}\right\}_{m=1}^{\infty} $ (that is, $\left\{ \nabla_GE(U^{(m)})\right\}_{m=1}^{\infty} $) is uniformly bounded in $ L^{2}\left(0,T ; \big(H^{-1}(\Omega)\big)^N\right)$ with respect to $m$. Thus, there exists a subsequence, again denoted as  $\left\{U^{(m)}(t)\right\}_{m=1}^{\infty}$, and a function $	U \in L^{2}\left(0,T; \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N\right)$, such that
	\begin{equation}
		\label{weekly convergence of U^{(m)}}
		\begin{aligned}
			U^{(m)} \rightharpoonup U \quad  \text{ in }L^{2}\left(0,T; \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N\right)\quad  \text{ as } m\rightarrow \infty.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}
	Therefore, for all $ V \in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$ and $ \phi\in C_0^{\infty}(0,T)$,
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\int_{0}^{T} \left(U^{(m)}(t), V\right) \phi(t) \mathrm{d}t \rightarrow 	\int_{0}^{T} \left(U(t), V\right) \phi(t) \mathrm{d}t  \quad \text{as } m \rightarrow \infty.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}

	Let $V\in \left(\mathcal{V}_k\right)^N$ for fixed $k$ and let $m\geqslant k$. We have
	\begin{equation}
		\label{dUm}
		\begin{aligned}
			\frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t} \left(U^{(m)}(t), V\right)  
			= -\left\langle \mathcal{P}_m \nabla_G E\left( U^{(m)}(t) \right),V \right\rangle 
			=  - \left\langle  \nabla_G E\left( U^{(m)}(t)\right), V\right\rangle.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}
	Integration of (\ref{dUm}) yields the key relation
	\begin{equation*} 
		\begin{aligned}
			\int_{0}^{T}	\frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t} \left(U^{(m)}(t), V\right) \phi(t) \mathrm{d}t  
			=- \int_{0}^{T} \left\langle  \nabla_G E\left( U^{(m)}(t)\right), V\right\rangle \phi(t) \mathrm{d}t  
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	for all $V\in \left(\mathcal{V}_k\right)^N$ and $ \phi\in C_0^{\infty}(0,T)$. Since $\bigcup\limits_{m=1}^{\infty} \mathcal{V}_m$ is dense in  $H_0^1\left(\Omega\right)$, it is true that
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\lim\limits_{m\rightarrow\infty}\int_{0}^{T}	\frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t} \left(U^{(m)}(t), V\right) \phi(t) \mathrm{d}t  
			=- 	\lim\limits_{m\rightarrow\infty}\int_{0}^{T} \left\langle  \nabla_G E\left( U^{(m)}(t)\right), V\right\rangle \phi(t) \mathrm{d}t  
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	holds for all $V\in\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$.

	We see that $ \left\langle \nabla_GE(U), V \right\rangle =\operatorname{tr}(V^\top \mathcal{H} U) -\operatorname{tr}(V^\top U\cdot U^\top \mathcal{H}U), \forall V \in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$ is continuous with respect to $U\in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$ for fixed $V\in\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$. The uniformly boundedness of $\left\{  \nabla_G E\left( U^{(m)}\right)\right\}_{m=1}^{\infty} $ and Banach–Alaoglu theorem imply that there exists a subsequence, again denoted as  $\left\{U^{(m)}(t)\right\}_{m=1}^{\infty}$, 
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\lim\limits_{m\rightarrow\infty} \int_{0}^{T} \left\langle \nabla_G E\left( U^{(m)}(t)\right), V\right\rangle \phi(t) \mathrm{d}t  = \int_{0}^{T} \left\langle \nabla_G E\left( U(t)\right), V\right\rangle \phi(t) \mathrm{d}t 
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}

	As a result,
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			&	\int_{0}^{T}	\frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t} \left(U(t), V\right) \phi(t) \mathrm{d}t   = 	-\int_{0}^{T} \left(U(t), V\right) 	\frac{\mathrm{d}  \phi(t)  }{\mathrm{d}  t}\mathrm{d}t 
			\\ =& -\lim\limits_{m\rightarrow\infty} \int_{0}^{T} \left(U^{(m)}(t), V\right) 	\frac{\mathrm{d}  \phi(t)  }{\mathrm{d}  t}\mathrm{d}t  = \lim\limits_{m\rightarrow\infty} 	\int_{0}^{T}	\frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t} \left(U^{(m)}(t), V\right) \phi(t) \mathrm{d}t  
			\\ =& -\lim\limits_{m\rightarrow\infty} \int_{0}^{T} \left\langle \nabla_G E\left( U^{(m)}(t)\right), V\right\rangle \phi(t) \mathrm{d}t  = -\int_{0}^{T} \left\langle \nabla_G E\left( U(t)\right), V\right\rangle \phi(t) \mathrm{d}t, 
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	that is,
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\int_{0}^{T}	\frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t} \left(U(t), V\right) \phi(t) \mathrm{d}t  +\int_{0}^{T} \left\langle \nabla_G E\left( U(t)\right), V\right\rangle \phi(t) \mathrm{d}t  =0, \quad \forall V \in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N,
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	which means that the limit $U(t)$ of the sequence $\{U^{(m)}(t)\}_{m=1}^{\infty}$ is the solution of (\ref{refined weak formulation}).

	It follows from (\ref{weekly convergence of U^{(m)}}) and Proposition \ref{existence of weak form of Galerkin equation} that, for any $m\in \mathbb{N}_+$, there holds
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			&	\|U\|_{L^{2}\left(0,T ; \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N\right)}\leqslant \lim_{m \rightarrow \infty}	\left\|U^{(m)}\right\|_{L^{2}\left(0,T ; \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N\right)}<\infty,
			\\& 	\left\|\nabla_G E\left( U\right) \right\|_{L^{2}\left(0,+\infty ;  \big(H^{-1}(\Omega)\big)^N\right)}\leqslant \lim_{m \rightarrow \infty}		\left\| \nabla_G E\left( U^{(m)}\right) \right\|_{L^{2}\left(0,T ;  \big(H^{-1}(\Omega)\big)^N\right)}<\infty.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	We see from Lemma \ref{lemma of du} that there exists the generalized derivative $\frac{\mathrm{d}U    }{\mathrm{d}  t}  =- \nabla_G E\left( U\right) $, and then arrive at the conclusion that $U$ is the solution of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}), satisfying
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			U \in L^{2}\left(0,T ; \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N\right) \text{ with }\frac{\mathrm{d}U    }{\mathrm{d}  t}  \in L^{2}\left(0,T ; \big(H^{-1}(\Omega)\big)^N\right),
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	and thus (\ref{refined weak formulation}) can be rewritten as
	\begin{equation*}
		\label{rewrite refined weak formulation}
		\begin{aligned}
			\left\{
			\begin{aligned}
				&	\left\langle 	 \frac{\mathrm{d}   U }{\mathrm{d}  t}(t) , V\right\rangle + \left\langle \nabla_GE(U(t)), V \right\rangle  = 0, \quad \forall V \in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N,
				\\&U(0)  =U_0 \in  \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N.
			\end{aligned}
			\right.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}

	As for the uniqueness of the solution, it follows from
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}U}\left\langle \nabla_GE(U), V \right\rangle = \mathcal{H}V  - U^\top \mathcal{H}U   \cdot V- 2V^\top U\cdot  \mathcal{H} U\in \big(H^{-1}(\Omega)\big)^N
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	that
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\left\|	\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}U}\left\langle \nabla_GE(U), V \right\rangle \right\|_{-1} = \sup\limits_{W \in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N \backslash \{0\}} \frac{\left|\langle\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}U}\left\langle \nabla_GE(U), V \right\rangle , W\rangle \right|}{\| W \|_1}
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	is locally bounded with respect to $U\in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$. Thus, $ \left\langle \nabla_GE(U), V \right\rangle$ is locally Lipschitz. The Picard–Lindel\"{o}f theorem ensures that (\ref{refined weak formulation}) possesses a unique solution $U(t)$ on the maximal time interval $[0, T^{*})$, where $ T^{*}>0$.

	Suppose by contradiction that $T^*<T$,
	We see from Proposition \ref{existence of weak form of Galerkin equation} and Corollary \ref{T be infty} that the sequence $\left\{U^{(m)}(t)\right\}_{m=1}^{\infty}$ is uniformly bounded in $\big(L^2(\Omega)\big)^N$ for any $t\in [0,T)$. Thus, $\|U(t)\|$ remains bounded for all $t \in[0,T)$. Since the nonlinear term $\left\langle \nabla_G E(U), V \right\rangle$ is locally Lipschitz, the boundedness of $U(t)$ ensures that the solution can be extended beyond $T^*$. which contradicts the maximality of $T^*$. Therefore, the solution must exist globally, i.e., $T^* =T$.  
	And the uniqueness of the solution $U$ guarantees that all the weakly convergence subsequences of $\{U^{(m)}\}_{m=1}^{\infty}$ have the same limit $U$.

	The proof of the existence and uniqueness of the solution is thus complete.  
\end{proof}

Also by the Lions–Magenes theorem in \cite{lionsmagenes1972}, we conclude that 
\begin{corollary}
	\label{continty in L2}
	If $U_0\in 	\mathcal{L}_0 $, then for any given $T>0$, the solution of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}) has a unique continuous representative in the space $C\left([0, T) ;\left(L^2(\Omega)\right)^N\right)$, namely,
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			U \in C\left([0, T) ;\left(L^2(\Omega)\right)^N\right).
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
\end{corollary}


\subsection{Analytic representation}
In this subsection, we present an analytical representation of the solution of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}).

\begin{lemma}
	\label{negative condition of initial value}
	If $U_0\in \mathcal{L}_0$, then for any $k >0$, there holds $$U_0^\top \exp(-k\mathcal{H}t) U_0 \geqslant U_0^\top U_0$$ for all $ t\geqslant0.$
\end{lemma}
\noindent\textbf{Proof.} The proof is provided in Appendix~\ref{proof of lemma: negative condition of initial value}. \qed

With the projection operator $\mathcal{P}_m$, we can rewrite (\ref{Galerkin equation}) as
\begin{equation}
	\label{finite dimension of operator}
	\left\{\begin{aligned}
		& \frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t}   U^{(m)}= -\left( \mathcal{H}_m U^{(m)}- U^{(m)} (U^{(m)})^\top\mathcal{H}_m U^{(m)}\right),
		\\&U^{(m)}(0)=\mathcal{P}_mU_0,
	\end{aligned}\right.
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{H}_m=\mathcal{P}_m\mathcal{H}\mathcal{P}_m: \mathcal{V}_m \rightarrow \mathcal{V}_m$.  
For the finite-dimensional approximation model, we can get the following lemma.
\begin{lemma}
	\label{expression of Glaerkin solution}
	If $U_0\in \mathcal{L}_0$, then the solution of (\ref{Galerkin equation}) 
	can be expressed as
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			U^{(m)}(t) 	=& \exp (-\mathcal{H}_m t) U^{(m)}(0)\left[ I_N-\left(U^{(m)}(0)\right)^\top U^{(m)}(0)\right.
			\\&\qquad  \qquad \left. +\left(U^{(m)}(0)\right)^\top \exp (-2 \mathcal{H}_m t) U^{(m)}(0)\right]^{-1 / 2} Q^{(m)}(t) ,
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	where $Q^{(m)}(t)\in \mathcal{O}^N$ depends on $t$ only.
\end{lemma}
\noindent\textbf{Proof.} The proof is given in Appendix~\ref{proof of lemma: expression of Glaerkin solution}. \qed



Consider the following evolution problem for any constant $k>0$
\begin{equation}
	\label{linear evolution problem}
	\left\{\begin{aligned}
		& \frac{\mathrm{d}  W  }{\mathrm{d}  t} =-k\mathcal{H}W &&\text{on } \Omega \times [0,\infty),
		\\&W(0)=U_0           &&\text{on }\Omega.
	\end{aligned}\right.
\end{equation}
Combining the properties of the operators $\mathcal{H}$, we can obtain the following lemma, and its proof is provided in Appendix \ref{proof of lemma:solution of linear evolution problem}
\begin{lemma}
	\label{solution of linear evolution problem}
	For every $U_0\in \left(\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{H})\right)^N$, the linear evolution problem (\ref{linear evolution problem}) has a unique solution $W \in C^1\left([0, +\infty) ;  \left(\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{H})  \right)^N  \right) $, given explicitly by
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			W(t)= \exp\left(-k\mathcal{H}t\right)U_0,
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	where the semigroup $\left\{\exp(-k\mathcal{H}t)\right\}_{t\geqslant0}$ acts as the ``solution operator" that evolves the initial data $U_0$ forward in time.
\end{lemma}



We then present the following theorem, which provides an analytical expression for the solution of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}).
\begin{theorem}
	\label{expression of solution(linear operator)}
	If $U_0\in \mathcal{L}_0\bigcap \left(\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{H})\right)^N$, then the solution of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}) can be expressed as
	\begin{equation}
		\label{expression of solution(constant)}
		\begin{aligned}
			U(t)=\exp (-\mathcal{H} t) U_0\left[ I_N-U_0^\top U_0+U_0^T \exp (-2 \mathcal{H} t) U_0\right]^{-1 / 2} Q(t) ,
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}
	where $Q(t)\in \mathcal{O}^N$ depends on $t$ only.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
	Denote $$\check{U}(t):= \exp (-\mathcal{H} t) U_0\left[ I_N-U_0^\top U_0+U_0^T \exp (-2 \mathcal{H} t) U_0\right]^{-1 / 2}$$
	for any fixed $t\geqslant 0$.
	From Lemma \ref{negative condition of initial value}, it follows that the matrix
	$$\left[ I_N-U_0^\top U_0+U_0^T \exp (-2 \mathcal{H} t) U_0\right]^{-1 / 2}$$ is bounded for all $t\geqslant 0$.
	In combination with the conclusion of Lemma \ref{solution of linear evolution problem}, we deduce that
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\check{U} \in C\left([0,+\infty);\,\left(\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{H})\right)^N\right) \subset C\left([0, +\infty) ;\left(H_0^1(\Omega)\right)^N\right).
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	Since
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\lim\limits_{m\rightarrow\infty } \left\|U^{(m)}(0) -U_0\right\|_1 = 	\lim\limits_{m\rightarrow\infty } \left\|\mathcal{P}_m U_0 -U_0\right\|_1  = 0
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	and
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\lim\limits_{m\rightarrow\infty }\left\|\exp(-k\mathcal{H}_mt)U^{(m)}(0)  - \exp(-k\mathcal{H}t)U_0\right\|_1= 0
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	for any constant $k>0$ and $t\geqslant0$,
	it follows that
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			&\lim\limits_{m\rightarrow \infty}\operatorname{dist}_{H_0^1}\left([U^{(m)}(t)] ,\left[	\check{U} (t)\right] \right) = 0.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}

	Combined with the weakly convergence relation (\ref{weekly convergence of U^{(m)}}), it indicates that there exists a subsequence $U^{(m_k)}$ such that for any fixed $t\geqslant0$
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			U^{(m_k)}(t) \rightharpoonup U(t) \text{ in } \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N .
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	Therefore, we come to the conclusion that the solution $U(t)$ of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}) satisfies $U(t)\in \left[\check{U} (t) \right] $ and can be expressed as
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			U(t)=\exp (-\mathcal{H} t) U_0\left[ I_N-U_0^\top U_0+U_0^T \exp (-2 \mathcal{H} t) U_0\right]^{-1 / 2} Q(t),
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	where $Q(t)\in \mathcal{O}^N$ depends on $t$ only.
\end{proof}



\section{Asymptotic behavior}
This section investigates the asymptotic behavior of the system as $t\rightarrow+\infty$, focusing on asymptotic orthogonality of the solution, convergence to equilibrium, and convergence to the minimizer.

\subsection{Asymptotic orthogonality}
Theorem \ref{expression of solution(linear operator)} shows 
\begin{equation}
	U_0^\top U_0 = I_N \   \Rightarrow \  U(t)^\top U(t) = I_N, \quad \forall t\geqslant0.
\end{equation}
Actually, this is still a tendency to approach orthogonality even if the initial value is not orthogonal, i.e. the asymptotic orthogonality of the solution.

Let $\tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{0} $ denote the set of admissible initial conditions defined by
$$\tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{0} = \left\{U\in \left(\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{H})\right)^N: U^\top \mathcal{H}U < 0\right\},$$
which is a proper subset of $\mathcal{L}_{0}$.
We first turn to illustrate the asymptotic behavior of the singular values of the solution $U(t)$ to equation (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}). The proof is provided in Appendix~\ref{proof of lemma: singular values of soltion}

\begin{lemma}
	\label{singular values of soltion}
	Let $U(t)$ be the solution of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}). 
	If $U_0\in \tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{0}$, then there exist $N$ nonzero eigenvalues $\sigma_1(t) \leqslant \sigma_2(t) \leqslant \cdots \leqslant \sigma_N(t)$ of $U(t)^\top U(t)$ and there holds
	\begin{itemize}
		\item[(1)] $\lim \limits_{t \rightarrow \infty} \sigma_i(t)=1, \quad 1 \leqslant i \leqslant N$;
		\item[(2)] If $\sigma_i(0) \leqslant 1$ for $1 \leqslant i \leqslant N$, then $\sigma_i(t)$ is a nondecreasing function of $t$ and $ \sigma_i(t)\leqslant 1$ for $1 \leqslant i \leqslant N$;
		\item[(3)] If $\sigma_i(0) \geqslant 1$ for $1 \leqslant i \leqslant N$, then $\sigma_i(t)$ is a nonincreasing function of $t$ and $ \sigma_i(t)\geqslant 1$ for $1 \leqslant i \leqslant N$;
		\item[(4)] Denote $\beta = \min\{1, \sigma_1(0)\}$, then
		$$  \sigma_i(t)\geqslant \beta, \quad \forall i=1, 2,\cdots,N.$$ 
	\end{itemize}

\end{lemma}


\begin{lemma}
	\label{Bound of UHU}
	Let $U(t)$ be the solution of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}). If $U_0\in  \tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{0}$, then 
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			U(t)^\top \mathcal{H}U(t) <0, \quad \forall t\geqslant0,
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}	
	and there exists a constant $c_0>0$, which only depends on $U_0$, such that
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			U(t)^\top \mathcal{H}U(t)   \leqslant -c_0 I_N , \quad \forall t\geqslant0.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
\end{lemma}
\noindent\textbf{Proof.} The proof is given in Appendix~\ref{proof of lemma: Bound of UHU}. \qed



Lemma \ref{Bound of UHU} shows the solution $U(t)$ is in the set
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
		\left\{U\in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N : U^\top \mathcal{H}U \leqslant -c_0I_N\right\},
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
which $c_0$ only depends on the initial value $U_0$. We then define the new admissible set
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
		\mathcal{L}_{c_0} = \left\{[U]\subset\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N: U^\top \mathcal{H}U \leqslant -c_0I_N\right\},
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
and we conclude that $[U(t)]$ remains in $	\mathcal{L}_{c_0}$ for all $t\geqslant0$.



Based on the above lemmas, we can establish the following theorem.
\begin{theorem}
	\label{limiting solution in stiefel manifold} 
	Let  $U(t)$ be the solution of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}). If $U_0\in \tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{0}$, then 
	\begin{equation}
		\label{Quasi-orthogonality}
		\begin{aligned}
			\left\|I_N-U(t)^\top   U(t)\right\| \leqslant\left\|I_N-U_0^T U_0\right\| \exp \left(-2c_0 t\right), \quad 
			\forall t \geqslant 0.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}
	In particular,
	$$
	\lim _{t \rightarrow \infty} U(t) ^\top U(t)=I_N.  
	$$

\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
	It follows from Lemma \ref{Bound of UHU} that
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\lambda_{\max}\left(	U(t)^\top \mathcal{H}U(t) \right)\leqslant -c_0.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	Then, there holds
	$$
	\begin{aligned}
		\frac{\mathrm{d}    }{\mathrm{d}  t} \left\|I_N-U(t)^\top  U(t)\right\|^2=& 4 \operatorname{tr}\left[\left(I_N-U(t)^\top  U(t)\right) \cdot {U(t)^\top  \mathcal{H} U(t)}\cdot\left(I_N-U(t)^\top  U(t)\right)\right]\\
		\leqslant& -4c_0\operatorname{tr}\left[\left(I_N-U(t)^\top  U(t)\right)^2 \right] \\
		\leqslant& -4c_0\left\|I_N-U(t)^\top  U(t)\right\|^2,
	\end{aligned}
	$$
	we derive from Gr\"{o}nwall's inequality that
	\begin{equation*}
		\left\|I_N-U(t)^\top  U(t)\right\|^2\leqslant \left\|I_N-U_0^\top U_0\right\|^2  \exp(-4c_0t), \quad \forall t \geqslant 0.
	\end{equation*}
	As such, the proof is concluded.
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
	The above theorem implies that the solution of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}) tends to become orthogonal and converges exponentially as $t$ increases. 
	Moreover, once the solution $U(t)$ becomes orthogonal at a certain time point, it remains orthogonal at all subsequent time points.

	While not requiring initial orthogonality, the asymptotic orthogonality of the solution naturally emerges, even in the presence of numerical perturbations. This inherent property ensures that the solution remains orthogonal despite computational errors, thereby circumventing the failure of traditional methods caused by error accumulation.
\end{remark}


\subsection{Gradient behavior}
In this subsection, we prove that the quasi-Grassmannian gradient flow model (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}) will converge to the equilibrium state.
\begin{lemma}
	\label{convergence of Z}
	Let $U(t)$ be the solution of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}). If $U_0\in \tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{0}$, then the operator
	$$
	\mathcal{Z}(t) = U(t)U(t)^\top,
	$$
	which maps $\bigl(L^2(\Omega)\bigr)^N$ into $\bigl(\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{H})\bigr)^N$, converges exponentially as $t\to\infty$ to a limit operator $\mathcal{Z}_{\infty}$ that satisfies the following operator equations on $\bigl(\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{H})\bigr)^N$:
	\begin{equation}
		\label{converge to operator equation}
		\begin{aligned}
			&	\mathcal{H} \mathcal{Z}_{\infty}+\mathcal{Z}_{\infty}\mathcal{H} = 2\mathcal{Z}_{\infty} \mathcal{H} \mathcal{Z}_{\infty},
			\\& \mathcal{Z}_{\infty}^2  = \mathcal{Z}_{\infty}.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}  
\end{lemma}
\noindent\textbf{Proof.} The proof is deferred in Appendix~\ref{proof of lemma: converge to operator equation}. \qed


The following theorem states that the gradient vanishes as $t\rightarrow \infty$ and exponentially decreases over time, which implies that the gradient flow model (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}) exponentially converges to the equilibrium state.
\begin{theorem}
	\label{nabla_G E(U(t))_F = 0}
	Let $U(t)$ be the solution of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}). 
	If $U_0\in \tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{0}$, then there holds 
	$$\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow \infty}\|\nabla_G E(U(t))\| = 0.$$
	Moreover, there exist two constants $\gamma>0$ and $K>0$ such that
	\begin{equation}
		\label{exponential of gradient}
		\begin{aligned}
			\|\nabla_G E(U)\| \leqslant K \exp (-\gamma t).
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof} 
	Since $\nabla_G E(U(t))^\top \nabla_G E(U(t))$ has the same nonzero eigenvalues as 
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\nabla_G E(U(t))\nabla_G E(U(t))^\top & =  \left(\mathcal{I} - \mathcal{Z}(t) \right) \mathcal{H}\mathcal{Z}(t)  \mathcal{H}  \left(\mathcal{I} - \mathcal{Z}(t)  \right)
			\\& = \left(\mathcal{H}\mathcal{Z}(t)  - \mathcal{Z}(t) \mathcal{H}\mathcal{Z}(t)  \right)  \mathcal{H}  \left(\mathcal{I} - \mathcal{Z}(t)  \right),
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	we derive from (\ref{converge to operator equation}) that
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow\infty} \left(\mathcal{H}\mathcal{Z}(t)  - \mathcal{Z}(t) \mathcal{H}\mathcal{Z}(t)  \right)  \mathcal{H}  \left(\mathcal{I} - \mathcal{Z}(t)  \right)
			=&\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow\infty} \left( \mathcal{Z}(t) \mathcal{H}\mathcal{Z}(t)  -\mathcal{Z}(t) \mathcal{H}\right)  \mathcal{H}  \left(\mathcal{I} - \mathcal{Z}(t)  \right)
			\\=&-\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow\infty}\mathcal{Z}(t) \mathcal{H} \left( \mathcal{I}-\mathcal{Z}(t) \right)  \mathcal{H}  \left(\mathcal{I} - \mathcal{Z}(t)  \right),
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	which also has the same nonzero eigenvalues as 
	$$-\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow\infty}\mathcal{H} \left( \mathcal{I}-\mathcal{Z}(t) \right)  \mathcal{H}  \left(\mathcal{I} - \mathcal{Z}(t)  \right)\mathcal{Z} (t) = -\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow\infty}\mathcal{H} \left( \mathcal{I}-\mathcal{Z}(t) \right)  \mathcal{H}  \left(\mathcal{Z}(t)  - \mathcal{Z}(t) ^2 \right)= 0,$$
	i.e. the nonzero eigenvalues of $\nabla_G E(U(t))^\top \nabla_G E(U(t)) \rightarrow 0$, as $t\rightarrow \infty$.  

	Thus we have demonstrated the desired result
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow \infty} \|\nabla_G E(U(t))\|^2  =\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow \infty}  \operatorname{tr}\left(\nabla_G E(U(t))^\top \nabla_G E(U(t))\right)= 0.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}

	Similar to the derivation of the form for $Z(t)$ in the proof of Lemma \ref{converge to operator equation}, $\|\nabla_G E(U(t))\|^2$ can be also expressed as
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\sum\limits_{j=1}^{\infty}	\sum\limits_{k=1}^{\infty}	\sum\limits_{p=1}^{N}		\tilde{\alpha}_{jkp}(t),
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	where $\tilde{\alpha}_{jkp}(t)  = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{\infty} \tilde{a}_i \exp \left(\tilde{\gamma}_i t\right)}{\sum_{i=1}^{\infty} \tilde{b}_i \exp \left(\tilde{\beta}_i t\right)}$,  $\tilde{\gamma}_1\geqslant \tilde{\gamma}_2\geqslant\cdots \geqslant 0, \tilde{\beta}_1 \geqslant\tilde{\beta}_2\geqslant\cdots \geqslant 0$, and $\tilde{a}_1, \tilde{b}_1>0$ for all $j,k =1, 2,\cdots$ and $p =1, 2, \cdots, N$.


	It must be true that $\gamma_1<\beta_1$ with $\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow \infty} 	\|\nabla_G E(U)\|  = 0$. As a consequence, there exist two constants $\gamma>0$ and $K>0$ such that
	$$
	\|\nabla_G E(U)\| \leqslant K \exp (-\gamma t).
	$$
\end{proof}

Under the assumption 
\begin{equation}
	\label{expression of Hess}
	\begin{aligned}
		&	\left\langle \nabla^2 E(U) \nabla_G E(U) - \nabla_G E(U)  U^\top \nabla E(U)  , \nabla_G  E(U)\right\rangle 
		\\&\qquad\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad \qquad \qquad \geqslant \mu \| \nabla_G  E(U) \|^2 ,
		\quad \forall [U] \in B\left(\left[V^{(*)}\right], \delta_2\right)
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
for some $\mu>0$, we can provide a more precise estimate of the exponential decay rate of $\|\nabla_GE(U)\|$.
\begin{remark}
	We see from \cite{dai2017conjugate,edelman1998geometry} that the tangent space on $\mathcal{G}^N$ is
	$$
	\mathcal{T}_{[U]} \mathcal{G}^N=\left\{W \in\left(H_0^1\left(\Omega\right)\right)^N: W^{\top} U=0\right\},
	$$
	and the Hessian of $E(U)$ on $\mathcal{G}^N$ is
	$$
	\begin{aligned}
		\operatorname{Hess}_G E(U)[V, W]=\left\langle \nabla^2 E(U) W- W U^{\top} \nabla E(U), V \right\rangle, \quad \forall V, W \in \mathcal{T}_{[U]} \mathcal{G}^N . 
	\end{aligned}
	$$
	Moreover, $\operatorname{Hess}_G E(U)[W, W] \geqslant \mu \|W\|^2, \quad \forall W \in \mathcal{T}_{[U]} \mathcal{G}^N $ has been already applied in \cite{dai2017conjugate,dai2020,schneider2009direct}.

	It is clear that $\nabla_{G}E(U)^\top U  = 0, \forall U \in \mathcal{M}^N$, that is $\nabla_{G}E(U)\in \mathcal{T}_{U} \mathcal{G}^N $.
	In a sense, we can view (\ref{expression of Hess}) as an extension of the Hessian to the space $\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N$, denoted as $\operatorname{Hess}_{\tilde{G}} E(U)\left[\cdot, \cdot \right]$. Indeed, (\ref{expression of Hess}) is equivalent to 
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			&\operatorname{Hess}_{\tilde{G}} E(U)\left[\nabla_{G}  E(U) , \nabla_{G}  E(U) \right]
			\\	=&\left\langle \nabla^2 E(U) \nabla_{G} E(U) 
			- \nabla_{G} E(U)     U^\top \nabla E(U),\nabla_{G}  E(U)   \right\rangle
			\\ \geqslant &\mu \| \nabla_{G}  E(U) \|^2.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
\end{remark}
\begin{corollary}	
	\label{convergence rate}
	Let $U(t)$ be the solution of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}). Suppose $U_0 \in \tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{0} $ and $U_0^\top U_0 \leqslant I_N$. If the assumption (\ref{expression of Hess}) holds, then
	\begin{equation*}
		\small
		\begin{aligned}
			\left\|\nabla_GE(U(t))\right\|^2
			\leqslant& \left\{
			\begin{aligned}
				&	\left\|\nabla_GE(U_0)\right\|^2e^{-2\mu t}+4|\lambda_1|^3 \|I_N -U_0^\top U_0\|t e^{-2\mu t} \qquad\qquad\qquad   \ \text{if }\mu =c_0;
				\\	& 	\left\|\nabla_GE(U_0)\right\|^2e^{-2\mu t}+\frac{2|\lambda_1|^3 \|I_N -U_0^\top U_0\|}{\mu -c_0} \left( e^{\left(2\mu-2c_0\right)t}  -1\right) e^{-2\mu t} \quad \text{else}.
			\end{aligned}\right.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
	We see that
	\begin{equation}
		\label{d nabla_GE(U)_F/dt}
		\begin{aligned}
			&\frac{1}{2}\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}\|\nabla_GE(U(t))\|^2	
			\\=& \left\langle  \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} \nabla_GE(U(t)), \nabla_GE(U(t)) \right\rangle 
			\\=&\left\langle \nabla^2 E(U(t))\frac{\mathrm{d}  U(t)  }{\mathrm{d}  t}  -\frac{\mathrm{d}  U(t)  }{\mathrm{d}  t} U(t)^\top \nabla E(U(t)), \nabla_GE(U(t)) \right\rangle  
			\\ &	-\left\langle  U(t)   \cdot  (\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}U(t)^\top \nabla E(U(t)) ), \nabla_{G}  E(U(t))  \right\rangle .
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}

	For the first term on the right side of the equation (\ref{d nabla_GE(U)_F/dt}), we have 
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			&	\left\langle \nabla^2 E(U(t))\frac{\mathrm{d}  U(t)  }{\mathrm{d}  t}  -\frac{\mathrm{d}  U(t)  }{\mathrm{d}  t} U(t)^\top \nabla E(U(t)), \nabla_GE(U(t)) \right\rangle  
			\\=&-\left\langle \nabla^2 E(U(t)) \nabla_GE(U(t)), \nabla_GE(U(t)) \right\rangle  +\left\langle  \nabla_GE(U(t)) U(t)^\top \nabla E(U(t)), \nabla_GE(U(t)) \right\rangle 
			\\  = & - \operatorname{Hess}_{\tilde{G}} E(U)\left[\nabla_{G}  E(U) , \nabla_{G}  E(U) \right]
			\\ \leqslant & -\mu \| \nabla_{G}  E(U(t)) \|^2,
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	and we consider decomposing the second term as
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			&	-\left\langle  U(t)   \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}(U(t)^\top \nabla E(U(t)) ), \nabla_{G}  E(U(t))  \right\rangle 
			\\=&	-\left\langle  U(t)   \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}(U(t)^\top \nabla E(U(t)) ), \nabla_{\tilde{G}}  E(U(t))  \right\rangle 	
			\\ &-\left\langle  U(t)   \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}(U(t)^\top \nabla E(U(t)) ), \nabla E(U(t)) \left(I_N -U(t)^\top U(t)\right)  \right\rangle,
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	where $\nabla_{\tilde{G}}E(U) = \nabla E(U)U^\top U - U U^\top \nabla E(U)$ refers to the extended gradient as mentioned in \cite{dai2020}]. We see that $U^\top \nabla_{\tilde{G}}E(U) = -\nabla_{\tilde{G}}  E(U)^\top U$ is skew-symmetric and $  \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}(U^\top \nabla E(U) )$ is symmetric, then we come to the conclusion that
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			&\left\langle  U(t)   \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}(U(t)^\top \nabla E(U(t)) ), \nabla_{\tilde{G}}  E(U(t))  \right\rangle 
			\\=&\operatorname{tr}\left( \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}(U(t)^\top \nabla E(U(t)) ), U(t)^\top\nabla_{\tilde{G}}  E(U(t)) \right)       
			\\=& 0.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	And for the remaining term, we obtain that
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			&	-\left\langle  U(t)   \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}(U(t)^\top \nabla E(U(t)) ), \nabla E(U(t)) \left(I_N -U(t)^\top U(t)\right)  \right\rangle 
			\\ =& -\operatorname{tr}\left[  U(t)^\top \mathcal{H}U(t) \left(I_N -U(t)^\top U(t)\right)  \left( \left( \frac{\mathrm{d}U(t)}{\mathrm{d}t}\right)^\top \mathcal{H} U(t) +U(t)^\top \mathcal{H}\frac{\mathrm{d}U(t)}{\mathrm{d}t} \right) \right]
			\\ = &\operatorname{tr}\left[  U(t)^\top \mathcal{H}U(t) \left(I_N -U(t)^\top U(t)\right)  \left( \nabla_GE(U(t))^\top \mathcal{H} U(t) +U(t)^\top \mathcal{H}\nabla_GE(U(t))\right) \right].
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	Since $U_0^\top U_0\leqslant I_N$, Lemma \ref{singular values of soltion} indicates the solution of $U(t)$ satisfies $I_N - U(t)^\top U(t)\geqslant 0 $. 
	With the symmetry  of $\nabla_GE(U)^\top \mathcal{H} U = U^\top \mathcal{H}^2 U - \left(U^\top \mathcal{H}U\right)^2$, we estimate as follows
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			&\operatorname{tr}\left[  U^\top \mathcal{H}U \left(I_N -U^\top U\right)  \left( \nabla_GE(U)^\top \mathcal{H} U +U^\top \mathcal{H}\nabla_GE(U)\right) \right]
			\\	=& 2\operatorname{tr}\left[  U^\top \mathcal{H}U \left(I_N -U^\top U\right)  \left( U^\top \mathcal{H}^2 U - \left(U^\top \mathcal{H}U\right)^2\right) \right]
			\\ \leqslant &-2\operatorname{tr}\left[  U^\top \mathcal{H}U \left(I_N -U^\top U\right)  \left(U^\top \mathcal{H}U\right)^2 \right]
			\\	\leqslant &2|\lambda_1|^3 \|I_N -U^\top U\|.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	Consequently, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\frac{1}{2}\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}\left\|\nabla_GE(U)\right\|^2   \leqslant&
			-\mu \left\| \nabla_G E(U)\right\|^2 +2|\lambda_1|^3\|I_N-U^\top U\|
			\\ \leqslant& 	 -\mu \left\| \nabla_G E(U)\right\|^2 +2|\lambda_1|^3 \|  I_N-U_0^\top U_0\|  \exp\left(-2c_0 t \right) ,
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	and thus we conclude that 
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			&	\left\|\nabla_GE(U)\right\|^2
			\\ \leqslant& \left\{
			\begin{aligned}
				&	\left\|\nabla_GE(U_0)\right\|^2e^{-2\mu t}+4|\lambda_1|^3 \|I_N -U_0^\top U_0\|t e^{-2\mu t} \qquad\qquad\qquad  \  \ \text{if }\mu =c_0;
				\\	& 	\left\|\nabla_GE(U_0)\right\|^2e^{-2\mu t}+\frac{2|\lambda_1|^3 \|I_N -U_0^\top U_0\|}{\mu -c_0} \left( e^{\left(2\mu-2c_0\right)t}  -1\right) e^{-2\mu t} \qquad \text{else}.
			\end{aligned}\right.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	The proof is now complete. 
\end{proof}			



\subsection{Energy behavior}
In this subsection, we prove that the energy associated with the solution of the model (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}) decays exponentially over time. Moreover, we demonstrate that the solution of the model (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}) converges to the minimizer of the energy in (\ref{minimization in Grassmann}), which corresponds to the solution of the eigenvalue problem.

For simplicity, in the subsequent analysis we define
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{aligned}
		\mathcal{M}^N_{\leqslant} = \left\{ U \in \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N : 0<U^\top U \leqslant I_N \right\} .
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
Unlike $\mathcal{M}^N$, which demands the column vectors to be orthonormal, $\mathcal{M}^N_{\leqslant}$ only requires that the columns of $U$ are linearly independent and $ \lambda_i(U(t)^\top U(t))$ to be bounded within $ (0,1]$.
Correspondingly, we define the quotient set \( \mathcal{G}^N_{\leqslant} \) of \( \mathcal{M}^N_{\leqslant} \) as
$$\mathcal{G}^N_{\leqslant} = \mathcal{M}^N_{\leqslant}/ \sim ,$$
referred to as the quasi-Grassmannian.
Lemma \ref{singular values of soltion} indicates that if the initial value $U_0\in \mathcal{M}^N_{\leqslant}$, then the solution $U(t)\in  \mathcal{M}^N_{\leqslant}$ for all $t\geqslant0$, that is, $[U(t)]\in  \mathcal{G}_{\leqslant}, \ \forall t\geqslant0$.  


First, we show that the energy $E(U(t))$ decreases monotonically over time.
\begin{theorem}
	\label{energy decreases}
	Let $U(t)$ be the solution of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}). If $U_0\in \mathcal{M}_{\leqslant}^N \bigcap \tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{0}$, then
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\frac{\mathrm{d} E(U(t))}{\mathrm{d} t}\leqslant 0, \quad \forall t\geqslant 0,
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	i.e. the solution of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}) satisfies energy diminishing.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
	It is easy to see that
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			&\|\nabla_GE(U)\|^2 - \operatorname{tr}\left(\nabla E(U)^\top \nabla_GE(U)\right)
			\\=&-\operatorname{tr}\left(\nabla E(U)^\top U U^\top \nabla_GE(U)\right)
			\\=&- \operatorname{tr}\left[ (U^\top\mathcal{H} U)^2 (I_N - U^\top U) \right]
			-\operatorname{tr}\left(\nabla E(U)^\top U U^\top \nabla_{\tilde{G}}E(U)\right).
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	The facts that $U^\top \nabla_{\tilde{G}}E(U)$ is skew-symmetric and $\nabla E(U)^\top U$ is symmetric together imply that $\operatorname{tr}\left(\nabla E(U)^\top U U^\top \nabla_{\tilde{G}}E(U)\right) = 0$.

	We see from Lemma \ref{singular values of soltion} that $U(t)^\top U(t) \leqslant I_N, \forall t\geqslant 0$ with $U_0^\top U_0\leqslant I_N$. Then we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\frac{\mathrm{d} E(U(t))}{\mathrm{d} t}
			=&\frac{\delta E}{\delta U} \cdot \frac{\mathrm{d} U}{\mathrm{~d} t}=-\operatorname{tr}\left(\nabla E(U(t))^{\top} \nabla_G E\left(U(t)\right)\right)
			\\ =& - \operatorname{tr}\left[ (U^\top \mathcal{H} U)^2 (I_N - U^\top U) \right] -\|\nabla_GE(U)\|^2
			\\\leqslant&-\|\nabla_GE(U)\|^2\leqslant 0.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	This establishes the result.
\end{proof}



In the following analysis, we make a natural assumption as follows: the initial value $U_0 \in B([V^{(*)}], \delta_1)$ for some $\delta_1\in (0,1)$. 
And	we further explain the above assumption of initial condition. The proof is given in Appendix~\ref{proof of lemma: meaning of initial condition}. 
\begin{lemma}
	\label{meaning of initial condition}
	If $U \in B([V^{(*)}], \delta)$ for any given $\delta\in (0,1)$, then $U$ contains the component of all the eigenvectors corresponding to the $N$ smallest eigenvalues of $\mathcal{H}$.
\end{lemma}

It is clear that the minimizer $[V^{(*)}]\in \mathcal{G}^N$ is the unique critical point of (\ref{minimization in Grassmann}), thus $[V^{(*)}]$ is the unique point in $B\left(\left[V^{(*)}\right], \delta_1\right)\bigcap \mathcal{G}^N$. Actually, based on the definitions of the quasi-Grassmannian $\mathcal{G}^N_{\leqslant}$ and the admissible set $\mathcal{L}_{c_0}$, we can extend  this uniqueness to the region $$B\left(\left[V^{(*)}\right], \delta_1\right)\bigcap \mathcal{G}^N_{\leqslant}\bigcap \mathcal{L}_{c_0}. $$ This means $[V^{(*)}]$ is also the unique critical point within the extended set $B\left(\left[V^{(*)}\right], \delta_1\right)\bigcap \mathcal{G}^N_{\leqslant}\bigcap \mathcal{L}_{c_0}$.

For a fixed constant $\delta_2\in \left(0, \delta_1\right) $, we define
\begin{equation*}
	E_0 = \min\left\{ E(\tilde{U}): [\tilde{U}] \in \overline{ B\left(\left[V^{(*)}\right], \delta_1\right) \backslash B\left(\left[V^{(*)}\right], \delta_2\right)}\bigcap \mathcal{G}^N_{\leqslant}\bigcap\mathcal{L}_{c_0}   \right\}.
\end{equation*}
We presents the convergence theorem as follows.
\begin{theorem}
	\label{convergence}
	Let $U(t)$ be the solution of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}). If $U_0 \in \mathcal{M}^N_{\leqslant}\bigcap\tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{0} $ and
	$$E(U_0) \leqslant \frac{E_0+E(V^{(*)})}{2} \stackrel{\Delta }{=} E_1,$$
	then
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			&\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow \infty}E(U(t)) = E(V^{(*)}),
			\\& \lim\limits_{t\rightarrow \infty}\operatorname{dist}_{H_0^1}\left([U(t)], [V^{(*)}]\right) =0.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
	For the uniqueness of critical point $V^{(*)}$ in $B\left(\left[V^{(*)}\right], \delta_1\right)\bigcap \mathcal{G}^N_{\leqslant}  \bigcap  \mathcal{L}_{c_0}$, we have $	E(V^{(*)}) < E_0$.
	We see from Lemma \ref{singular values of soltion} that $ U(t) \in \mathcal{M}^N_{\leqslant}$ with $U_0\in \mathcal{M}^N_{\leqslant} $, and from Lemma \ref{Bound of UHU} that $U(t)^\top \mathcal{H}U(t)\leqslant -c_0 I_N$ for all $t\geqslant0$. Combining with the result of Theorem \ref{energy decreases}, we obtain 
	$$
	[U(t)] \in B\left(\left[V^{(*)}\right], \delta_2\right)\bigcap \mathcal{G}^N_{\leqslant}  \bigcap  \mathcal{L}_{c_0}\bigcap \mathcal{L}_{E_1},\quad \forall t\geqslant0 ,
	$$
	where $\mathcal{L}_{E_1}=\left\{[U]\subset \big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N: E(U) \leqslant E_1\right\}$ is the level set. Since set
	$$
	\mathcal{S}=\left\{ U \in\big(H_0^1(\Omega)\big)^N:[U]\in B\left(\left[V^{(*)}\right], \delta_2\right)\bigcap \mathcal{G}^N_{\leqslant}  \bigcap  \mathcal{L}_{c_0}\bigcap \mathcal{L}_{E_1}\right\}
	$$
	is compact, there exist a subsequence $\left\{U\left(\tau_{k}\right)\right\}_{k=0}^{\infty}$ and $\hat{U} \in \mathcal{S}$ that $U\left(\tau_{k}\right)$ converge to $\hat{U}$, and then $ \|\nabla_GE(\hat{U})\| = \lim\limits_{k\rightarrow\infty} \|\nabla_GE(U(\tau_{k}))\|  = 0$. 
	Thus we get $\nabla_G E(\hat{U})=0$. 


	By the uniqueness of critical point $\left[V^{(*)}\right]$ in $B\left(\left[V^{(*)}\right], \delta_1\right) \bigcap \mathcal{G}^N_{\leqslant}  \bigcap  \mathcal{L}_{c_0} $ and continuity of $E$, we get $[\hat{U}]=\left[V^{(*)}\right]$ and
	$$
	\lim _{t \rightarrow \infty} E(U(t))= \lim _{k \rightarrow \infty} E\left(U\left(\tau_{k}\right)\right)= E\left(V^{(*)}\right) .
	$$

	We claim that $\lim\limits_{t \rightarrow \infty} \operatorname{dist}_{H_0^1}\left([U(t)],\left[V^{(*)}\right]\right)=0$. Otherwise, there exists a subsequence $\left\{U\left(\tau_p\right)\right\}_{p=0}^{\infty}$ that for some fixed $\hat{\delta}>0$, $\operatorname{dist}_{H_0^1}\left([U\left(\tau_p\right)],\left[V^{(*)}\right]\right) > \hat{\delta}$. Since $\mathcal{S}$ is compact, there exist a subsequence $\left\{U\left(\tau_{p_q}\right)\right\}_{q=0}^{\infty}$ and $\bar{U} \in \mathcal{S}$ that $U\left(\tau_{p_q}\right)$ converge to $\bar{U}$. Therefore,
	$$
	E(\bar{U})=\lim _{q \rightarrow \infty} E\left(U\left(\tau_{p_q}\right)\right)=E\left(\left[V^{(*)}\right]\right),
	$$
	and $[\bar{U}]=\left[V^{(*)}\right]$, which contradicts the assumption $\operatorname{dist}_{H_0^1}\left([U\left(\tau_p\right)],\left[V^{(*)}\right]\right) > \hat{\delta}$. Hence, the theorem is proved.
\end{proof}

The theorem establishes the existence of $\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow \infty}U(t)$, and shows that $\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow \infty}U(t) \in [V^{(*)}]$.  
In other words, the equilibrium point of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}) is a minimizer of $E(U)$.
Furthermore, we can show that the solution of the model converges exponentially to the minimizer as time progresses.
\begin{corollary}
	With the same hypothesis as in Theorem \ref{convergence}, 
	there hold
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			& \operatorname{dist}_{L^2}\left([U(t)], [V^{(*)}]\right) \leqslant \frac{K}{\gamma} \exp (-\gamma t),
			\\&	E(U(t)) - E(V^* ) \leqslant \frac{|\lambda_1|^2}{2c_0} \left\| I_N-U_0^\top U_0 \right\|\exp(-2c_0t) +\frac{K^2}{\gamma^2} \exp (-2\gamma t).
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	where $\gamma>0$ and $K>0$ are constants mentioned in Theorem \ref{nabla_G E(U(t))_F = 0}.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
	With the inequality (\ref{exponential of gradient}), it follows that for all $s_2>s_1 \geqslant 0$,
	$$
	\begin{aligned}
		\left\|U\left(s_2\right)-U\left(s_1\right)\right\|
		\leqslant &\int_{s_1}^{s_2} 	\|\nabla_G E(U)\| d t
		\\ \leqslant &\int_{s_1}^{s_2} K \exp (-\gamma t) d t 
		=\frac{K}{\gamma}\left[\exp \left(-\gamma s_1\right)-\exp \left(-\gamma s_2\right)\right].
	\end{aligned}
	$$

	If $t$ is sufficiently large and $s_2, s_1 \geqslant t$, then $\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow \infty}\left\|U\left(s_2\right)-U\left(s_1\right)\right\| =0$. It is clear to see $\lim\limits _{t \rightarrow \infty} U(t)$ exists and is an equilibrium point of (\ref{operator of gradient flow based model}). 

	Let $s_2$ go to infinity and $s_1=t$, then we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\left\|U(t)-\lim\limits _{t \rightarrow \infty} U(t)\right\| \leqslant \frac{K}{\gamma} \exp (-\gamma t).
	\end{equation*}
	Thus, 
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\operatorname{dist}_{L^2}\left([U(t)], [V^{(*)}]\right) =& \inf\limits_{Q\in \mathcal{O}^N} \left\|U(t)Q-V^{(*)}\right\|
			\\ \leqslant&\left\|U(t)-\lim\limits _{t \rightarrow \infty} U(t)\right\| \leqslant \frac{K}{\gamma} \exp (-\gamma t).
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}

	Since 
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\operatorname{tr}\left[ (U^\top \mathcal{H} U)^2 (  I_N - U^\top U) \right]
			\leqslant &\sigma_{\max}(U^\top \mathcal{H} U)^2 \|   I_N- U^\top U\|
			\\\leqslant& |\lambda_1|^2 \|   I_N- U^\top U\|,
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	there holds 
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\frac{\mathrm{d} E(U(t))}{\mathrm{d} t}
			=& - \operatorname{tr}\left[ (U^\top \mathcal{H} U)^2 (  I_N - U^\top U) \right] -\|\nabla_GE(U)\|^2
			\\\geqslant& -|\lambda_1|^2\|   I_N- U^\top U\|  -\|\nabla_GE(U)\|^2.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	It leads to
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			E(U(t)) - \lim\limits_{t\rightarrow\infty}E(U ) \leqslant \int_{t}^{\infty} |\lambda_1|^2 \|   I_N- U^\top U\|+ \|\nabla_GE(U(t))\|^2   \mathrm{d} t ,
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	that is,
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			E(U(t)) - E(V^* ) \leqslant \frac{|\lambda_1|^2}{2c_0} \left\| I_N-U_0^\top U_0 \right\|\exp(-2c_0t) +\frac{K^2}{\gamma^2} \exp (-2\gamma t).
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	which means the energy has exponential decay as $t$ increases. This concludes the proof.

\end{proof}
Building on Corollary \ref{convergence rate}, we can derive a more precise estimate for the exponential decay rate of \(\operatorname{dist}_{L^2}([U(t)], [V^{(*)}])\) and $E(U(t))$ discussed above. To avoid redundancy, we omit unnecessary details here.



\section{Concluding remarks}
In this paper, we have proposed and analyzed a quasi-Grassmannian gradient flow model for eigenvalue problems of linear operators. We have shown that this model inherently ensures asymptotic orthogonality, allowing solutions to naturally evolve toward orthogonality over time.

To solve the quasi-Grassmannian gradient flow model, in our coming work \cite{Wang2025}], we propose and analyze a quasi-orthogonality algorithm, which preserves the model's structure.
We also intend to extend the framework to nonlinear eigenvalue problems, and validate the approach through numerical experiments. These efforts aim to further enhance the model’s practical applicability and broaden its use in solving complex operator-related problems.

