\section{Introduction}
In the fast-paced landscape of modern data systems, particularly within SQL development and testing, the demand for high-quality mock data that accurately mirrors real-world scenarios is more critical than ever \citep{whiting2008creating,gartner_marketguide,bhangdiya2015xda,chandra2015data,somwase2024data,arcuri2019restful}. This need is amplified in industrial settings where data is often confidential and/or sensitive. In such environments, direct access to production data is severely restricted, and traditional approaches like production data sampling become time-consuming, infeasible, or violate data privacy requirements. Historically, mock data generation has relied on manual creation, production data sampling or mathematical models \citep{somwase2024data,gartner_marketguide,9155857, soltana2017synthetic}. 

One key challenge for effective test data generation lies in achieving realism and diversity within industrial contexts. Mock data must be sufficiently realistic and diverse to effectively simulate the wide range of scenarios encountered in real-world production systems, including both common and rare edge cases. Existing data generation methods often fall short in capturing this diversity, while rule-based systems tend to be inflexible and limited in their ability to generate varied data that reflects the complexities of industrial data distributions \citep{mannino2019real,hackolade_generatemockdata}. 
Another challenge is supporting test cases with complex data structure, consistency, and data integrity requirements \citep{somwase2024data,chandra2015data,he2024verieql,bhangdiya2015xda}. For industrial applications heavily reliant on structured data  types such as JSON, XML, protocol buffers (a.k.a. proto~\citep{protobuf}), SQL and Open API schemas, it is paramount that the generated mock data strictly adheres to the specified structure and maintains data integrity and consistency \citep{endres2022synthetic,hernandez2022synthetic}. 
Specifically, our SQL code generation services utlizie GoogleSQL~\citep{googlesql} that supports complex nested data types, like array, struct and proto.
Manual processes are highly susceptible to errors that can compromise the validity of testing and development. Furthermore, scalability and efficiency of generation are critical for enterprise needs, especially when a large amount of test data is needed \citep{gretel_definitiveguide,mockaroo,hackolade_generatemockdata}. As enterprise software systems grow in scale and complexity, the need for large volumes of mock data increases exponentially. Manual and heuristic data generation approaches become impractical and unsustainable in such environments, hindering development velocity and quality assurance efforts.

The emergence of Large Language Models (LLMs) presents a paradigm shift, offering new avenues for automating the generation of mock data, especially for structured formats such as schemas and protocol buffers. 
The use of LLMs for synthetic data generation is becoming popular in various domains \citep{singh2024exploring,gretel_definitiveguide,lu2023machine,karmarkar2024navigating,liu2023fill,zhu2025bare,li2023synthetic,patel2024datadreamer}, the generation of structured data introduces unique challenges that are particularly relevant to industrial applications. 
LLMs, when appropriately trained, can provide realistic and privacy-preserving data synthesis. They can generate mock data that more closely approximates real-world textual data distributions and patterns, capturing subtle nuances and semantic relationships between data attributes that are difficult to replicate with traditional methods \citep{xu2024llms,sui2024table}.
This is crucial for realistic testing while also avoiding the privacy risks associated with using actual production data (regulatory security and privacy policy restricts access to user data). Additionally, LLMs can understand SQL structures and facilitate structurally compliant and schema-aware data generation \citep{singh2024exploring,borisov2022language,sui2024table,nguyen2024generating}. Given a schema or proto definition, LLMs can be guided to generate mock data that adheres to the specified structure and constraints. However, tabular data generation via language models remains an active and evolving field, facing challenges such as accurately capturing complex inter-column (pairwise) relationships, mitigating the risk of ``hallucinations'' or data inconsistencies, and effectively handling rare values and outliers (also see Table~\ref{tab:customer_table_data_generation} for vanilla LLM evaluation results)~\citep{sui2024table,nguyen2024generating}. 

Deep generative models \citep{zhang2023mixed,lee2023codi,liu2023goggle} have shown promise in tabular data generation, particularly for categorical, numerical, and text attributes. However, they are fundamentally limited by their focus on flat schemas, neglecting nested structures, and their reliance on training data, rendering them infeasible in scenarios where production data is restricted. In contrast, our LLM-based approach, leveraging \textit{gemini}~\citep{gemini} out-of-the-box, directly addresses these limitations by generating structurally compliant data for arbitrarily complex, nested schemas without requiring fine-tuning.

However, current LLM-based data generation approaches often fall short in several critical areas. A significant limitation lies in their ability to effectively handle more complex data structures commonly found in our workloads. Specifically, LLMs struggle to accurately generate data that conforms to large (many columns or fields) and complex nested structures. While LLMs can generate data that adheres to basic schemas, accurately generating data that conforms to deeply nested structures or schemas with complex dependencies remains a challenge. This limitation is particularly problematic in testing real-world SQL generation services that rely on intricate data models, where the nuances of nested relationships over very wide tables are critical for realistic testing and validation.

Furthermore, ensuring the semantic validity of the generated data is another crucial challenge that must be addressed to generate truly realistic test data. This is even more critical for testing generated SQL queries (e.g., in NL2SQL systems) on user data. While LLMs might generate data that is syntactically correct and structurally valid, it often lacks the necessary semantic coherence across columns and tables. Most test data generation approaches focus on approximating  per-column or pairwise column distributions from the production data \citep{zhang2023mixed,lee2023codi,liu2023fill,mannino2019real,whiting2008creating,bhangdiya2015xda,chandra2015data,somwase2024data} leading to illogical combinations of column values, semantically incoherent join tables -- violating real-world business rules. Overcoming these limitations, including both structural and semantic challenges, is crucial for fully realizing the potential of LLMs in generating comprehensive and accurate mock data for complex workloads.

In this paper, we address the critical need for generating syntactically correct and semantically ``meaningful'' mock data for structurally well-defined data formats, focusing on the specific requirements of industrial use cases. We define ``meaningful'' data as data that effectively supports a wide range of complex test cases for Google SQL code generation services, like NL2SQL and SQL code assistance systems. Robust evaluation of such generation systems requires realistic and complex test data, which are often inaccessible due to privacy or lack of complex data generation capabilities. Services, like NL2SQL, support arbitrarily complex use cases, generating intricate SQL queries involving joins, aggregations, and nested subqueries—scenarios prevalent in real-world applications. 
The meaningful test data generation should also include the ability to generate data that respects various constraints, such as generating data within a specific date range when the SQL query under test includes a corresponding filter (e.g., \textit{WHERE year = 2022}).
Moreover, we emphasize the importance of maintaining column correlations and data dependencies within a schema, which are often crucial for simulating realistic data behavior. 
While the ability to generate complex, multi-faceted datasets is essential for thoroughly evaluating these queries, semantic integrity of test data is also critical, in that incorrect or illogical data can lead to misleading test results, failing to expose critical flaws in the generated SQL queries. 
Without such robust testing, the reliability and trustworthiness of SQL code generation services are severely compromised.

Our key contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{LLM-based High-Fidelity and Efficient Test Data Generation:} We present a novel approach leveraging LLMs, enhanced with pre- and post-processing, to generate high-fidelity test data compliant with complex and large schemas, including nested column types, addressing the limitations of existing data generation methods in production. Our generation method is significantly more efficient than manual generation used in production where production data access is restricted.
    \item \textbf{Enhanced Test Coverage for Complex SQL Queries:} We demonstrate that our generated test data significantly improves test coverage of Google SQL code generation services, where many test cases are disabled due to limited access to production data and the difficulties of manual data generation for large, complex schemas.
    \item \textbf{Semantic Coherence With Test SQL Queries:} We emphasize and achieve the generation of semantically meaningful and validated test data. We provide a comparative analysis against production data sampling, showcasing the ability of our generated test data to provide meaningful tests that cover edge cases and semantically relevant predicate columns.
\end{itemize}



The remainder of this paper is structured as follows. Section 2 discusses real-world SQL code generation services that serve as use cases for our work. Section 3 presents our proposed method for generating high-quality mock data. Section 4 describes our experimental setup and presents the evaluation results. Section 5 discusses related work in the field of data generation. Section 6 provides a discussion of our findings and their implications. Finally, Section 7 concludes the paper.

