\noindent\textbf{Handling Subqueries:}
The pre-processor module treats each subquery as a separate query and associates each of the signals for data generation mentioned above separately for each subquery and the main query, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:preprocessor_extraction}.
\\\\
\noindent\textbf{Handling Joins:}
Joins information is extracted from the SQL query separately to show table and column association for the join. The example SQL above the join information will be extracted as in Figure~\ref{fig:preprocessor_extraction}. Our method generates data (rows) per schema given a SQL query, the extracted join information will then be used in the post-processing to match the values in the table and columns present in the Join Constraints data structure. It is not possible to capture these nuances in a naive approach using Vanilla LLM.


\subsection{Context extractor}
This module generates context relevant to the schema. We use a separate system to collect human written comments for a schema and proto from comments in data schema, source code and documentations. These comments are available at the schema/proto level and also individual column level. We use LLM to make these comments consistent and fill in missing columns. 
For protos, by themselves or as a column in a schema, we generate the proto description with comments for additional context for data generation. 
This component will also be able to extract column correlations from the SQL query body and annotated schema that would be useful in generating meaningful data. 
\\\\
\noindent\textbf{Minimizing edge cases with constraints:}
We pass constraints extracted directly from the test SQL query, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:preprocessor_extraction}, so that the model can explore and generate different values a relevant column in the predicates should take for the test (for instance, the LLM understands that it should generate for \textit{currency} $\in \{``USD", ``GBP", ``EUR"\}$ from the query in Figure~\ref{fig:simple_selection_query}). The same set of constraints are later used for the semantic quality verification of the generated data as well. In Section~\ref{sec:business_analytics_discussion} we discuss another example where our LLM-based generation successfully generate test data around $date\_trunc(logdate, quarter)$ constraint, leaving no quarter without test data.

