When we make the LLM call for generation, we make the request for each nested field separately on a separate thread (see Section~\ref{sec:cost_analysis} for more details). For the primitive/scalar fields, we group and pass one generation call to handle 30 fields at a time. This sometimes leads to unexpected hallucinations, especially when the schema is small with a fewer number of primitive fields (see Section~\ref{sec:error_analysis} for an example). To guard against such hallucination, we also keep track of the requested column generations so that any hallucinated columns can be detected and removed.


\subsection{Post-processor}
This module can add the data constraints and variations by copying the basic data generated by the LLM or existing data written by the user. Since the constraints and variations will affect only a smaller number of columns in a proto, we could easily just modify those components. 

This module will not make any LLM calls and will update the relevant fields identified in the preprocessor module. This will also provide us a chance to correct any problems with the generated data. This will also reduce the total time taken for data generation as the LLM call latency scales linearly with the number of data points to be generated. This will also make the changes more deterministic in nature.

\subsection{Data validator}\label{sec:data_validator}

We would evaluate the generated data using a proto parser~\citep{protobuf}. This has an in-built check for the datatype and any other constraint on the proto fields. In case of any failure, we would be retrying the data generation, at most 2 retires, along with an error message. The integrated testing will be done by the framework.
