Table~\ref{tab:cost_analysis} presents a cost analysis of the proposed test data generation measured using the "gemini-2.0-flash" model. It quantifies the average and standard deviation of the accumulated number of input/output tokens, as well as the generation time per test query. The high variability in token counts and generation time suggests a wide range of query and schema complexities. 
We report the number of input and output tokens instead of the monetary cost directly, as it depends on the pricing model~\citep{gemini_pricing} (subject to change over time). The total cost of generation is proportional to the number of input/output tokens.
Overall, our method can generate more comprehensive and complex test data (rows) much more efficiently than the manual generation -- compared to the human labor cost, this is much cheaper and scalable, too.

\subsection{Generating for test SQL query semantics}
\label{sec:business_analytics_discussion}
Test data's semantic coherence to its test target SQL query is critical in evaluating SQL code generation services, like NL2SQL. One of the key challenges in testing NL2SQL is evaluating semantic correctness of the predicted query~\citep{floratou2024nl2sql,chung2025long}. While the NL2SQL prediction quality evaluation is beyond the scope of this study, a meaningful test data generation should provide a set of test data instances that are semantically relevant to the target SQL query. One way to address this is to cover all the edge cases for the relevant columns used in the SQL query, as we have focused in Section~\ref{sec:diversity_eval}. 

% What were conversion rates on Text Ads within each quarter of 2022?
\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \node[rectangle, fill=mycolor] (rect) {
    \begin{tabular}{@{}p{\linewidth}@{}} 
      %\toprule
      \begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
  \begin{lstlisting}
SELECT date_trunc(logdate, quarter), sum(conversions) / sum(clicks) 
FROM fake_table_1
WHERE result_type = 'TEXT_AD' AND logdate BETWEEN '2022-01-01' AND '2022-12-31' 
GROUP BY date_trunc(logdate, quarter);
  \end{lstlisting}
\end{minipage} \\
    %\bottomrule
    \end{tabular}
  };
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Meaningful test data generation for this business analytics query requires generating data instances for each quarter in year 2022.}
\label{fig:quarterly_report_query}
\end{figure}

To illustrate the problem, consider a business analytic query in Figure~\ref{fig:quarterly_report_query}. A meaningful test data for this query should ensure that enough example \textit{conversions} instances are present across all the quarters in the requested date range. 
Our LLM-based generator can recognize such semantic importance of \textit{date\_trunc(logdate, quater)} and generate data for all quarters in the range. Moreover, if one has a domain knowledge about the data, such as ``conversions were better in Q4 than Q1," then it can be passed to the generator as \textit{user\_criteria} as part of input, which can guide the generation to favor Q4 instances.
Such understanding and reasoning capabilities of LLMs can scale semantic-aware test data generation.


\subsection{Generation error analysis}
We observe some common error patterns in our data generation process:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Output data formatting error. We request output in JSON format, but the actual responses had incorrect JSON formatting failing the JSON parser. This can be fixed with retries.
    \item Missing fields from large and complex schema, which requires some post-processing steps to identify and retry.
    \item Generating fewer than requested amount of data/rows, which can be fixed with retries to generate data incrementally.
    \item For some schemas with a very small number of primitive columns, the LLM hallucinated and introduced new columns. Care is needed to keep track of requested columns/fields for each (nested) schema.
    \item Missing some edge cases relevant to given SQL queries/functions.
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \node[rectangle, fill=mycolor] (rect) {
    \begin{tabular}{@{}p{\linewidth}@{}} 
      %\toprule
      \begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
  \begin{lstlisting}
json[{    
    "fake_service_id": 1234567890, 
    "user_dims": {  
        "dimension1": "value1",
        "dimension2": 10
        },
    "id": 12345,
    "customer_dims": {
       "dimension3": "value3",
       "dimension4": 20
       },
   "customer_details": {
       "detail1": "detailValue1",
       "detail2": 30
       },
   "fake_service_dump_freshness_usec": 1678886400000000,
   "user_details": {
      "detail3": "detailValue3",
      "detail4": 40
      }
    },
    ...]
  \end{lstlisting}
\end{minipage} \\
    %\bottomrule
    \end{tabular}
  };
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{LLM hallucinates to introduce new columns. Only ``fake\_service\_id", ``fake\_service\_dump\_freshness\_usec", and (user) ``id" are requested, but new fields are being introduced in the data.} 
\label{fig:llm_hallucination}
\end{figure}

We have implemented some number of safeguards against LLM hallucination. Most notably, we have observed in some simple schema cases, the LLM introduced new fields -- even nested ones. Figure~\ref{fig:llm_hallucination} illustrates one such example where many more new fields are introduced next to just 3 required fields; the LLM even introduces nested fields.

The last item is very difficult to fully address. We use LLM-as-a-judge to validate the semantic coherence of generated data, which runs the risk of false positives, but we do not have an oracle (or access to the real production tables) to ensure all relevant values are covered and generated. If we can provide some domain knowledge, as discussed in Section~\ref{sec:business_analytics_discussion}, that could help inform the LLM to generate for particular cases. 
This is an important problem to further improve the fidelity of mock data generation in the production settings. There are also techniques to statistically impute missing values and estimate potential impact of missing values on query results   \cite{biessmann2019datawig,chung2018estimating}, which would be interesting to consider in the test data generation settings.




\section{conclusion and future work}
In this paper, we addressed the critical challenge of generating high-fidelity and complex test data for Google SQL code generation services, particularly in environments where production data access is restricted. We highlighted the limitations of traditional data generation methods, focusing on their inability to handle large, deeply nested schemas and maintain semantic integrity to the target test cases. By leveraging the latest \textit{gemini-2.0-flash} model, coupled with strategic pre- and post-processing, we demonstrated the capability to generate comprehensive test data instances compliant to large complex schemas that effectively supports complex SQL testing, including queries with joins, aggregations, and nested subqueries. Our experiments demonstrate the practical effectiveness of our approach, delivering a reliable method for generating realistic test data essential in industrial environments where access to production datasets is restricted. By deploying our test data generator, we've enabled more thorough SQL code testing, resulting in significantly improved test coverage.

As part of our future work, we plan to extend the capabilities of our framework to support other nested data types, such as XML, JSON, and Open API schemas. This will enable us to address a broader range of real-world applications beyond SQL code generation services, including RESTful API testing. Furthermore, we aim to explore techniques for enhancing the semantic coherence of generated data, ensuring that it not only adheres to structural constraints but also accurately reflects real-world business context and semantics. By expanding the scope and capabilities of our approach, we seek to provide a comprehensive solution for generating high-quality test data that addresses the evolving needs of industrial and large-scale data service development and testing.

