\section{The complexity of temporal $CSP$ languages} \label{secpoly}


\subsection{pp formulas, interpretations and polymorphisms}

We introduce the basic notions from the algebraic theory for $CSP$, see, e.g., Barto, Krokhin and Willard \cite{BKW}, and Meyer-Opr\v{s}al \cite{MO} and Kun-Szegedy \cite{KSz} on related approaches.

In this paper we only use a few results from this theory, particularly, the Bodirsky-K\'ara dichotomy theorem \cite{BK}  extending earlier works of Cameron \cite{C} and Bodirsky-Ne\v{s}et\v{r}il \cite{BN}.

For a relational signature $\tau$, a first-order $\tau$-formula is called {\it primitive positive} (or pp for short) if it is of the form

$$\exists x_1, \dots ,x_n (\psi_1 \wedge \dots \wedge \psi_m),$$

where the $\psi_i$ are atomic, i.e., of the form $y_1=y_2$ or $R(y_1, \dots , y_k)$ for a $k$-ary relational symbol $R \in \tau$ and not necessarily distinct variables $y_i$.

Let ${\bf T}$ be a structure with a finite relational signature $\tau$. Then we can view
$CSP({\bf T})$ as a decision problem for a given primitive positive (pp) $\tau$-sentence $\phi$ whether $\phi$ is true in ${\bf T}$.

%For every structure ${\bf S}$ there is a formula over ${\bf T}$ describing that ${\bf S}$ is homomorphic to ${\bf T}$ with variables corresponding to the elements of $S$ and atomic relational formulas corresponding to relational tuples in ${\bf S}$. On the other hand, given such a pp formula we can correspond to it the structure on its set variables with relational tuples corresponding to atomic relations. This one-to-one correspondence extends to a one-to-one correspondence between evaluations where the formula holds and ${\bf S} \to {\bf T}$ homomorphisms.

A pp-formula with parameters can contain, in addition, elements of the domain of the $CSP$.
This means that we add certain elements of the domain as a constant relation also enlarging the relational type $\tau$: in the language of homomorphisms, the image of certain elements under the homomorphism is prescribed. %The addition of a pp definable relation does not increase the complexity of the $CSP$.

%\begin{lemma}
%Let ${\bf T}$ be a relational structure of finite type $\tau$ and $R$ a relation with primitive positive definition in ${\bf T}$. Then $CSP({\bf T})=CSP(T,\{ Q({\bf T}): Q \in \tau \})$ and $CSP(T, \{Q({\bf T}): Q \in \tau\} \cup \{ R \})$ are polynomially equivalent.
%\end{lemma}

Given a set $S$ a $k$-ary {\it operation} $f$ is a mapping $f:S^k \to S$. This induces a $k$-ary operation on a power $S^n$ defined by

\noindent
$\big((x_{1,1} \dots ,x_{1,n}),(x_{2,1},\dots ,x_{2,n}), \dots ,(x_{k,1},\dots ,x_{k,n})\big) \mapsto$ \\ $\big( f(x_{1,1}, \dots ,x_{k,1}),f(x_{1,2}, \dots ,x_{k,2}), \dots ,f(x_{1,n}, \dots ,x_{k,n}) \big).$

An {\it operation on $S$ preserves an $r$-ary relation} $R \subseteq S^r$ if the induced operation on $S^r$ preserves $R$. A {\it polymorphism} of a relational structure ${\bf S}$ is an operation that preserves every relation of ${\bf S}$. %We write $Pol({\bf T})$ for the set of polymorphisms of the structure ${\bf S}$.

%Note that if a set of relations is preserved by an operation then any relation pp definable from them is also preserved by this operation. Remarkably, the converse also holds, at least over finite domains: a relation is pp-definable in ${\bf T}$ iff it is preserved by $Pol({\bf T})$. {\color{blue}REF} This connection has allowed to build an algebraic theory for finite $CSP$'s leading to an algebraic version of the Feder-Vardi dichotomy conjecture by Bulatov, Jeavons and Krokhin \cite{BJK} that has been eventually solved by Bulatov and Zhuk. {\color{blue}REF}

%The algebraic theory can be built for nice ($\omega$-categorical) infinite domains, see Bodirsky and Ne\v{s}et\v{r}il leading to a generalization of the algebraic dichotomy conjecture.
We concentrate on {\it temporal constraint languages}, by which we mean $CSP$ languages with domain $\mathbb{Q}$ and relations first-order definable from $(\mathbb{Q},<)$. Bodirsky and K\'ara \cite{ BK} proved dichotomy for temporal $CSP$ languages and gave a characterization. Here we use it in the version of Bodirsky and Pinsker (Theorem 51, \cite{BP}). We will introduce the nine operations from the theorem in the next subsection. See Definition 13 in \cite{BKW} for a suitable definition of {\it pp interpretation}.

\begin{theorem}\label{temporal} Given a temporal constraint language ${\bf T}$ exactly one of the followings holds.

\begin{itemize}

\item
{The relations of ${\bf T}$ are preserved by one out of nine binary polymorphisms: min, mi, mx, lx, their duals or a constant operation, and $CSP({\bf T})$ is in $P$.}

\item
{NOT-ALL-EQUAL SAT (NAE) has a primitive positive interpretation in ${\bf T}$ with finitely many parameters. In this case, $CSP({\bf T})$ is $NP$--complete.}

\end{itemize}

\end{theorem}

Since $NAE$ (or, equivalently, $3$-hypergraph $2$-coloring) is $NP$-complete when restricted to bounded degree structures and pp-interpretation preserves bounded degree, we obtain the following useful corollary using Lemma \ref{degree}. We need this corollary in the proofs of Theorem \ref{main2} and Theorem \ref{main3}, since the deterministic SIL Theorem \ref{ordered} only holds for bounded degree structures (unlike the randomized SIL Theorem \ref{temporalSIL}).

\begin{corollary} \label{useful}
Given a temporal $NP$-complete constraint language ${\bf T}$ there exists $D$ and finitely many rational numbers such that adding the constant relations corresponding to these numbers to ${\bf T}$ gives a $CSP$ that is already $NP$-complete when restricted to structures with maximum degree $D$.
\end{corollary}

%We need the following Lemma \ref{degree} in order to deduce Corollary \ref{useful} from Theorem \ref{temporal}.
We have not found the following lemma in the literature, so we prove this straightforward consequence of the definitions.

\begin{lemma} \label{degree}
Consider the relational structures ${\bf A}$ and ${\bf B}$ of the same finite type. Assume that
${\bf A}$ pp-interprets ${\bf B}$, and that there is a $D_{\bf B}$ such that $CSP({\bf B})$ restricted to structures with degree at most $D_{\bf B}$ is $NP$-hard. Then there exists a $D_{\bf A}$ that $CSP({\bf A})$ restricted to structures with degree at most $D_{\bf A}$ is $NP$-hard.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By Definition 13 in \cite{BKW} of pp interpretation there exist $n>0, S \subseteq A^n$ and $\varphi: S \to B$ surjective mapping such that ${\bf A}$ pp-defines

\begin{itemize}
\item{the relation $S$,}

\item{the $\varphi$-preimage of the equality relation on ${\bf B}$,}

\item{the preimage of every $\tau$-relation in ${\bf B}$.}
\end{itemize}

Consider the structure ${\bf S}$ of type $\tau$ on base set $S$ and with the preimage of every $\tau$-relation in ${\bf B}$.
First, we show that there exists $D_{\bf S}$ such that $CSP({\bf S})$ is $NP$-hard when restricted to structures with degree at most $D_{\bf S}$.

For every $R \in \tau$ and relation $R({\bf B})$ of arity $r$ there exists a gadget structure
${\bf G}_R$ with $r$ distinct elements referred to as roots such that $\underline{s} \in S^r$ is in the relation $\varphi^{-1}(R({\bf B}))$
iff there exists a homomorphism
${\bf G}_R \longrightarrow {\bf S}$ mapping the $i$th root to $s_i$. Given a structure ${\bf U}$ with maximum degree $\Delta({\bf U}) \leq D_{\bf B}$ consider the following structure ${\bf V}$. Every element of $U$ is in $V$. For every relational tuple of $R({\bf S})$, where $R \in \tau$, we add a copy of the gadget structure ${\bf G}_R$ to ${\bf V}$ by identifying the roots with the coordinates of the relational tuple, while all the other elements will be pairwise distinct for every other gadget. Now ${\bf V} \in CSP({\bf S}) \iff {\bf U} \in CSP({\bf B})$. The maximum degree of ${\bf V}$ is at most is at most $D_{\bf S}=\Delta({\bf U})\cdot max_{R \in \tau} \Delta({\bf G}_R) \leq D_{\bf B}\cdot max_{R \in \tau} \Delta({\bf G}_R)$, since every element of $V$ is in at most $\Delta({\bf U})$ gadget structures.

Now we show that $CSP({\bf A})$ is also $NP$-hard for bounded degree structures. Since $S$ is pp-definable in ${\bf A}$ there exists a gadget structure ${\bf G}_S$ with $n$ roots such that for $\underline{a} \in A^n$ there exists a homomorphism ${\bf G}_S \to {\bf A}$ mapping the $i$th root to $a_i$ iff $\underline{a} \in S$. Given a structure ${\bf V}$ assign to it the structure ${\bf Z}$, where $Z$ contains $V \times [n]$, for every $v \in V$ we have a copy of the gadget ${\bf G}_S$ such that the $i$th root is identified with $(v,i)$ (else these additional gadget elements are pairwise distinct), and we inherit relational tuples from ${\bf V}$ (we assign to every $r$-tuple in ${\bf V}$ an $(nr)$-tuple in ${\bf Z}$). Now
${\bf Z} \in CSP({\bf A}) \iff {\bf U} \in CSP({\bf S})$, and $\Delta({\bf Z})\leq \Delta({\bf V})+\Delta({\bf G}_S)$. Hence $CSP({\bf A})$ is $NP$-hard when restricted to structures with degree at most $D_{\bf A}=D_{\bf S}+\Delta({\bf G}_S)$.
\end{proof}


\subsection{The complexity of temporal $CSP$ languages corresponding to a single ordered graph}

The goal of this subsection is to prove Corollary \ref{Rodlcor} of Proposition \ref{Rodl} in order to prove the Duffus-Ginn-R\"odl conjecture \cite{DGR}, that ordering problems described by a single forbidden biconnected ordered graph are $NP$-complete. This boils down to the question if certain temporal constraint languages are $NP$-complete, i.e., they are not preserved by the nine operations from the characterization of the dichotomy \cite{BK}.

Consider the symmetric group $S_r$ and a subgroup $H \leq S_r$. Define the $r$-ary relation $R_{S_r, H}$ on $(\mathbb{Q},<)$ by $(x_1, \dots ,x_r) \in R_{S_r,H}$ iff the permutation $i \mapsto x_i$ is {\bf not} in $H$. We often abbreviate it as $R_H$ or just $R$ when the context allows it.

\begin{proposition} \label{Rodl}
Consider $H \leq S_r$, assume that $H$ contains neither the stabilizer of $1$ nor the stabilizer of $r$. Then none of the nine operations min, mi, mx, ll, their duals and the constant operations preserve $(\mathbb{Q},R_{S_r,H})$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{corollary} \label{Rodlcor}
Assume that ${\bf G}^<$ is not isomorphic to any of the following ordered graphs: a complete graph, an independent set, a star where the center is a minimal or a maximal element, a complete graph plus one isolated vertex that is minimal or maximal. Then none of the nine operations min, mi, mx, ll, their duals and the constant operations preserve $(\mathbb{Q},R_{Sym(G), Aut({\bf G})})$.
\end{corollary}

We prove the Duffus-Ginn-R\"odl conjecture assuming Corollary \ref{Rodlcor} of Proposition \ref{Rodl}.

\begin{proof}(of Theorem \ref{main3})
Consider the structure ${\bf T}=(\mathbb{Q},R_{Sym(G),Aut({\bf G})})$. If we add finitely many parameters (rational numbers as unary, constant relations) then the resulting ${\bf T}_+$ becomes $NP$-complete by Corollary \ref{Rodlcor} and Theorem \ref{temporal}. Corollary \ref{useful} shows that $CSP({\bf T}_+)$ restricted to bounded degree relational structures is still $NP$-complete.

Consider the $\{ {\bf G}_+^< \}$-ordering problem, the $\{ {\bf G}^< \}$-ordering problem where we also allow to prescribe the ordering on a subset of constant size (corresponding to the rational numbers we added to obtain ${\bf T}_+$). The $\{ {\bf G}_+^< \}$-ordering problem and the $\{ {\bf G}^< \}$-ordering problem are polynomially equivalent by Lemma \ref{param}.

Proposition \ref{precise} describes the $\{ {\bf G}_+^< \}$-ordering problem for a biconnected ${\bf G}$ by forbidden lifts, we choose $\tau'=\mathbb{Q}$, while $\tau$ contains one binary relation and for every parameter a unary one. Thus, $CSP({\bf T}_+)$ for bounded degree relational structures can be reduced to the $\{{\bf G}_+^<\}$-ordering problem by $(2)$ of Theorem \ref{theCSP} and the deterministic SIL Lemma \ref{ordered}.
This completes the proof of Theorem \ref{main3}.
\end{proof}

%Consider a finite ordered graph $({\bf G},<)$, where we may assume wlog that the set of vertices is $[|G|]=\{1, \dots ,|G| \}$ and the ordering is just the natural ordering. And consider the $|G|$-ary relation $R=R_{({\bf G},<)}$ on $(\mathbb{Q},<)$, where $(x_1, \dots ,x_{|G|}) \in R$ iff $i \mapsto x_i$ is NOT an automorphism of the graph ${\bf G}$.
The rest of the section is devoted to the proof of Proposition \ref{Rodl}.
We check when are relations $R_H$ preserved by certain polymorphisms on $(\mathbb{Q},<)$ in Claims \ref{min}-\ref{ll} in order to prove that $CSP(R_{Sym(G),Aut({\bf G})})$ is $NP$-complete.
It will follow that with a few exceptions every such $CSP$ is $NP$-complete.


In the biconnected case the corresponding ordering problem is also $NP$-complete.

We say that a tuple $\underline{x} \in \mathbb{Q}^{|G|}$ {\it represents a permutation} $\mu \in Sym([|G|])$ if $\mu(i)<\mu(j) \iff x_i<x_j$ for every $1 \leq i,j \leq |G|$.

We will utilize the corollary of the following simple claim.

\begin{claim}
Given a positive integer $r$ and an arbitrary permutation $\mu \in S_r$ there is a sequence $\alpha_0, \dots ,\alpha_k \in S_r$ such that $\alpha_0=1, \alpha_k=\mu$ and for every $0 \leq \ell \leq k$ there exists $i_{\ell} \in [r]$ that $\alpha_{\ell+1}\alpha_{\ell}^{-1}(j)=j$ for $j < i_{\ell},\mu(i_{\ell})=r$ and $\alpha_{\ell+1}\alpha_{\ell}^{-1}(j)=j-1$ for $j>i_{\ell}$.
\end{claim}

\begin{proof}
We find a sequence $\alpha_0=1, \dots \alpha_r=\mu$, where for every $1 \leq m \leq \ell$ we have
$\alpha_{\ell}(\mu^{-1}(m))=m+r-\ell$.
We construct $\alpha_1, \dots ,\alpha_r$ recursively.
Once $\alpha_{\ell}$ is defined set
$\alpha_{\ell}(\mu^{-1}(m))=m+|G|-\ell-1$ for $1 \leq m \leq \ell+1$, and $\alpha_{\ell}(m)=\mu(m)-\ell$ for $m \notin \mu^{-1}([\ell])$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary} \label{perm}
Given a positive integer $r$ and a subgroup $H \lneq S_r$ there exists a permutation $\pi \notin H$ and $i \in [r]$ such that $\pi(j)=j$ for $j < i,\pi(i)=r$ and $\pi(j)=j-1$ for $j>i$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Apply the previous Claim to a permutation $\mu \notin  H$. There exist $\ell$ and $\alpha_{\ell} \in H, \alpha_{\ell+1} \notin H$. Set $\pi=\alpha_{\ell+1}\alpha_{\ell}^{-1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma} \label{shortlist}
Consider the positive integers $1 \leq i \leq r$ and a subgroup $H \leq S_r$. Assume that $H$ contains every permutation $\mu \in S_r$ such that $\mu(i)=1$. Then either $H=S_r$ or $i=1$ and $H$ is the stabilizer of $1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The subgroup $K \leq S_r$ generated by such permutations $\mu$ should contain the stabilizer of $i$, in fact, it equals $K^{-1} \cdot K$. The stabilizer is maximal subgroup, thus, the lemma follows.
\end{proof}

First, we consider the binary operation $min$, the minimum w.r.t. the natural ordering of $\mathbb{Q}$.

\begin{claim} \label{min}
If $H \lneq S_r$ and the binary operation $min(x,y)$ preserves $R=R_{S_r, H}$, then $H=S_r$ or $H$ is the stabilizer of $1$.
\end{claim}

\begin{proof}
First, we use Corollary \ref{perm} to get a permutation $\pi \notin H$ of a special form w.r.t. an $i \in [r]$. Define the $r$-tuple $\underline{x} \in \mathbb{Q}^r$ by $x_j=\pi(j)$, so $\underline{x} \in R$.
Choose an $r$-tuple $\underline{y} \in \mathbb{Q}^r$ such that $y_i=i-\frac{1}{2}$ and $r-1 < y_j < r$ if $j \neq i$, and the coordinates of
$\underline{y}$ are pairwise distinct. Note that every permutation in $S_r$ mapping $i$ to $i$ can be represented this way. If every such permutation is in $H$ then we are done by Lemma \ref{shortlist}.
Else consider such an $\underline{y}$ representing a permutation in $R$. Note that $min(\underline{x},\underline{y}) \notin R$, since it represents the identity permutation, hence $R$ is not preserved by $min$.
\end{proof}

Now consider the binary operation(s) {\it mx} defined by

$$mx(x,y)= \begin{cases}
\alpha(min\{ x,y \}) \text{ if } x \neq y, \\
\beta(x) \text{ if } x=y,
\end{cases}$$

\noindent
where $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are strictly monotone, unary operations on $(\mathbb{Q},<)$ such that $\alpha(x) < \beta(x) <\alpha(x+\varepsilon)$ for any $x \in \mathbb{Q}, 0<\varepsilon \in \mathbb{Q}$.

\begin{claim}
If $H \lneq S_r$ and the binary operation $mx$ preserves $R=R_{S_r, H}$, then $H=S_r$ or $H$ is the stabilizer of $1$.
\end{claim}

\begin{proof}
The proof is the same as for the previous claim, since we might assume that the elements in the tuples $\underline{x}, \underline{y}$ are distinct from each other. Note that $mx=\alpha(min)$ when restricted to distinct pairs of elements. And the operations $\alpha(min)$ and $min$ preserve the same relations, hence we can follow the same argument as in the previous proof.
\end{proof}

The next operation to study is $mi$ defined by

$$mi(x,y)= \begin{cases}
\alpha(x) \text{ if }x <y,\\
\beta(x) \text{ if } x=y, \\
\gamma(y) \text{ if }y<x,
\end{cases}$$

where $\alpha, \beta$ and $\gamma$ are strictly monotone, unary operations of $(\mathbb{Q},<)$ such that $\beta(x) < \gamma(x) <\alpha(x) <\beta(x+\varepsilon)$ for any $x \in \mathbb{Q}, 0<\varepsilon \in \mathbb{Q}$.

\begin{claim}
If $H \lneq S_r$ and the binary operation $mi$ preserves $R=R_{S_r, H}$, then $H=S_r$ or $H$ is the stabilizer of $1$.
\end{claim}

\begin{proof}
The proof follows the same ideas as the previous ones, but we should be more careful. We use Corollary \ref{perm} to get a permutation $\pi \notin H$ of a special form w.r.t. $i \in [r]$.
First, we choose $\underline{x}=(x_1, \dots ,x_r) \in \mathbb{Q}^r$ representing the permutation $\pi$, such that $\gamma(x_{i-1}) < \alpha(x_{i+1})$. Note that $\underline{x} \in R$.

We choose again a permutation $\mu \notin H$ such that $\mu(i)=1$:
if there was no such permutation then Lemma \ref{shortlist} would yield the claim. Else choose $\underline{y} \in \mathbb{Q}^r$ representing the permutation $\mu$ with coordinates different from the coordinates of $\underline{x}$ such that $\alpha(x_{i-1}) < \gamma(y_i) < \alpha(x_{i+1}), y_i<x_i$ and $y_j > x_j$ for every $j \neq i$.
Note that $mi(\underline{x}, \underline{y})$ represents the identity permutation, since $\alpha$ is strictly monotone and $\alpha(x_{i-1})<\gamma(y_i) < \alpha(x_{i+1})$. Hence $mi(\underline{x}, \underline{y}) \notin R$, that is, $mi$ does not preserve $R$, and the Claim follows.
\end{proof}

Finally, consider the operation(s) ll satisfying the inequality $ll(a,b)<ll(a',b')$ if  $a \leq 0$ and $a < a'$, or $a<0, a=a'$ and $b<b'$, or $a,a'>0$ and $b<b'$, or $0<a<a'$ and $b=b'$.

\begin{claim} \label{ll}
If $H \lneq S_r$ and the binary operation $ll$ preserves $R=R_{S_r, H}$, then $H=S_r$ or $H$ is the stabilizer of $1$.
\end{claim}

\begin{proof}
We use again Corollary \ref{perm} in order to get a permutation $\pi \notin H$ of a special form w.r.t. $i \in [r]$. Consider $\underline{b} \in \mathbb{Q}^r$ representing $\pi^{-1}$ such that $b_j>0$ for every $j$, hence $0<b_2<b_3< \dots <b_r$.

If $H$ is not the stabilizer of $1$ then Lemma \ref{shortlist} provides a permutation $\mu \notin H$ such that $\mu(1)=1$. Consider $\underline{a} \in \mathbb{Q}^{|G|}$ representing $\mu$ such that $a_1<0$ and $a_j>0$ for every $j>1$. $ll(\underline{a},\underline{b})$ represents $1$:

$ll(a_1, b_1)<ll(a_j, b_j)$ if $2 \leq j \leq 2$, since $a_1 < 0 < a_j$,

$ll(a_j,b_j)<ll(a_k, b_k)$ if $1 \leq j  <  k \leq r$, since $a_j, a_k>0$ and $b_j<b_k$.

Therefore, $\underline{a}, \underline{b} \in R$ and $ll(\underline{a},\underline{b}) \notin R$, so $ll$ does not preserve $R$. This completes the proof of the claim.
\end{proof}


\begin{proof}(of Proposition \ref{Rodl})
The previous four claims and their duals show that none of the operations min, mi, mx, ll or their duals preserve $R_{S_r,H}$. The constant operations also do not preserve it, so Theorem \ref{temporal} implies the proposition.
\end{proof}



