\subsection{Algorithms}
This section describes the optimization algorithms     applied to Table \ref{combinedtable} to  generate the results given in Table \ref{tab:results}. 

\subsubsection{Random Search}
Grid search has long been considered inefficient for exploring large hyperparameter spaces. Fu et al. show that grid search is $1000-10000\times$ times slower than the default learner \cite{fu2016differential}. Random search, which samples options randomly, is a simple alternative. Although non-adaptive, it runs in reasonable time and is widely used as a baseline for evaluating adaptive algorithms \cite{bergstra2012random}.  

\subsubsection{Hyperband}
Hyperband \cite{li2018hyperband} is a multi-fidelity optimization algorithm that accelerates the search by performing cheap, low-fidelity labelling early on. It combines successive halving and early stopping by evaluating many randomly sampled options at lower fidelity levels early and runs the best ones at higher fidelity levels in later rounds, until the most optimal options are found. Thus, it solves manually specifying a fixed fidelity budget or number of options to evaluate. However, Hyperband is non-adaptive - it does not use the results from previous iterations to guide the selection of options in the subsequent ones. 

\subsubsection{Bayesian Optimization (BO)}
As labelling is expensive, BO constructs a surrogate model to approximate labelling. It uses this surrogate model to predict the performance of each option and applies an acquisition function, used for balancing exploration/exploitation, to select the next option to evaluate. The selected option is now labelled and added to the data to retrain the surrogate. 

Common surrogate models include Gaussian Process Models (GPM) \cite{rasmussen2006gaussian}, which provide smooth uncertainty estimates, but are not scalable with $O(n^3)$ complexity. An alternative is the Tree Parzen Estimator (TPE) \cite{bergstra11TPE}, which models the surrogate over input space using separate Kernel Density Estimators for best-performing (best) and low-performing (rest) regions, and selects the option that maximizes their ratio. TPE's linear-time complexity makes it a scalable choice over GPMs. 

BOHB \cite{falkner2018bohb} combined the strengths of Bayesian Optimization (BO) and Hyperband. It replaced Hyperband's initial random sampling with BO-guided search, incorporated early-stopping to reduce wasting resource on poor candidates. Consequently, BOHB outperforms GPM, TPE, and HB. Later, Awad et al. showed that BOHB does not scale well to high-dimensional spaces with discrete dimensions, which led to the development of DEHB \cite{awadijcai2021p296}. 

\subsection{Evolutionary Algorithms}

Many of the problems in Table~\ref{combinedtable} are multi-objective. This requires considering the trade-offs between possibly conflicting, non-linear goals. In SE, such problems  are traditionally explored via  {\em Evolutionary Algorithms (EA)}, which {\em mutate} a {\em population} of choices through many {\em generations}

Early methods such as {\em simulated annealing} algorithms~\cite{kirkpatrick1983optimization} (SA) have a populations size of $n=1$. The current solution is randomly perturbed (i.e. mutated), and selected if it yields a higher score under an objective function. To escape local optima, SA occasionally accepts worse solutions during its  initial ``hot'' phase. As the algorithm cools, the likelihood of accepting worse solutions decrease.

When memory became less expensive, researchers scaled up to larger populations. For example, Holland-style 
{\em genetic algorithms}~\cite{holland1992genetic} (GAs) build an initial population of $n\gg1$ (e.g. $n=100$) choices (called {\em generation0}) then mutates it for $G\gg1$ (e.g. $G=100$) generations, where $n$ and $G$ and chosen by engineering judgment. In each generation, a small portion of each choice is mutated, and  {\em children} are formed by combining parts of two {\em parent} choices. The best children are {\em selected} to survive and breed in the next generation. 

Differential Evolution (DE) \cite{price2005differential} builds on the GA principles while introducing a more systematic approach to mutation. New candidates are generated based on the differences between two other candidates. DE has shown faster convergence than many variants of GA \cite{das2010differential}.  This can be made more scalable by incorporating early stopping such as the one in Differential Evolution and Hyperband (DEHB), proposed by Awad et al. to incorporate the benefits of both DE and HB while solving the problems of BOHB mentioned previously. DEHB starts with random selection of candidates and uses Hyperband over these candidates for resource allocation and to construct a subpopulation at each fidelity level. This subpopulation works as the optimal region, also called `parent pool` in DEHB which can be sampled for mutations for DE evolution in higher budget, instead of directly suggesting best candidates. Evaluated on non-SE benchmarks, DEHB outperformed BOHB both in terms of performance and speed. Although DEHB is the current state-of-the-art optimizer, we found only one study applying DEHB to SE problems by Yedida et al. for hyperparameter optimization. In our case, we study a more comprehensive sets of problems spanning over 41 multiobjective optimization problems and comparing the performance against DEHB. 

In the Holland-style EAs mentioned above,  some evaluation function $f$ would be called $n{\times}G=10,000$ times.
When $f$ is hard to access, or slow to execute, data mining tools can be used
to build an approximate {\em surrogate} model that can   quickly guess $y$ values~\cite{pmlr-v28-zuluaga13}. For example, to explore a very large number of configurations,
 Nair et al. built  a CART regression tree   from a relatively small sample of $x,y$ pairs. The tree was then used to explore the other configurations; e.g. given two configurations, Nair et al. would prune the one that generates a lower score from the surrogate~\cite{nair2017using}. 

\section{SWAY}
For four reasons, in our own recent work, we have moved away from EAs:
\begin{enumerate}
\item
If surrogates can be an effective guide for EA (as shown by Nair et al.), why not dispense with the EA and just use the surrogate?
\item EA assumes problems of the form \[y=f(x)\] where $f$ is known and can be executed quickly to generate many $y$ values.
But for many  of the Table~\ref{combinedtable}  problems, we have examples of the input-output, but no prior knowledge of how the outputs are generated from the inputs. To say that another way, for this paper, we assume extensive access to $x$,  much less access to $y$, and no access to $f$.
\item For our purposes. EA needs too many labels. Recall that in the above example, the Holland-style EA required $n{\times}G=10,000$ labels, which is much more than the $k=24$ labels required by \S\ref{lessdata}.
\item
In 2016, Nair et al. accidentally found an alternative to EA~\cite{nair2016accidental}. {\em Diversity search} is a tactic for multi-objective optimization. After picking $k$ choices, a diversity-based EA seeks a new $k+1$ choice that is far away from the $k$ options explored before\footnote{This might also be called {\em tabu search}~\cite{glover1998tabu}.}. When porting a diversity-based EA to another language, Chen et al. accidentally broke the mutator so that {\em generations 1,2,3...} was very similar to {\em generation 0}. For problems similar to Table~\ref{combinedtable}, the ``broken'' optimizer worked just as well as the ``unbroken'' version.
\end{enumerate}
Chen went on to define two styles of mutation: 
\bi
\item  {\em Holland-style} mutation: e.g.  n=100, G=100; i.e. fill {\em generation 0} with $n=100$ candidates, then  mutate for  $G=100$ further generations.
\item   {\em Chen-style} mutation: e.g.  $n=10,000, G=0$; i.e. over-generate {\em generation 0} with $n=10,000$ candidates,  then  mutate for  no further generations (but explore the $10,000$ candidates with a  diversity search).
\ei

Based on this, Chen et al. proposed the Sampling Way (SWAY) approach, where generating very large {\em generation0}s, then applying diversity search by evaluating a few candidates and discarding many candidates near the evaluated candidates. In theory,  Holland-style mutation could work better. This method generates new options at runtime, allowing the inference to explore choices  in-between the initial guesses. Chen-style inference, on the other hand, is trapped into just exploring the initial set of options. However, Chen et al. \cite{nair2016accidental,Chen19} fourth that their style of EA worked just as well as Holland-style inference for SE problems like Table~\ref{combinedtable}. Moreover, SWAY is much faster than Holland-style algorithm which requires $n{\times}G=10,000$ labels, while the Chen-style algorithm only has to label the choices touched by the diversity search. We will return to Chen-style reasoning after discussing semi-supervised and active-learning.
\subsection{Active Learning}
Semi-supervised learning relies on the following assumptions. 
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Continuity Assumption:} Data points close to each other in a particular space tend to have similar labels.
    \item \textbf{Cluster Assumption: } Data that belongs to the same cluster are likely to belong to the same class.
    \item \textbf{Manifold assumption: }Data lies approximately on a manifold of   lower dimensions than the input space.
\end{itemize}
This infers that we do not need to label every points and labeling a few can be enough if others nearby are similar. In other words, decision boundaries should traverse low-density regions, thus avoiding the denser areas where clusters of data reside.

There are various strategies to exploit these assumptions, such as label propagation and graph-based reasoning~\cite{grandvalet2004semi}. We have already discussed SWAY, which is a simpler and computationally efficient based on these assumptions. In this method, initial candidate points are drawn from historical logs or generated through techniques such as Chen-style mutation. These candidates are then recursively refined using randomization or diversity clustering methods, which aim to select points that are sufficiently distant from others, ensuring better coverage of the input space. SWAY suffers from the problem with every greedy technique, once it splits the points to two candidate halves to recurse on, it freezes this choice. Hence, it can miss the global optimum if it lies off the pivot axis. 

LITE proposed by Menzies et al. used a Naive Bayes model to refit after every new label, incorporating global reflection. It enjoys the benefits of being computationally cheap because it uses Chen-style mutations similar to SWAY, initially selecting large random samples and iterating based on that. Instead of hard selecting after the first selection, it divides it into `best` and `rest` candidates, and trains a Naive Bayes surrogate on it. Next, it labels the unlabeled points using the trained model and passes the predictions through an acquisition function to get the next point to evaluate. This point is evaluated and added to the `best` or `rest`, and the Naive Bayes model is retrained. Thus, LITE does not permanently discard any region like SWAY. We observe that this process is actually a BO or Active Learning process with TPE-like surrogate via Naive Bayes. The rationale behind using a simple model like Naive Bayes is - surrogates remain useful even if imprecise, as long as they rank bad choices lower than good ones~\cite{nair2017using}. 

