\section{Our Method: {\IT}}


Diversity samplers with good scalability properties are Nyström algorithms and k-means++. Nyström methods, originally used to approximate large kernel matrices, provide a way to project data into lower dimensions by selecting a subset of representative points. A variant, FASTMAP, accelerates this by iteratively projecting points onto a line defined by two distant pivots, efficiently preserving pairwise distances. This projection enables diverse sampling by spreading points across different regions of the input space. 

Similarly, k-means++ is an enhancement of the k-means clustering algorithm that initializes cluster centers more strategically. By selecting each new center with a probability proportional to its squared distance from the nearest existing center, k-means++ ensures that initial clusters are well-separated. This minimizes the chances of poor local minima and leads to more robust, diverse sampling even without performing the full clustering step. 


While not widely recognized as such, the k-means++ pre-processor can be used without subsequent clustering to select a representative set of diverse choices. This ensures broad coverage of the input space and reduces the number of evaluations needed for downstream tasks. Combined with Nyström/Fastmap, this often yields faster convergence in optimization problems by focusing on a few well-distributed candidates \cite{zhu2005semi,chapelle2006semi}.

SWAY was the first optimizer in SE to convert FASTMAP's one dimensional projection into a search strategy. We have already discussed that SWAY's use of FASTMAP yields axis bias - assuming that the line connecting two pivots intersects or is nearby to the region containing the optimal points. Moreover, SWAY's stopping rule, user specific or $\sqrt{N}$, where is the initial sample size, as used in the paper, is heuristic. There is not guarantee that remaining candidates do not contain the optimal points. While LITE repairs SWAY's greedy nature, its initial samples are random rather than diversity-maximizing, which can begin far from optimal regions. Most importantly, LITE offers no analytical guidance on how many labels are enough. Practitioners must select a budget ex ante or depend on ad hoc convergence threshold.   

We propose LINE which replaces FASTMAP's one-dimensional projection with a single pass of KMeans++. As each of the centroids is selected probability proportional to its squared distance from all previously selected centroids, the resulting set of points is provably well-spread across the configuration space. This "spread-first" step ensures that at least one centroid falls inside every major cluster, eliminating the possibility of discarding an optimal region. In fact, KMeans++ comes with formal guarantees  that its centroids close to the centers of the actual structures in a  table of data~\cite{arthur2006k} After sampling, LINE performs no iterative learning. It evaluates the centroids, orders them by score, and returns the best one. The question of when to stop is answered by the LI + NE principle (discussed below). Thus, LINE preserves the economy of SWAY and LITE while avoiding their weaknesses.  

Algorithm~1 described LINE. This algorithm takes as input as a space of candidate solutions. These solutions are found  either by {\em finding} or {\em generating} examples via - (1) Some stochastic processing like fuzzing; or (2) Accessing some historical record. The algorithm then uses  $k$-means++ ~\cite{arthur2006k} centroid detector to find $k$ centroids. it  selects the first centroid at random, then $k-1$ times, select a new centroid by assigning a probability to each remaining example based on its squared distance from the nearest existing  centroid.  This selection procedure ensures that the centroids are spread over the space of examples. Next, the surviving $k$ choices are evaluated, sorted by their objective function score, and the best one is returned (Not shown in the algorithm). 

{\em LINE}   makes two assumptions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item[LI:] {\em Limited investigations}   Unlike standard Pareto reasoning~\cite{Deb02}, we do not rank all   generated solutions. Instead, we only seek to find a few (perhaps even just one) solution(s) that is  notably better that most of the rest.
    \item[NE:] {\em Near Enough}:  
  Given results $r_1,r_2$ from $k_1,k_2$ samples where $k_1 < k_2$, we  can stop at $k_1$ if $r_1,r_2$ are statistically indistinguishable  from the optimal.
\end{itemize}

 % (as determined by   
 % non-parametric significance   and effect size %tests~\cite{romano2006appropriate,efron1994introduction}).
This first ``LI'' assumption  leads to a very simple implementation: i.e. pick $k$ things, and ignore the rest (see Algorithm~1).  To implement that picking, we use the $k$-means++ centroid detector.

The second ``NE'' assumption offers an early stopping rule. It says we need not to seek the optimal solution. Instead, we just need to find a solution that is  ``near enough'' (i.e. statistically indistinguishable)  to the best solution.

If we seek just one solution (LI) that is near enough (NE) to the best solution, it turns out that that the results of this paper can be predicted mathematically:
\bi
\item Cohen~\cite{gignac2016effect} rule's defines ``near enough'' for Gaussian: two events in a normal distribution are  negligibly different if they differ by less than $q*\sigma$. A value of $q=0.35$ is the border between Cohen's {\em small} effects (at $q=0.2$) and a {\em medium} effects (at $q=0.5$). Most of the mass of a normal curve is found across the  six standard deviations 
  $-3\sigma \le x \le 3\sigma$. Hence,  at probability $p=0.35/6$,  any choice we make is``near enough'' to the best choice.
\item According to Hamlet's {\em probable correctness theory}, events at probability $p$ do not happen after $k$ trials at probability $(1-p)^k$. Hence we are certain at some level $C$ of seeing that event at $C=1-(1-p)^k$. Assuming $p=0.35/6$, then  probable correctness theory tells us that the number of labels required to be ``near enough'' to the best choice is:
\begin{equation}\label{epct}
C=1-(0.94^k) 
\end{equation} 
\ei
\begin{figure}
\begin{center} \includegraphics[width=3in]{theory_conf_2.png}
\end{center}
\caption{Theoretical predictions
from  Equation~\ref{epct} closely match the   experimental results shown later in this paper.  }\label{pcf}
\end{figure}
As shown by the red line of Figure~\ref{pcf},
Equation~\ref{epct} predicts the results noted in the introduction of this paper.
\bi
\item A very small number of labels can find a good solution;
\item A very large number of labels may not find a better one.
\ei
The above maths makes numerous simplifying assumptions. Cohen's rule assumes that the distributions are Gaussians. It also makes use of certain ``magic numbers'' (e.g. 0.35). As to Hamlet's equation, it assumes that solutions fall evenly across the abscissa (i.e. they do not bunch into one corner) and that the $k$ trials are independent.

Despite these potential limitations, Equation~\ref{epct}'s predictions are very accurate. Later in this paper, we will ask a variety
of methods to solve multi-objective functions after labeling just $k\in\{6,12,18,24,50,100,200\}$ choices. Those results are shown as the large blue dots of Figure~\ref{pcf}. Note that these actual results are remarkably close to the theoretical predictions of Equation~\ref{epct} (shown as the red line). 
\section{Experimental Rig}
The following experimental rig was designed to evaluate LINE.
\subsection{Optimization Techniques}
From the aforementioned techniques, we selected DEHB, LITE, SWAY, and Random to compare against our approach. We selected DEHB because it is the state-of-the-art evolutionary approach, surpassing its predecessors (e.g, BOHB, TPE, GP, etc.) in performance. Random is often recommended as a baseline \cite{bergstra2012random} as it helps to evaluate how adaptive approaches improve over random sampling \cite{bergstra2012random}. Lite and SWAY were selected because these are recent, data-efficient optimizers that are designed for low-budget optimization. Moreover, as we build on Chen-style mutations similar to LITE and SWAY, these serve as meaningful baselines to evaluate how our approach improves against similar foundations.  

We ran DEHB, Random, LITE, and LINE for 6-200 labels. This is because we observed all of the approaches to plateau before 200 labels, and found no benefit of conducting additional runs. All experiments were repeated 20 times with different random seeds.   

We implement DEHB using the package\footnote{\url{https://github.com/automl/DEHB}} provided by the authors. As our experiments are based on tabular benchmarks with a fixed set of options, DEHB-generated options may not align with the available ones. We address this by rounding the configurations proposed by DEHB to the nearest available configuration in the dataset, which is a common approach when working with such benchmarks \cite{pfisterer2022yahpo, zela2022}. 

