\section{Background}
\subsection{optimization and SE}

Modern software offers a vast array of settings, providing
flexibility but also creating a significant challenge: choosing the
right ones. As systems evolve, they accumulate even more
configuration options \cite{Xu15a, VanAken2017}, often far too
many for users to manage effectively or even understand.
Consequently, many options are ignored \cite{Xu15a}, and software
may not perform optimally---a ``configuration gap'' between its
potential and actual performance \cite{Xu15a}. This isn't just
about missing peak performance; incorrect configurations or
outdated defaults are a primary cause of system failures
\cite{Zhou2011} and can lead to drastically slow performance,
sometimes hundreds of times worse than achievable
\cite{Herodotou11, Jamshidi2016proposal}.

Given that manual management of this complexity is often
unfeasible, automated assistance is crucial \cite{Apel2020}. This
is where ``auto-configuration'' comes in---using techniques to
help systems find their best settings. The process of identifying
these optimal settings from countless possibilities is known as
``optimization'' \cite{Siegmund2015}. Optimization is essential
because the number of possible configurations can be unimaginably
large. For instance, one database system (MySQL) was cited as
having more potential configurations ($2^{460}$) \cite{Xu15a}
than there are estimated stars in the observable universe
($2^{80}$) \cite{doe2023personal}. Trying all combinations is
impossible. Thus, specialized optimization methods are vital to
intelligently search this vast space and find effective settings
efficiently \cite{Siegmund2015}. This need for optimization
extends beyond software products to the processes used to build
them \cite{Menzies2009}, making optimization a necessary and
fundamental discipline in modern software engineering for achieving
specific goals effectively.



