\subsection{Motivation }
This paper was inspired by a recent failed experiment with   the Gemini 1.5 Pro language language model~\cite{senthilkumar2024can}.
In that work, the
 optimization problems of Table~\ref{combinedtable}
where attempted  using the LLM as a ``warm start'' to create good initial guesses. 
Compared to  UCB (a non-neural active learning algorithm~\cite{settles2009active}), this LLM's optimizations were sub-optimum, especially for larger problems. Another failure was the CPU cost-- 
LLMs were  two orders of magnitude slower that simple frequency count methods.

Those negative  results motivated  experiments at the ``other end'' from big data.
Instead of using petabytes of data and millions of dollars to train an LLM, we asked  how much could be done with something much simpler. Hence, this paper.


\subsection{Why Explore ``Little Data'' ?}
We explore ``little data'' methods for three reasons.

First, as mentioned in the introduction, bigger is not always better,
In the SE domain there are numerous examples
where
other methods may produce results better
and/or faster~\cite{grinsztajn2022why,somvanshi2024survey,Tawosi23,majumder2018500+,Ling24,Fu17,johnson2024ai}. Hence we argue that it is mistake to say that big data methods are always better (or, indeed, always worse) than alternatives. Rather, they should be explored alongside those    alternates. {\IT} is useful since it is an alternative that can be explored very quickly.

Secondly
there are methodological concerns with basing our results on a few models built at great expense by just a  few private companies. 
A  recent 2024  report by the Royal Society on ``Science in the Age''~\cite{royal2024ai}   
comments that the reproducibility  of big data results is often limited, as AI-driven results are difficult to replicate\footnote{For one example of that, a student in our lab came back from a summer internship at Google  to report that she was using three years of CPU, each day, for image processing experiments. Given the resources of a standard university researcher, there is no way that our lab could ever repeat and check results generated in that manner.}.
That report warned that  open science is constrained by a black-box nature of AI and the limited transparency of commercial models.
The report also raised concerns with the growing
environmental costs of big AI methods, with large compute infrastructures consuming vast amounts of energy. 



Thirdly, even though there is   much  publicly available SE data, much of that  is  often irrelevant,  corrupted or incomplete. 

{\bf Data irrelevancies:} SE development comprises many activities. Data collected from those projects hence relates to many effects, not all of which may be relevant
to a particular analytics task.
Therefore it is not surprising that feature section and instance selection
(a.k.a. column pruning and row pruning)
 can discard much SE data, without loss of signal. For example, 
Chen, Kocaguneli,   Tu, and Peters et.al 
report that for 
predicting
Github issue close time,
effort estimation, and 
defect prediction,  their learners   are competitive even    pruning 80\%, 91\%, 97\%, 98\%  (respectively) of their  project data~\cite{Chen05, Kocaguneli13,Tu22,peters2015lace2}.

{\bf Data corruption:} When Yu et al.~\cite{9226105} checked by hand the  labels from prior work exploring technical debt, he found that over 90\% of the "false positives" were incorrectly labeled. In other domains,
errors in data labels have corrupted many  of  security bug datasets~\cite{9371393}; or
data sets exploring  false alarms in static code analysis~\cite{10.1145/3510003.3510214}.
Clearing up such corrupted data can be arduously expensive\footnote{After an extensive analysis, Tu et al.~\cite{Tu22z} proposed a cost model for labeling the
  476K commit files  they were studying.  Assuming two people checking per commit,   labeling would need three years of effort.}.
The results of this paper offer  a    much cheaper approach,  i.e. ignore most it and only check tiny parts of the data.

{\bf Data incompleteness:} 
 SE data  is incomplete when there are missing columns. 
 Consider tabular datasets where rows represent examples  from a function \( y = f(x) \). Here, the \( x \) columns capture data on independent input factors, which may include observable or controllable variables (e.g., lines of code). Also,  the \( y \) columns store data on dependent outcomes (e.g., the number of bugs per line).  
A common challenge in SE is that we can find much $x$ column data, but far less $y$ data. 
In this context:  
\begin{itemize}
    \item {\em Unlabeled} examples have \( x \) values but no   \( y \) values.  
    \item {\em Labeling} refers to the process of determining \( y \) values based on the given \( x \) values.  
\end{itemize}
Unlabeled \( x \) data is abundant and often freely available (e.g.,  open-source projects on GitHub).  
But high-quality labeled \( y \) data is significantly more difficult to obtain.  
For instance:
\bi
\item
While it is straightforward to find software projects on GitHub, determining the resources or costs involved in creating that software, such as team expenditures, is far more challenging (particular when reasoning
abut proprietary systems built by organizations that hide their developers behind firewalls). 
\item In requirements engineering, the software management models explored by Chen et al.~\cite{Chen19} had    
$5^{24}\approx10^{16}$ possible input settings. 
\item
Software configuration files can so many  options  ($x$) that it becomes impractical to 
computer all their   effects ($y$).
For example,  the Makefiles explored by 
Nair et al.~\cite{Nair} had  over three billion  possible settings. 
\item
Hyperparameter optimization adjust the   control parameters ($x$) of a learner (and/or the data pre-prcessing) in order to improve the predictions ($y$) of those learners.  Table~1   of Agrawal et al.~\cite{agrawal2021simpler} lists the billions of possible  hyperparameter options seen in recent SE papers.
\ei
From these examples, we assert that   it is useful to have methods that can reason about SE problems using extensive knowledge of $x$ variables
but limited knowledge of $y$.
