In interactive search-based software engineering (iSBSE), humans  act as oracles to guide the search process. In our own iSBSE research~\cite{lustosa2024learning} we can confirm that high-quality human insights were collected at the expected rate of two dozen labels per hour.
\item
{\em Repertory grid studies} further illustrate the time-intensive nature of labeling tasks. Such studies interviews where humans justify attributes and attribute settings by repeatedly contrasting    three examples, randomly drawn from a larger dataset. Easterby-Smith~\cite{EASTERBYSMITH19803} advises keeping repertory grids small, as grids with ten elements and ten constructs can take up to two hours to complete. Larger grids require substantially more time. Kington~\cite{kington2009defining} concurs, noting that reflecting on 16 examples with 16 attributes using repertory grids typically requires an hour.
