On the other hand, suppose the  buckets represent naturally occurring structures in the data that must be respected. In this second case, by restricting the thinking to just the buckets, pool-based optimization should do as well (or better) as membership queries.



There’s also a third setting worth knowing:

3. Stream-based (or online) Active Learning
Data points arrive sequentially (a stream).

The learner must decide on the fly whether to query the label for each instance.

Useful when you can't store or scan the full pool.


Automated Software Engineering (SE) thrives on effective Hyperparameter
Optimization (HPO). Our discussion begins by acknowledging a critical
phenomenon: the "BINGO effect." This effect states that in high-
dimensional SE data, when attributes are binned, most data collapses
into surprisingly few unique buckets ($n \ll b^d$)~\cite{snippet3}. This
suggests that extensive, CPU-heavy optimization methods, which evaluate
thousands to millions of solutions~\cite{Menzies07a,linares2015optimizing},
are often needlessly elaborate. Instead, simpler approaches that exploit
this inherent data sparsity can be equally effective, yet orders of
magnitude faster.

---
Consequently, \textit{Random Search}~\cite{bergstra2011algorithms} becomes
understandable. Despite its lack of adaptive intelligence, simply sampling
random configurations can surprisingly yield good results in SE, such as
for defect prediction~\cite{nevendra2022empirical}, because it has a
higher chance of hitting one of those "few buckets." However, it still
lacks the sophistication to leverage discovered good regions.

---
Extending this simplicity while adding intelligence, many \textit{Evolutionary
Algorithms} (EAs) also benefit from the BINGO effect. Unlike older
"Holland-style" mutation~\cite{holland92}, which is computationally heavy,
\textit{Differential Evolution (DE)}~\cite{storn1997differential} is more
efficient. DE's iterative refinement of a small population aligns well
with searching a sparse space, finding optimal "buckets" by mixing
candidates. This has been widely applied in SE optimization, from tuning
defect prediction~\cite{Fu2016Tuning} to optimizing security issue
detectors~\cite{shu2022dazzle} and project health predictors
\cite{xia2022predicting}.

---
\textit{Multi-Fidelity Methods} act as a meta-principle for HPO,
leveraging "cheap proxy tasks." \textit{Successive Halving}
\cite{li2018hyperband} in \textit{Hyperband (HB)} exemplifies this. By
starting many configurations with minimal budget and iteratively pruning
poor performers, these methods efficiently explore a potentially vast
space, yet implicitly focus on the relevant "buckets" without exhaustive
search.

---
Complementing this, \textit{Iterated Racing} algorithms similarly evaluate
and discard inferior solutions. Successive Halving is one such form.
\textit{irace}~\cite{lopez2016irace}, implementing Iterated F-race,
also efficiently refines algorithm configuration, implicitly navigating
the BINGO-reduced search space.

---
For costly evaluations, \textit{Bayesian Optimization} (BO)
\cite{NIPS2012_05311655} builds a "surrogate model" to quickly estimate
outcomes, guiding selection via an "acquisition function" through "active
learning"~\cite{settles2009active}. This focus on intelligent sampling over
exhaustive search naturally aligns with the BINGO effect. While
\textit{Gaussian Process Models (GPMs)} are common surrogates, they can
be slow~\cite{nair2018finding}. \textit{Tree of Parzen Estimators (TPE)}
\cite{bergstra2012random} is a faster alternative.

Crucially, \textit{LITE}~\cite{menzies2024streamlining}, a distinct BO
method, explicitly leverages the BINGO effect's implications. Inspired by
TPE but forgoing complex Parzen windows, LITE employs a simpler Bayes
classifier to model "best" and "rest" configurations~\cite{wu2008top}. It
selects the next evaluation by maximizing its unique acquisition function:
$$\frac{b+rq}{|bq - r + \epsilon|}$$
where $b,r$ are best/rest likelihoods, $\epsilon$ is tiny, and $q$ smoothly
transitions the strategy from "exploring" to "exploiting" as more data
becomes available within the budget. This focus on deriving maximum
information from minimal samples makes LITE ideal when the BINGO effect
is strong~\cite{snippet3}.

---
Earlier, \textit{SWAY} emerged as an SE optimizer using FASTMAP's one-
dimensional projection for search. However, SWAY's axis bias and heuristic
stopping rule limited its BINGO-awareness, as it could miss relevant
"buckets" not aligned with its projection.

---
Finally, \textit{LINE} (also \textit{\IT}), offers an efficient and
provably robust HPO approach that directly exploits the BINGO effect's
implications. Unlike SWAY's problematic projections or LITE's random
initial samples (before learning begins), LINE uses a single pass of
\textit{K-Means++}~\cite{arthur2006k} to select $k$ diverse centroids.
K-Means++ picks one random centroid, then $k-1$ more. Each new centroid
is chosen with probability proportional to its squared distance from the
*nearest* existing centroid. This "spread-first" strategy ensures selected
points are well-distributed across the data's inherent, lower-dimensional
structure (the "few buckets"), guaranteed to be near true cluster
centers~\cite{arthur2006k}. After selection, LINE simply evaluates these
$k$ centroids and returns the best, providing an economical and robust
alternative that capitalizes on data sparsity.

---
In contrast to these "BINGO-style" optimizers, the current state-of-the-
art, \textit{DEHB}~\cite{awad2021dehb}, represents a different paradigm,
focused on maximizing performance even when the BINGO effect might be less
pronounced. It masterfully synthesizes \textit{Differential Evolution (DE)}
within \textit{Hyperband}'s successive halving brackets. DEHB evolves
configurations at varying fidelity levels, allowing crucial information
flow. It intelligently reuses subpopulations, reducing redundant random
sampling. This balance of exploration and exploitation enables DEHB to
consistently outperform predecessors like BOHB~\cite{awad2021dehb} (itself
superior to Hyperopt~\cite{bergstra2015hyperopt}), making it a premier
choice for demanding deep learning and automated SE tasks, even when
data complexities might exceed the simplest BINGO interpretations.

