\subsection{Acoustic stimulus}

Acoustic stimulus is the form of an incident wave that is sent out to get information from scattered objects. In this paper, we use Exponential Sine Sweep (ESS), a type of acoustic stimulus that is proven to provide a high signal-to-noise (SNR) ratio in impulse response measurements \cite{farina2007advancements,garai2015optimizing}. This signal is formed by generating a sine sweep signal with exponentially increasing frequencies, as described by the following equation:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:acoustic-stim}
    x(t) = \sin\left[\frac{\omega_1T}{\ln\left(\frac{\omega_2}{\omega_1}\right)}\left(e^{\frac{1}{T}\ln\left(\frac{\omega_2}{\omega_1}\right)} — 1\right)\right]
\end{equation}
Where $w_1$ denotes the starting angular frequency, $w_2$ is the ending angular frequency and $T$ is the total stimulus duration in seconds. 
% The selection of frequency range and impulse duration depends on certain applications. 
% Since we do not have specific preferences, the frequency range was selected to be suitable with our available equipment. 

To comprehensively study the reflection, refraction, and scattering phenomena across a wide frequency range, we select $w_1=100$ and $w_2=24000$ as the frequency limits, with a stimulus duration of $T=5s$. Given the sensitivity of ESS measurements to non-stationary noise, we conduct our experiments in a soundproof room that maintains a reverberation time RT60 of $0.5s$. This controlled environment ensures accurate and reliable data collection.


\subsection{Hair moisture assessment}

