The success of self-supervised learning (SSL) models has extended beyond text to other domains such as audio and images \cite{doh2023lp}. SSL speech models like HuBERT \cite{hsu2021hubert} and Wav2Vec2 \cite{baevski2020wav2vec} have proven to be effective in speech and sound classification problems \cite{10889972, liu2025nes2net, gupta22_interspeech, 9979878, truong24b_interspeech}. In this paper, to apply SSL to audio classification, we utilised the Wav2Vec2-Conformer large \cite{wang2020fairseq} model with rotary position embeddings. The model was pre-trained for 960 hours on Librispeech. This SSL model performed better than others in our preliminary experiments. We keep the model hyperparameters the same as the pre-trained configuration, with a total number of parameters of 593.6M. Besides, we used a pre-trained voice activity detector \cite{silero} to remove left-right silence regions and environmental noises from the input waveforms. Drawing inspiration from \cite{wang2021fine}, we experimented with two finetuning strategies as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:conformer}. In (a) partial fine-tuning, only the parameters of the Conformer Encoder are updated while freezing the state of CNN feature extractors; the complete fine-tuning strategy in (b) updates all the parameters of the Wav2Vec2-Conformer model.


