\section{Conclusions}
\label{sec:conclusion}
% In this work, we introduce the use of acoustic scattering for non-invasive object classification, with a specific case study on hair assessment. We explore different sound AI classification techniques, achieving close to 90\% accuracy. The best method identified is semi-supervised learning with big model fine-tuning. This work has succeeded in showing that wav2vec can be used for a wide range of applications in audio as it was possible to classify the different types of damaged hair as well as hair conditions with a small amount of data. Our results demonstrated that fine-tuning wav2vec2 models are superior alternatives to models pre-trained on audio classification tasks. In addition, it was found that fine-tuning the weights of the feature encoder for the target downstream task is crucial. The effect of finetuning the CNN weights improves the domain gap between the pre-train domain and the target domain.

In this study, we explored acoustic scattering as a novel technique for non-invasive object classification, with a specific case study on hair moisture and type classification. From the scattered acoustic signals, we evaluated multiple sound classification methods, including embedding-based models, fully supervised deep learning, foundation model fine-tuning, and SSL model fine-tuning.
% Our experiments demonstrated that various approaches, particularly SSL model fine-tuning, can achieve classification accuracies close to 90\%. 
Among these methods, fine-tuning SSL models such as Wav2Vec2-Conformer demonstrated the strongest performance, achieving up to 90\% accuracy. This highlights the effectiveness of convolutional layers and attention mechanisms in capturing structural properties from scattered acoustic signals.
% Furthermore, fine-tuning feature encoders, particularly CNN layers, proved to be a critical step in improving performance by addressing domain differences between pre-trained models and the target task. 
These results illustrate the potential of acoustic scattering for the non-invasive classification of complex object structures, offering a privacy-preserving alternative to traditional vision-based methods. Beyond hair analysis, this approach could be extended to material science, quality control, and other industrial applications, paving the way for further research and real-world implementation. Our code and analysis are open-sourced for further exploration.\footnote{https://github.com/tuanio/Acoustic\_Scattering\_AI-Noninvasive\_Object\_Classifications}
